{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat review-inputs/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-16 07:40:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-16 14:40 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude (claude-fable-5-1)\nSession-ID: not exposed to this session (stdout log: collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.fable.stdout.log)\n\n# Slice 6d plan v2: answer to the second architect's review, and a complete replacement plan\n\nRole: Architect. Worktree `datomworld-6d-review` = `origin/master` at `7c4dff32` (the reviewer's HEAD). Inputs: the second review (`collab/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md`, \"the review\"), my v1 plan (`collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md`, \"v1\"), the 2026-10-13 assessment (`collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md`). Every `file:line` below was re-read in this tree today. **What I ran:** one throwaway JVM test namespace (`test/yin/vm/semantic/probe_6d_test.cljc`, `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic.probe-6d-test`, 3 tests, 9 assertions, 0 failures) that reproduces the review's F1, F2 and F5 counterexamples on the real kernel and layouts; it is deleted and `git status` shows only `collab/`. Nothing else was executed; no code or design doc was edited. The owner's invariant (preemptive migration at any pc is primary) is settled and is the premise throughout. Vocabulary per UCF §7.5.3 (cursor / cursor cell / cursor-ref / cell).\n\n**Verdict on the review: all nine findings ACCEPTED.** I dispute nothing of substance. Three were reproduced by execution (F1, F2, F5), six by reading the cited transitions. v1's `PLAN-READY` was unsupported. Section 1 gives the evidence per finding; section 2 lists what v1 got wrong; section 3 is plan v2, a complete replacement.\n\n**Summary for the owner.**\n\n1. **The task is a product, not a sum.** The scheduler constructs a machine with active control **and** remaining ordered waits (`resume-from-run-queue` leaves `:wait-set` untouched, `engine.cljc:2305-2312`; `register-restore` sets control, `semantic.cljc:116-118`), and with active control **and** a queued completed wake (`run-loop` steps the active activation before draining the queue, `engine.cljc:2211`, `:2216`). Both reproduced (§1 F1, F2). The root is therefore `(kind, activation?, ordered waits, ordered ready outcomes, parked table, installs, store …)`. v1's R0 (\"a wait set beside control is malformed\") and the ready-queue refusal are withdrawn. A `:ready` row carries a completed wake (its value, status and cursor update); `:yin.k/not-quiescent` dies.\n2. **Fuel must reach children.** `scheduler-round` runs every install child with `vm/run` to a park or halt (`engine.cljc:1475`). `run-fuel` gives each task of the tree at most `n` instructions per program tick; a child stopped mid-body stays at phase `:running` (the arm already exists, `:1490-1491`) and is carried as a `:running` child task. The guarantee is cooperative: interruption between completed instructions, no wall-clock bound for one instruction or one host seam (`(apply f args)` at `semantic.cljc:193`, `vm/create-stream!` at `engine.cljc:480`, a bridge step, one sweep).\n3. **Tenure loss keeps the boundary, but adopts nothing.** `run-cycle` computes `m1` and then rechecks tenure before `m1` reaches the state (`driver.cljc:2120-2123`, `:2139`); the catch ends the run on the *pre-slice* state (`:2252-2253`). 6d-b assoc's `m1` before every later check so the ended holder retains the newest pure state as `:yin.k/retained-boundary` (inspection only). Expiry recovery stays the last durable checkpoint; **progress since it is lost**, the documents say so, and the mitigation is the composition's `hand-off` before the bound. Adoption of a retained boundary by a regrant is a ledger rule the owner may add later (decision 4); not in 6d.\n4. **The stack-layout equation was false.** `stack-layouts` assigns only parse-node pcs (`layout.cljc:124`); the conditional's `:jump` is `:jump` metadata, not a child (`code.cljc:389-396`), and terminators are outside the expression (`:413-414`). Reproduced: at the jump pc `L = #{0}`, layout `nil`, uses `#{}`. v2 defines `stack-layout(jump) = stack-layout(if) ++ [rd(if)]`, `[]` at terminators, an **ordered** check (`layout(p) ++ uses(p)` in emitter order against the kernel's region), and the dead-prefix rule for tail residuals (the kernel keeps `stack'` on a tail call, `stack.cljc:731`, `:747`). The register pairing argument stands (the review agrees).\n5. **Six corrections to v1's briefs**: keep the halted grant exemption (`rows.cljc:2039` is right as written); the register-target refusal oracle is over the whole payload, not `L(pc)`; harness order is lift, lower, immediate relift (law 2), then run (law 1) in an isolated resource world; a `run` row with a pending is S1 `:frame-shape`, not P1 `:pending-presence`; `:push` is canonicalised to its successor (no guest semantics run), not refused; the amended text separates representability, portability, satisfaction and export readiness.\n6. **Module-pins commit 2 is not conflict-free with 6d** (v1 said it was): it edits `discharge!`/`install-node`/`lift-task*`'s `requires` (`rows.cljc` hunks at 1603-1700), replaces `check-install-admission!` with `check-modules!` (1883-1918) and `lower-task` (2273-2304), and re-mints `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` (1,182 lines) and `ledger-v2.txt`. Since it is merging now, **6d-a branches from master after it lands** and re-mints once (the new task slots, decision 1). Line numbers in this report are master `7c4dff32`; they shift by about +48 lines after 1883 once commit 2 is in.\n7. **Order**: 6d-a (grammar, validator, lift/lower, `run-fuel`, the jump-layout fix and the static checks, the reference-lane stepping harness) → then 6d-b (holder, custody, expiry, recovery, Stage E) **and** 6e (layouts' running mode, ordered stack check, `:push` canonicalisation, tail residuals, laws 4/5 on aligned boundaries) in parallel on two branches; docs with each slice. Nothing of 6d runs in parallel with module-pins' merge except writing the 6d-a brief.\n8. **Effort**: 6d-a 3 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 4.5 days; 6d-b 2.5 + 1.5 = 4; 6e 3.5 + 1.5 = 5; docs 1.5 → **15 days**, up from v1's 11; no runtime measurement supports any wall-time forecast. Routing: codex (gpt-6.1-sol) implements, Fable reviews, glm/cmd as non-author fallback (owner 2026-10-15).\n9. **Owner decisions** (§3.10, defaults stated so 6d-a can start): new task slots 17/18 (recommended) vs. a mixed slot 13; fuel as a holder config key, default nil; explicit `hand-off` only, no auto-arm in 6d-b; no adoption of a retained boundary; keep edge-reachability liveness (Q6) and pin the pre-branch `ghost` refusal; completed `:put` wakes are completed operations, not retained writes; canonicalise `:push`; holds stay refusals as documented driver transients.\n10. **Readiness**: with the defaults, **6d-a is implementation-ready the moment module-pins commit 2 is on master**, and its brief can be written now. 6d-b and 6e are implementation-ready as specified here but depend on 6d-a's grammar. One acceptance item is a check, not an open design: whether the lane fragment contains a tail call with a non-empty ambient layout (6e item 6 settles it either way).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The nine findings, verified\n\nLegend: **ACCEPTED** = the counterexample or citation holds as stated; evidence is what I read or ran. Probe lines are quoted from the JVM run (`summary` prints `:control :blocked? :halted? :waits :wait-reasons :ready`).\n\n### F1 — ready-queue exception contradicts any-pc migration: ACCEPTED (reproduced)\n\nReading: `run-loop` (`engine.cljc:2205-2219`) tests `(active? v)` first (`:2211`, `active-continuation?` = not blocked and not halted, `:258-261`) and reaches `(seq q)` only after the activation parks or halts (`:2216`). `vm-hot`'s active path never touches `:ready-queue` (`semantic.cljc:139-268`). `check-wait-set` queues **every** woken entry in one call (`:2197-2199`). The transport preserves ordered multiple waits (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:458-460`) and the harness transports `two-callers` (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1093-1107`).\n\nProbe (two-callers, both responses appended, `sweep`, `resume-from-run-queue`, `vm-hot 1`, `lift-task`, `vm/run`):\n\n```\nF1 after sweep:                 {:control nil, :blocked? true, :halted? false, :waits 0, :ready 2}\nF1 after resume-from-run-queue: {:control {:segment -1, :pc 3}, :blocked? false, :halted? false, :waits 0, :ready 1}\nF1 after vm-hot 1:              {:control {:segment -1, :pc 5}, :blocked? false, :halted? false, :waits 0, :ready 1}\nF1 lift refusal:                #:yin.k{:status :yin.k/not-quiescent, :ready 1}\nF1 after vm/run:                {:control nil, :blocked? true, :halted? false, :waits 2, :wait-reasons [:next :next], :ready 0}\nF1 queued entry keys:           (:call-id :cursor :cursor-ref :deliver :env :k :pc :reason :resource-updates :segment :status :stream-id :value :window)\n```\n\nSo an active activation beside a queued completed wake is a real kernel state; one instruction leaves the queue untouched; today's lift refuses it; only running the activation to its next park drains it, which for a pure recursion is unbounded. v1 §2.5 (\"retry after the next cycle\") was not a bounded normalisation. The queued entry is the wait entry plus `:value :status :resource-updates :cursor` (`make-woken-run-queue-entries`, `:442-463`); the `:put` has appended and the `:next` has consumed its element, so it can be neither re-pended nor dropped. **Right carrier**: the task-state product of §3.1 with an ordered `:ready` row list; no normalisation proof exists without executing guest code, so none is claimed.\n\n### F2 — R0 rejects a state the scheduler constructs: ACCEPTED (reproduced)\n\nReading: `check-wait-set` keeps unresolved waits (`:2192-2196`); `resume-from-run-queue` pops one entry, clears `:blocked?`/`:halted?` and **does not touch `:wait-set`** (`:2305-2309`); `register-restore` sets control through `put-registers` (`semantic.cljc:116-118`, `:50-55`). Explicit `:resume` also restores into the existing machine (`engine.cljc:2360-2363`).\n\nProbe (two-callers, only the first response appended):\n\n```\nF2 admitted two-waiter machine: {:control nil, :blocked? true, :waits 2, :wait-reasons [:next :next], :ready 0}\nF2 after check-wait-set:        {:control nil, :blocked? true, :waits 1, :wait-reasons [:next], :ready 1}\nF2 after resume-from-run-queue: {:control {:segment -1, :pc 3}, :blocked? false, :waits 1, :wait-reasons [:next], :ready 0}\nF2 lift of control+wait today:  #:yin.k{:status :yin.k/not-at-safepoint, :pc 3}\n```\n\nActive control plus a remaining wait, no queue needed. The two-waiter carrier is admitted today (lift, lower and transport of it are tested), so v1's R0 would have refused an admitted state as \"inconsistent\". Withdrawn. Kind classification (root) is separated from activation shape (the `:run` row) in §3.1.\n\n### F3 — fuel does not bound an install child or the program tick: ACCEPTED\n\n`scheduler-round` (`engine.cljc:2324-2326`) → `check-wait-set` → `poll-links` (`:2168`) → `advance-installs` → `advance-install` → `(vm/run (stamp-gate (:vm inst) mode))` (`:1475`): the child runs to its own park or halt inside the round, whatever the root's fuel. v1's `run-fuel` fell into an unbounded round whenever the root was inactive. The lower admits only a `:blocked` child (`rows.cljc:1862-1863`) although the lift admits wire phase `:running` (`:1640`, today a synthetic phase, continuation design `:1202`). Also confirmed: `:call` of a host function is one synchronous `(apply f args)` (`semantic.cljc:193`), stream construction calls the composition's constructor (`engine.cljc:480`), `ffi/maybe-run` loops bridge steps on a blocked machine (`ffi.cljc:334-348`). Fuel counts instructions, never time. §3.4 gives the propagation, the child carrier, and the honest guarantee.\n\n### F4 — a tenure failure loses the just-computed boundary: ACCEPTED\n\n`run-cycle` (`driver.cljc:2111-2144`): `require-tenure!` (`:2118`), `m1` (`:2120-2122`), `require-tenure!` (`:2123`) **before** `(assoc state :machine m4)` (`:2139`); `require-tenure!` throws `::cause :lease-bound` (`:2060-2063`); `step-active` catches and calls `(end-run state …)` on the argument, which still holds `m0` (`:2252-2253`). Later cuts: replay/emit/read call `require-tenure!` through `request!` and the observe seam (`:2127-2137`); `binding-check` ends the run before the cycle (`:1991`, `:2013`); a renewal that lands past the bound ends it (`:1810-1811`); `exit-mint` requires tenure (`:1144`); `abort` requires live tenure and proof no offer was accepted (`export.cljc:609-622`), and the driver's abort path ends the run when tenure is gone (`driver.cljc:2868-2887`). Recovery rebuilds only a complete fenced snapshot (`rebuilt-cell`, `:2442-2484`; `reopen-source`, `:2487-2513`). v1 §2.2's sentence (\"tenure dying between slices ends the run with the machine at a boundary, and the holder … can arm the exit before the bound\") conflated two cases and specified neither. §3.3 gives the lifecycle.\n\n### F5 — the all-pc stack equation is false: ACCEPTED (reproduced)\n\n`stack-layouts` walks `(:expr body)` and `:children`, writing `(assoc out (:pc node) pending)` only (`layout.cljc:118-131`); the parser's conditional node is `{:op :if :pc branch-pc :jump jpc :end …}` with the jump as metadata (`code.cljc:389-396`); `parse-body` parses `[start, term)` so the terminator is no node (`:413-414`). The stack kernel: `:const` pushes (`stack.cljc:689-690`), `:jump` only sets pc (`:777-778`), `:branch-false` pops the test (`:780-784`), `:halt`/`:return` peek the top (`:775`, `:787`). Probe over `layout/segment` of two projected programs (the equation column is `(set layout) ∪ uses = L`):\n\n```\nF5 bare (if true 10 20):\n    3 [:jump 5]            L=#{0}    layout=nil  uses=#{}   eq=false\n    5 [:halt 0]            L=#{0}    layout=nil  uses=#{0}  eq=true\nF5 operand position ((fn [x] x) (if true 10 20)):\n    1 [:const 3 true]      L=#{1}    layout=[1]  uses=#{}   eq=true\n    4 [:jump 6]            L=#{1 2}  layout=nil  uses=#{}   eq=false\n    6 [:call 0 1 [2] false] L=#{1 2} layout=[]   uses=#{1 2} eq=true\n```\n\nAt the jump the kernel holds the consequent's value (register 2 in the second program, 0 in the first), which both arms write (`derive.cljc:81-85` emits `then`, `[:jump end]`, `else`). Correct layouts: `stack-layout(jump) = stack-layout(if-pc) ++ [rd(if)]`; terminators `[]` (the equation then holds through `uses`). Set equality also loses duplicate operands; the check must be ordered. **Tail residuals**: a tail `:call` keeps `stack'` (`stack.cljc:731`, `:747`) and pushes no frame (`:737-738`), so the ambient layout of a tail call in operand position (admitted as layout syntax, continuation design `:805-810`; the tail flag is the AST's, `linearize.cljc:116`, judged only as a boolean by item 10, `code.cljc:644-659`) survives **below the callee**, in the current activation's region and in any later frame's slice; `slice-window` today refuses a count mismatch (`layout.cljc:505-506`). Lowering at the tail pc alone does not establish the next body-start boundary. §3.5 gives the dead-prefix rule. The register argument (`derive.cljc:127-130`, `:140-143`) stands.\n\n### F6 — grant-check instruction wrong: ACCEPTED\n\n`check-custody!` (`rows.cljc:2023-2039`): `(when-not (or fenced? (= :halted kind)) (accept-grant! …))`. A `:running` root is not halted, so it already takes `accept-grant!` once `task-ops` (`:1261`) and `custody-baseline` (`:1297`) admit the kind. v1 §4.2 item 4 (\"drop `(= :halted kind)`\") would have demanded a grant for an origin-only result, contradicting v1 §2.1 and the tuples' custody table (`:470`). Corrected brief: preserve the dispatch; extend the kind sets; pin \"a halted exclusive root restores without a grant, gated `:ended`\" as a regression.\n\n### F7 — the primitive refusal predicate is broader than L(pc): ACCEPTED\n\n`lower-defect` (`layout.cljc:916-953`) checks every wait's state through `state-prim` (`:947-948`) and every parked state (`:949-950`); `state-prim` checks the window **and** lexical bindings of the frame and of every `K` frame (`frame-prim`, `:853-860`; `:863-874`). The harness already refuses primitives in a lexical binding, a capture and a nested collection (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1931-1960`). So \"refusal count = pcs with a primitive in `L(pc)`\" undercounts whenever a primitive sits in `E` or in a saved `K` window. Also confirmed: `(state-prim code (first (:slots w)) …)` reads a **wait row's** first slot; fed a `:run` row directly it would read the segment address. §3.6 gives the oracle; the obstruction list (image collision, installs, free-env, retained FFI, registers) is kept whole.\n\n### F8 — harness order compares the wrong state and mutates its fixture: ACCEPTED\n\nv1 §4.1 item 4 said lift → lower → `vm/run` to the end → law 1 → relift → law 2: the relift is of a halted root, not the running one. The transport law says \"no execution between lower and lift\" (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:913-917`). The existing fixtures operate real toy streams, so running a receiver to completion on the stepping source's world appends effects and consumes scripted outcomes. §3.6 corrects the order and isolates worlds, and defines \"every pc\" as every boundary of the chosen executions, pinned by `(segment, pc, structural state)`.\n\n### F9 — the document text overstates and the inventory is incomplete: ACCEPTED\n\nVerified each cited site: `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:368-371` and `:403-408` (custody prose lists three kinds), `:433-435` (`frame/defect` contexts), `:1249-1252` (refusal rows naming the stepped-kernel refusal), `:2419-2425` (a second normative conformance text), `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:455-460` (kind rules), `:466-473` (custody table), `:797-801` (step 7 contexts), `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:800-805` (conformance runs \"to each safepoint\"), `:1912-1913` (\"no engine change\"), `:2842` (no-layer-collapsing row: hot loop untouched, lift over a blocked machine), `yin.vm.semantic.md:680-689` (explicit park is a no-wait safepoint; halt carries a result). v1 §3.3's safepoint definition (\"carries a delivery record and a pending wait\") was wrong for explicit park and halt; \"Every instruction boundary lifts\" overstated; the `:push` wording (\"no canonical state exists here\") was wrong (`stack.cljc:722-723` only advances pc); S1 vs P1 diagnostics conflicted. §3.7 is the corrected text; §3.8 the inventory, now at line granularity for every file `grep safepoint` reaches.\n\n**On the review's eight answers.** Adopted: `run-fuel` is telemetry-free in the sense \"no per-fuel snapshot\", not \"no telemetry\" (parks and resumes emit, `engine.cljc:2352`, `:2363`); step atomicity does not imply transactional external effects (an append that happened before a host exception is not rolled back); `abort` is conditional (`export.cljc:603-622`); exclusive grants authorise one holder's effects and do not physically prevent a trusted caller from lowering twice; \"one behavioural change\" in v1 hid the scheduler and expiry dependencies; the 11-day estimate was for a narrower design.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What v1 got wrong, plainly\n\n1. **R0.** Asserted a kernel invariant (\"every arm that creates a wait clears control, so control beside a wait is malformed\") that holds for a single-waiter organic machine and fails for admitted multi-waiter carriers resumed by the scheduler. Reproduced.\n2. **Ready queue as a refusal.** Called \"run to the next park\" a bounded crossing. It is unbounded guest execution; the queued wake is a performed effect's result that must be carried.\n3. **Fuel bounds the tick.** False: install children run `vm/run` inside the sweep; host seams are atomic and unbounded by fuel.\n4. **Tenure between slices.** Did not read `step-active`'s catch: the computed boundary is discarded, and expiry/recovery semantics were unspecified.\n5. **Stack equation at every pc.** False at jump pcs; `stack-layouts` has no entry at jump or terminator pcs; set equality loses duplicates; tail residuals ignored below a running callee.\n6. **Grant-check brief.** Would have broken halted restoration.\n7. **Refusal allowlist.** Too narrow (lexical and saved-frame primitives) and the `state-prim` call site would have mis-read a `:run` row.\n8. **Harness order and worlds.** Compared a halted relift to a running lift; shared mutable toy streams across points.\n9. **Document text.** Overstated liftability; wrong safepoint definition; wrong `:push` claim; S1/P1 conflict; six sites missing from the inventory; `yang.antlr.md` footnote aside, nothing else of the inventory was wrong, only incomplete.\n10. **Module-pins independence.** Claimed no textual conflict; commit 2 touches `lift-task*`, the install node, `check-install-admission!` → `check-modules!` and `lower-task`, and re-mints every fixture.\n11. **Effort.** Omitted the product carrier, running children, expiry, control-flow layouts and the oracle.\n\nWhat v1 got right and keeps (the review concurs): `:run` ≠ `:act` at one pc; body-start exception to C2/C3; nil-valued keys retained; whole root store, every parked record, source AST associations, fresh receiver seals; existing admission stages; four-key custody arm for a running root; gate `:running` on restore; the kernel-entry gate guard for `:exporting`/`:ended`; fencing before publication; fuel as a composition knob; reference-lane / cross-engine separation; no new global, callback, clock or shared mutable state.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plan v2\n\n### 3.1 State model: the task-state product\n\n**Task** (one machine of the tree, root or install child):\n\n```\ntask = ( kind,                      ; :running | :blocked | :parked | :halted\n         run?,                      ; the active activation ⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩, iff kind = :running\n         waits  = [wait …],         ; ordered pending waits (any kind but :halted)\n         ready  = [ready …],        ; ordered completed wakes, scheduler outcomes (any kind but :halted)\n         parked-table, result?, parked-id?,\n         store, module-stores, cursor-cells, free-env, id-counter, requires, code, installs, custody )\n```\n\n**Kind rules** (replace tuples §5.5 / continuation §2.6; the machine-side predicate in the lift, §3.2):\n\n| kind | machine | frames (waits) | run | ready | parked-id | result |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `:running` | `:control` set | 0..n | exactly one `:run` | 0..n | none | none |\n| `:parked` | control nil, `:value` a parked record the table holds | 0..n | none | 0..n | names a table key | none |\n| `:blocked` | control nil, not parked, `(or (seq waits) (seq ready))` | 0..n | none | 0..n (waits+ready ≥ 1) | none | none |\n| `:halted` | control nil, `halted-with-empty-queue?`, no waits | 0 | none | 0 | none | one |\n\nA machine with `:halted? true`, a non-parked value and a non-empty queue (one activation of a multi-waiter machine halted while a wake is queued) is kind `:blocked`: `run-loop` resumes the queue before it reads the halt (`:2216` before `:2219`) and the next halt overwrites `:value`, so the halt value is dead by the kernel's own order; restore gives it `:blocked? true :halted? false`, which `run-loop` treats identically (no waits wake; the queue pops). Law 1 and law 2 hold on it and the harness pins the case (§3.9 6d-a item 6).\n\n**Row grammar** (`rows.cljc` `row-table`; two new row kinds, two new task slots):\n\n```\n:run    [:A :N [:ref :env] [:ref :window] [:ref :k]]          ; five slots; no deliver, no pending\n:ready  [[:ref :wait] :V :D [:ref :cursor-cells]]              ; the wait it completes, value, status, cursor updates\n:task   [… slot 13 [:refs :wait]  14 :optional-D  15 :optional-V  16 :optional-D   ; unchanged\n           17 [:refs :run]        ; 0 or 1, by kind\n           18 [:refs :ready]]     ; 0..n, in queue order\n```\n\n- `:run`: `pc` is the instruction about to execute (`0 ≤ pc < count v`); the window is exactly `L(pc)` (`analysis/live`), nil present as `[:lit nil]`, sorted on the wire; `K` as for an `:act`.\n- `:ready`: its `[:ref :wait]` is a `:wait` row (state `:act`/`:tail` + pending), judged in context `:wait` exactly as a pending wait; `:V` is the woken `:value` (for a `:put` the appended datom; for a `:next` the element read, or the shaped refusal map; for an FFI response the response map, the entry's `:call-id` riding the pending as today); `:D` is the `:status` keyword, carried verbatim so the receiver's `terminal-resume-outcome` raises exactly as the source would have; `[:ref :cursor-cells]` is the entry's `:resource-updates`, a pairs row of `C → cursor-cell-body` over cells of the task's census (a `:next` wake advances one cell; a `:put` wake has none: empty row). The entry's redundant `:cursor` field is not carried (resume reads `:resource-updates`, `engine.cljc:2299`, `:2307`). Reasons admitted in a ready row: `:next`, `:put` (an FFI response is a `:next` on the response cursor); any other reason in the queue refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :ready-reason` and is pinned (6d-a verifies by reading `poll-links` whether link wakes ever enter the queue; today they restore directly).\n- Why two new slots rather than a mixed slot 13: every slot-13 traversal (`task-ops` `:1263-1277`, `protect!` `:1725-1727`, `check-pendings!` `:1873-1876`, `lower-defect` `:947-948`, restore's entry mapping) reads wait rows positionally; a `:run` or `:ready` row in the same list is exactly F7's silent-misread class. Separate slots leave those traversals untouched and give the new rows their own. Cost: every task row's bytes change, so every pinned root id re-mints once. Module-pins commit 2 re-mints them all anyway and is merging now; 6d-a lands right after and re-mints once more. Decision 1 (§3.10).\n\n**Validator** (`frame.cljc`), in the existing order:\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | `:yin.k/state ∈ #{:act :tail :run}`; a `run` has exactly `:yin.k/state :segment :pc :env :window :k` and no other key (so a `deliver` or `pending` key on a `run` is `:frame-shape`; R3 of v1 folds into S1) | `:frame-shape` |\n| C2 | `pc` an exact integer; `0 < pc < count v` for an `act` and every return frame; `0 ≤ pc < count v` for a `run` (a body-start pc is a legal running state) | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n| C3 | body continuity `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame; **not** for a `run` | `:body-continuity` |\n| R1 | every `run` window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n| R2 | `(set (keys window)) = (analysis/live a pc)` exactly (`window-defect` parameterised by the wanted set: `saved(pc, rd)` for act and return frames, `live(pc)` for a run; no `:window-self` analogue) | `:window-extra` / `:window-missing` |\n| A1-A6, T1 | act/tail only; skipped for a `run` | unchanged |\n| P1 | context `:wait` requires `pending`; `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running` require it absent; a `run` is admitted in context `:running` only and `:running` admits only a `run`; an `act` offered in context `:running` is `:pending-presence` (a shape question is S1's, a context question P1's: one ordered diagnostic, tested as such) | `:pending-presence` |\n| E1, E2, K1-K3, X1 | unchanged; a `run`'s `E` is judged at its own pc's chain | |\n\n**Custody by kind** (phase 7's tables, `rows.cljc`):\n\n| Site | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `task-ops` `:1261`, `custody-baseline` `:1297` | kind set `#{:running :blocked :parked :halted}` |\n| `task-ops` | a `:ready` row's `:put` is a **completed** operation: its op-id is checked well-formed (`check-op-id!`) but excluded from `:yin.k/ops` (retained writes) and from `protect!`'s enrolled-target check; a fork may carry it (the write happened under the source's own authority, as a fork's history does). The receiver never re-appends it: resume delivers `:V`. |\n| `header-custody` `:1704-1716` | `:running` takes the four-key arm (a running root has a next observable step; it is a lease subject) |\n| `check-pendings!` `:1877-1882` | the kind table above; `:running` requires `(count run) = 1`, every other kind `0`; `:halted` requires empty waits **and** empty ready |\n| `check-custody!` `:2039` | **unchanged** (F6); `:running` reaches `accept-grant!` through the kind sets |\n| `with-custody` `:2042-2064` | unchanged: numbers retained `:put` **waits** only; gate `:running` for every non-halted root |\n| `check-root-custody!` `:1200-1245` | `:running` validated as blocked/parked |\n| `check-install!` `:1862` | child kind `:blocked` with wire phase `:parked`; child kind `:running` with wire phase `:running`; else `:install-child` |\n| `lower-task` opts | `:grant`/`:protection` required by a blocked, parked **or running** exclusive root |\n\n### 3.2 Lift, lower, restore (`rows.cljc`, `layout.cljc` `state-prim`)\n\n**Lift** (`lift-task*` `:1650-1698`), new step 1-2:\n\n1. **Holds.** Refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch` on `unliftable-hold` as today. (The queue refusal at `:1652-1653` is deleted; `:yin.k/not-quiescent` leaves the outcome vocabulary; §3.7 says why holds stay.)\n2. **Kind.** `:running` when `:control` is set; else `:parked` when the value is a parked record the table holds; else `:blocked` when waits or ready are non-empty; else `:halted` when `halted-with-empty-queue?`; anything else (no control, no waits, no queue, not halted) is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` (unreachable by construction; keep the arm).\n3. **Nodes.** `run` = `{:segment (address! seg) :pc pc :env (:env m) :window (select-keys (:window m) (analysis/live a pc)) :k (or (:k m) [])}` through `state-node` (no `:site`, no `:deliver`) and `context!` for discharge (`live-pcs` from `pc`); waits as today (`:1671`); `ready` = for each queue entry, `(node :ready [(node :wait [(state-node ctx e) (pending-node ctx e)]) (lift-value ctx (:value e)) (:status e) (cells-node ctx (:resource-updates e))])`, where `cells-node` runs each update through `cell!` so the census numbers the advanced cell beside its current cell (two positions of one cell id: the task's `:cursor-cells` row holds the current, the ready row the successor; one canonical number per cell id, as the census already aliases shared cells); the task node gets slots 17 and 18.\n4. `validate-frames` judges slot 17 in context `:running` and each slot-18 wait in context `:wait`. The rest of the lift (store, parked, installs, module stores, discharge, requires, `custody-baseline`, emit) is unchanged; the walk is kind-independent, so a `:running` child is lifted by the same function with `role :child`.\n\n**Lower / restore** (`restore-task` `:2253-2268`): a `:running` task restores `:control {:segment local :pc pc} :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K) :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set entries`; `:blocked`/`:parked`/`:halted` as today; every kind restores `:ready-queue` from slot 18 in order, each entry rebuilt as `(assoc wait-entry :value V :status D :resource-updates {local-cell-key cell})` with cell ids mapped through the receiver's fresh keys (the mapping `with-custody`/`restore-task` already keeps for `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`). A fenced restore puts the ready list in `:yin.k/recovery-ready` beside `:yin.k/recovery-waits` and leaves `:ready-queue []`. Nothing runs. `vm/run` (or `run-fuel`) then continues: an active root steps from `pc`; an inactive one sweeps, and the first ready entry resumes before any newly woken wait, in carried order.\n\n**`lower-defect`** (`layout.cljc:916-953`): add `(state-prim code run-row [:run])` over slot 17 (window plus lexical bindings plus `K`, as `:act`) and `(state-prim code (first (:slots (first (:slots r)))) [:ready i])` over slot 18; the de Bruijn backends do not carry install children (unchanged refusal `:installs`), so a running **child** is never lowered into a backend.\n\n**`export/kind-of`** (`export.cljc:274-282`): `(some? (:control m)) → :running` first; `header-agrees?` (`:387-399`) already takes the four-key branch for any non-halted kind; `valid-state?` and `recovery-metadata` gain the ready list (zero issue stamps; the ready entries counted and compared on rehydration).\n\n### 3.3 Custody and lifecycle of a running root\n\n**A running root under a lease.** Full custody slot (policy, occurrence, arbitration, next-op-seq, origin on a successor); `accept-grant!` on lower; gate `:running` on restore; offerable by `ledger/offer-defect` (`ledger.cljc:277`) and `grant/offer-decision` (`authority/grant.cljc:74-75`) once both sets list `:running`; `protect!` checks its retained `:put` waits as for a blocked root (there may be some: F2). The restored machine's first effect parks under the gate (`handle-put` `engine.cljc:532-533`, `handle-next`, `ffi/put-request` answering `full`), so the driver performs it; unchanged from a parked root.\n\n**Four outcomes, specified separately.**\n\n1. **Preemption under live tenure** (the normal path). `run-cycle` runs `(run-fuel m0 n)` when `:fuel` is set (§3.4); the slice returns a machine parked, halted, or at a boundary. The cycle continues (replay, emit, reader step) exactly as today; `cycle-result` leaves the phase `:running` and adds `:yin.k/at-boundary true` to the status detail when `(:control m4)` is set. The composition calls `hand-off` (`driver.cljc:1118-1134`) when it wants to export: `arm-exit` → `export/enter` (its `first-refusal` queue check is deleted; holds remain) → the exit steps mint, prepare, encode (`kind :running`), journal the fence, freeze the recovery object, offer. Only a live-tenure holder reaches `exit-mint` (`:1144`). This is the path that closes the owner's invariant for the holder: a long pure computation is interruptible at every `n` instructions and exportable there.\n2. **Tenure dies after a completed slice** (at `:2123`, inside replay/emit/read, or at the next entry's `binding-check`). 6d-b restructures `run-cycle` so each stage's product is assoc'd into the state **before** the next tenure check (`(-> state (assoc :machine m1) require-tenure! …)`), and `step-active`'s catch ends the run on the state it was handed, which now carries the newest completed pure stage. `end-run` gates the machine `:ended` as today; the ended state additionally carries `:yin.k/retained-boundary {:yin.k/pc … :yin.k/segment … :yin.k/steps-since-checkpoint k}` for inspection and diagnostics. **No adoption**: an ended holder holds no tenure, so `exit-mint` refuses, `abort` refuses `:tenure-ended`/`:tenure-unconfirmed`, and the retained bytes are never offered or resumed. Why retention is safe: under the `:running` gate the slice performs no external observation (effects park; FFI requests are retained for the driver), so `m1` is pure local progress; retaining it loses nothing and discloses nothing.\n3. **Expiry and recovery.** The occurrence is regrantable from its **last durable checkpoint**: the pack this holder lowered, or the last successor it published. The successor relowers it (`step-validating` `:2291-2316`, awaiting-grant for a running root as for a blocked one), `accept-grant!` checks the input prefix and `next-op-seq`, and the writer replays retained writes idempotently by op-id (UCF §7.4.3). **Progress since that checkpoint is lost**: pure computation, and effects performed since (they are durable on the streams and will be re-performed only if their op-ids say they were not). Fuel does not repair this; only exporting before the bound does (outcome 1). The documents say so in those words (§3.7).\n4. **Reopen** (`rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-source`): a fenced snapshot of a running root rehydrates with `:control` set and gate `:exporting` (`rows/lower-task {:fenced true}`); `rehydrate-fenced` re-prepares and must reproduce the pack byte for byte (a `:run` frame is a pure function of the restored fields; the ready list likewise). The kernel refuses to step it: `vm-hot` gains the entry guard `(when (contains? #{:exporting :ended} (vm/gate-mode machine)) (fail \"Gate is closed\" …))` before the loop (`semantic.cljc:137`), one map read per call, not per instruction; `run-fuel` inherits it. Today the guard is moot for parked roots (control nil → `scheduler-round`, which observes nothing under a gate); for a running root it is what makes \"the exporting machine is pure data\" (UCF §7.7.4) true.\n\n**Auto-arm before the bound** (decision 3): recommendation is **not in 6d-b**. `hand-off` stays the composition's explicit act (UCF §7.8); `:yin.k/at-boundary` is the signal. A later policy \"arm the exit when remaining tenure is below one renewal interval\" needs only `lease/holding?` and the clock the driver already reads (`:2060-2063`), no clock-to-instruction conversion, and can live in `compose` without touching the carrier.\n\n**Tests (6d-b)**: clock-controlled cuts (a) immediately before the slice, (b) immediately after it (the `:2123` check), (c) in replay, in emit and in the reader step, (d) between two slices at the next entry's `binding-check`, (e) during `exit-mint`/`prepare`/`offer` of a running root; in each: the source is fenced or ended, no effect was performed past the bound, and **either** a durably authorized successor exists (the offer was accepted under live tenure) **or** the recovery checkpoint is the last pack and `:yin.k/retained-boundary` reports the lost steps; byte-equality of `rehydrate-fenced` for a running root with waits and with a ready entry; two competing candidates for a `:running` offer; stale-source stepping refused by the gate guard; `abort` with uncertain offer refused; expired-grant admission refused.\n\n### 3.4 Fuel and budget propagation\n\n**`yin.vm.semantic/run-fuel [machine n]`**, public, `n` a positive integer:\n\n```\n(ffi/maybe-run machine\n  (fn [m]\n    (if (engine/active-continuation? m)\n      (vm-hot m n)                       ; ≤ n instructions of the active activation\n      (scheduler-round-fuel m n))))      ; one sweep, children budgeted, one resume\n```\n\n- `vm-hot m n` already materialises the boundary at exhaustion (`semantic.cljc:142-143`).\n- `scheduler-round-fuel m n` = `check-wait-set` with `advance-installs` taking the budget: each install child is stepped with `(run-fuel child n)` instead of `(vm/run …)` (`engine.cljc:1475`); a child that returns with `:control` set is stored at phase `:running` (the existing `:else` arm, `:1490-1491`); a blocked child `:parked`; a halted child goes to `link-install` as today. Then `resume-from-run-queue` once. **Per-tree bound**: each task of the tree executes at most `n` instructions per program tick, so a tick is at most `n × (tasks in the tree)` instructions plus the sweep's scan plus the host seams listed below. Nested install children are tasks of the tree and get the same `n`.\n- `ffi/maybe-run` (`ffi.cljc:334-348`) loops bridge steps while the machine is blocked and a handler answered; each loop costs another slice; the bridge step itself is a host seam.\n- `vm/run` and `vm/step` are unchanged (`vm/step` keeps its telemetry snapshot; `run-fuel` emits none per slice, but parks and resumes inside it emit as today).\n\n**The honest guarantee** (goes into the documents verbatim): *Fuel counts completed instructions. A holder with fuel `n` is interruptible at the boundary after at most `n` instructions of each task of its tree per program tick. No wall-clock bound is claimed for one instruction: a `:call` of a host function (`semantic.cljc:193`), a stream constructor (`engine.cljc:480`), a bridge step, one scheduler sweep, and a `:var` resolution are atomic host seams whose duration the VM does not control. The VM has no clock and converts no time to instructions; `dao.lease` owns liveness; the composition picks `n` against its renewal interval.*\n\n**Running install children in the carrier**: a child with `:control` set lifts as a `:running` child task (same walk); wire phase `:running` ↔ child kind `:running`; `check-install!` admits it (§3.1); receiver admission per child is unchanged (`check-modules!` after module-pins); a running child restores with its control set and continues in the next sweep; the de Bruijn backends refuse installs as today. Tests (6d-a item 8): a long initializer preempted mid-body, lifted, lowered, continued to `linked`; a nested initializer (child of a child); handoff while a child is running (6d-b).\n\n### 3.5 Stack layouts at control-flow pcs; the register side\n\n**Static layouts** (`layout.cljc` `stack-layouts`, 6d-a): for a parse node `:if` at `b` with jump pc `j`, result register `rd(if)` (the `rd` the arms define; the emitter writes both arms to it, `derive.cljc:81-85` for H, the A linearizer for the register), define `layout(j) = layout(b) ++ [rd(if)]`; for a terminator pc (`:return r`, `:halt r`) define `layout = []`; every other pc as today. Then at every pc inside a body:\n\n```\nordered:  region(p) = layout(p) ++ [W[u] for u in uses-in-order(p)]        ; emitter order (derive.cljc:92-97): :call operator then operands, :ffi-call operands, :stream-put target then value\nset:      (set layout(p)) ∪ (set uses(p)) = L(p)   for every reachable non-tail pc\n          (set uses(p)) = L(p)                     for every reachable tail-call pc\n```\n\nThe ordered form is the check (duplicates `(f x x)` are two positions holding one value; the lift asserts they agree). `jump` and `branch-false` pcs: branch pc keeps the ancestors' layout (`uses` is the test, which H pops); jump pc as above. **`:push` pcs of H**: canonicalised, not refused: `stack-layout` at a `:push` is undefined because it is no A pc; the kernel state at a `:push` equals the state at its successor except pc (`stack.cljc:722-723`); `h->a` maps `hpc` to `h->a(hpc+1)` when `hpc` is a `:push` (never two pushes in a row, `correspondence` `:97-108`); the lift relabels, running nothing; laws 4/5 compare at the aligned A boundary. The design calls this a stutter of the stack backend, not a property of A.\n\n**Dead prefix (tail residuals)**: a tail `:call` consumes `f` and its args and keeps the rest of the region (`stack.cljc:731`, `:747`) without pushing a frame (`:737-738`). Hence inside a body entered by a tail call, and transitively, the current activation's region and any later frame's slice may carry a **dead prefix** below the layout: values the ambient layout syntax pushed, never read again (every H read is top-relative: `:call` by `f-pos` from the top, `:branch-false`/`:return`/`:halt` peek; `return-transition` truncates to `stack-base`, which includes the prefix, and the prefix stays dead). Rule: `lift-stack` takes the **top** `|layout(p)| + |uses(p)|` values of the current region and the top `|layout(pc_i − 1)|` of each frame's slice and drops the rest (`slice-window`'s count equality becomes `≥`, with the excess recorded as `:yin.k/dead-prefix n` in the lift's diagnostics, never in the frame); `lower-stack` produces no prefix and recomputes bases cumulatively (`stack-k` `:635-651` already does). Unobservable: law 1 on continuing to the reference result; law 2/4 since the relift of the lowered kernel has no prefix and the canonical form never had one. **6e item 6** either exhibits a lane-fragment case with a non-empty ambient layout at a tail call (then the rule is exercised) or proves by the static check `layout(p) = [] at every tail-call pc of the fragment` that none exists (then the rule is pinned on a hand-built vector). Both are acceptance, not open design.\n\n**Register side**: `allocation` gives distinct slots to virtuals both in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` (`derive.cljc:127-130`), freeing only when the interval end `< pc` (`:140-143`), so `virt(p, s)` over `L(p)`'s image is single-valued at every pc; `register-window` over `L(p)` for a run; `write-back`-style nil fill outside `L(p)`. Unchanged from v1; the review concurs; it is still a check (§3.9 6d-a item 7).\n\n### 3.6 Harness order, worlds, oracle, coverage\n\n**Order per point** (reference lane, 6d-a; backends, 6e): at boundary `S_i` of the stepping source: (1) **lift**; (2) **lower** into a fresh receiver of an **isolated world** (§ below); (3) **immediate relift** of the receiver and **law 2** (`canonical-under` root equality with the lift; no execution in between); (4) **law 6** on every lifted frame (plus the two window tampers); (5) **run the receiver to the end** in its world, **law 1** (result and effect trace equal the reference's, after `normalize`'s fresh-identity correspondence); (6) negative controls kept: cursor positions and descriptors, windows and lexical values tampered are refused or differ as today.\n\n**Isolated worlds**: each point builds the receiver's composition and streams fresh from the case's **deterministic script** (the same scripted inputs the reference run saw, fed to the receiver's own streams); the stepping source's world is never shared with a receiver; fresh identities normalise through the existing correspondence (`normalize`, `:238`). The reference result and trace are computed once per case on a pristine world.\n\n**\"Every pc\"** means every boundary of the chosen executions (the stepping driver's `S_0 … S_end` for each case and each wrapper), not every CFG-reachable pc nor every possible activation. Coverage is pinned by `(segment-address, pc, structural-class)` where the class is one of: body entry, branch taken, branch untaken, jump, non-tail call, return, tail transition, capture (`:current-continuation`), resume, park, FFI park, scheduler product (active + waits, active + ready, blocked + ready), install child running; the harness asserts each class is reached at least once on the corpus and pins the point count per case per host. The static all-pc checks (§3.5) and the dynamic pass are complementary; neither proves a universal theorem and the docs say so (§3.7 \"claims that are checks\").\n\n**Refusal oracle (6e)**: for each case and point, the expected register-target refusal is computed by an **independent traversal in the test** over the decoded task: any `:prim` row reachable from the `run`'s window or lexical bindings, from any `K` frame's window or bindings, from any wait's or ready's state, or from any parked state. Assert per direction: exactly the expected points refuse `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers` before any attach (attach count zero), and **every other point transfers** and continues to the reference result. The other obstructions (image collision, installs, free-env procedure, retained FFI) are asserted on their existing fixtures and stay in the documented limit list.\n\n### 3.7 Amended document text (exact)\n\nMarker on every clause: \"**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\". The owner's sentence is quoted once (continuation §0.1 ruling 9) and pointed to elsewhere. Four words are defined once and used consistently: **representable** (the carrier has a canonical form for the machine state), **portable** (every value in it has a wire form), **satisfied** (the receiver has the code, primitives, modules and streams it names), **export-ready** (the holder may publish it: custody, tenure, no hold).\n\n#### 3.7.1 `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md`\n\n**§0.1, new ruling 9:**\n\n> 9. **Preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm`** (owner, 2026-10-17: \"preemptive migration, the ability to pause and continue at any PC, is a primary invariant of yin.vm. It is why yin.vm is defined as a continuation VM based on the CESK model. The UCF being only defined at a wait or parked state is a violation of this invariant\"). The carrier expresses the evaluator's complete task state between **any** two instructions, including the scheduler's products (remaining waits, completed wakes), not only its two delivery states; a safepoint is a boundary at which the program stops by itself. Slices 6d-a (carrier, reference lane), 6d-b (holder, custody, expiry) and 6e (backends) land it.\n\n**§0.2, first sentence**, replace \"A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register machine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return` frames, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait (frozen §2.4, §4.2)\" with:\n\n> A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register machine, which is a `run` state `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` at any instruction boundary (§1.3) or one of its two delivery states, `act` or `tail`, with the saved window, the delivery record and the pending wait the evaluator mints when it parks (frozen §2.4, §4.2); and a task is the product of its activation, its ordered waits and its ordered completed wakes (§2.6)\n\nand after \"`:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and `:parked-table` rows\" insert \", the `:run` and `:ready` rows (6d)\".\n\n**§1.1, fourth shape:**\n\n> ```clojure\n> run  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K}      ; between instructions; no deliver, no pending\n> ```\n> - `run` is the state `vm-hot` materialises when fuel runs out (`put-registers`, `semantic.cljc:142-143`, `:50-55`): `pc` is the instruction **about to execute**, `W` is trimmed by the carrier to `L(pc)` (`analysis/live`), `K` as for `act`. A `run` at `n` differs by kind from an `act` at `n`: the `act` has executed `n−1`, carries `saved(n, rd)` and awaits `rd`; the `run` has not executed `n` and carries `L(n)`, which may contain that `rd`.\n\n**§1.2**, keyed form after the tail state:\n\n> ```clojure\n> ;; run state (6d)\n> {:yin.k/state :run\n>  :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n                ; the instruction about to execute, 0 ≤ n < (count v)\n>  :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n>  :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly L(n); sorted by r on the wire\n>  :yin.k/k [ … ]}                            ; no deliver, no pending\n> ```\n\n**New §1.3 \"States between instructions, and the task product\":**\n\n> **1.3 States between instructions, and the task product.** The two delivery states of §1.1 are the states at which the evaluator parks by itself. They are special cases of its general state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, which `vm-hot` materializes whenever fuel runs out (`semantic.cljc:142-143`) and which the carrier expresses as the `run` state of §1.2. A step is atomic: effects are observed only inside a step or inside the scheduler's sweep, so no state between steps holds a half-issued effect; atomicity of a step is not transactionality of the outside world (an append performed before a host exception is not undone). A task is a **product**: at most one active activation (the `run`), its ordered pending waits, its ordered completed wakes (the ready queue: a woken wait with the value, status and cursor update its effect produced, carried by the `:ready` row of §2.3), its parked table, and its stores. The scheduler constructs every combination: resuming one of several woken waits leaves the others queued and the unresolved ones pending (`engine.cljc:2294-2312`, `:2305`). Every such state is **representable**. The lift refuses only what is not **portable** (a value with no wire form), not **satisfied** (code or dependencies the receiver lacks), or a **hold** (a driver-internal observation in flight: §7.1 step 1), never a pc. The static correspondence of §4 holds at every pc of a body: distinct virtuals in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` hold distinct physical registers (`derive/allocation`), and the stack kernel's operand region at A pc `p` is `stack-layout(p) ++ uses(p)` above a dead prefix (§4.3); both are checks (§5.6), not theorems. A root whose task has an active activation has kind `:running` (§2.6) and is a custody subject like a blocked or parked root (§7.3).\n\n**§2.3 row table**, two rows, one note:\n\n> | `:run` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref` | the between-instructions state; no delivery, no pending; five slots (an `:act` has six) |\n> | `:ready` | `wait-cref  value-V  status-D  cursor-cells-cref` | a completed wake: the wait it completes, the value its effect produced, the status the sweep recorded (a terminal status raises on resume exactly as on the immediate path), and the cursor cells it advanced (a `:next`'s successor cursor; empty for a `:put`) |\n\nand `:wait`: \"`state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row, never a `:run`\".\n\n**§2.6 root**: slots `17 run (crefs to :run rows; exactly one for :running, none otherwise)` and `18 ready (crefs to :ready rows, in queue order; none for :halted)`; `kind` comment `; :running | :blocked | :parked | :halted`; kind rules paragraph = the table of plan v2 §3.1 (also tuples §5.5). Add: \"A machine halted with a non-empty queue is kind `:blocked`: the scheduler resumes the queue before it reads the halt, so the halt value is dead.\"\n\n**§3.2**: S1, C2, C3, R1, R2, P1 as plan v2 §3.1's validator table (R3 is folded into S1: a `run` with a `deliver` or `pending` key is `:frame-shape`). \"`window-defect` is parameterised by the wanted set: `saved(pc, rd)` for an act and a return frame, `live(pc)` for a run; there is no `:window-self` analogue.\"\n\n**§3.3**: step 7 judges \"every wait's state in context `:wait`, the root's `:run` in context `:running`, every `:ready`'s wait in context `:wait`, every parked state in `:parked`, every reified value in `:captured`\".\n\n**§4.3** (stack layouts), replace \"**Checked** (§5.6 item 5) … dead layout syntax).\" with:\n\n> **Defined at every pc of every body.** `stack-layout(p)` is the ancestors' pending pushes for a parse node's pc; for a conditional's `:jump` pc `j` it is `stack-layout(b) ++ [rd(if)]` (`b` the branch pc, `rd(if)` the register both arms define: the consequent's value is on the stack while control jumps); for a body's terminator (`:return r`, `:halt r`) it is `[]`. **Checked** (§5.6 item 5), ordered: the current region is `stack-layout(p) ++ [W[u] for u in uses(p)]` in emitter order (operator then operands; `:stream-put` target then value), and as sets `stack-layout(p) ∪ uses(p) = L(p)` at every reachable non-tail pc, `uses(p) = L(p)` at every reachable tail-call pc. **Dead prefix.** A tail `:call` consumes its operator and operands and keeps the rest of its region without pushing a frame (`stack.cljc:731`, `:747`), so a region entered through a tail call may hold, below the layout, values the ambient layout syntax pushed and nothing reads again (every read is top-relative; a return truncates to a base that includes them). The lift takes the top `|stack-layout(p)| + |uses(p)|` values of the current region and the top `|stack-layout(pc_i − 1)|` of each frame's slice and drops the prefix; the lower produces none and recomputes bases cumulatively; the prefix is unobservable by law 1 and absent from the canonical form by laws 2 and 4. A `:push` pc of H is a stutter: its state equals its successor's but for pc, and the lift relabels it to `h->a(hpc+1)` without running anything.\n\n**§4.5**, last clause → \"never invents a state: a kernel pc outside every body is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2, as amended; the name is historical); every other kernel pc lifts, as a delivery state when the kernel is parked and as a `run` state when it is between instructions (6e); a `:push` pc of H is relabelled to its successor. Until 6e lands, a de Bruijn kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` as a documented limit of the backends, not of the carrier.\"\n\n**§5.4 law 4**: \"one program at one **aligned instruction boundary** (its parking boundaries included; the stack lane's `:push` stutters relabelled) lifts to row sets whose root ids are equal …\". Laws 1, 2, 6: \"at every boundary of the stepping driver (§5.7)\".\n\n**§5.6**, items 3, 5, 10:\n\n> 3. … at every reachable pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is single-valued on the physical image of `L(p)`; the `live-out*(p)` clause stays for parked entries.\n> 5. … the ordered region check and the set equations of §4.3 at every reachable pc; the parked-boundary form `(set (stack-layout p)) = saved(p+1, rd)` stays; duplicate operands are two positions holding one value and the lift asserts they agree.\n> 10. Stepping: for every case the reference lane's boundary count to the first park and to the halt is pinned per host; the three lanes agree on it after the stack lane's `:push` relabelling (`correspondence`).\n\n**New §5.7 \"The stepping driver\"** = plan v2 §3.6 (order, isolated worlds, coverage classes, \"every pc\" definition) in design voice.\n\n**§7.1 steps 1-2**, replace:\n\n> 1. **Holds.** Refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch` on an observation in flight (an `:observe` wait, a held entry, an unminted cursor, a pending close, a link request without its cursor): these are the holder's own seams mid-step, resolved by the driver's next cycle without executing guest code; they are not task states at rest. There is no quiescence refusal: a non-empty ready queue is carried (§2.3 `:ready`).\n> 2. **Kind.** `:running` when `:control` is set; else `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked table holds; else `:blocked` when the wait set or the ready queue is non-empty; else `:halted` with empty wait set and queue; anything else is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` (unreachable by construction).\n\nand in step 4: \"a `:running` task's state node is the control's `run` state with the window trimmed to `L(pc)` (`select-keys`), contributing the context `(A, pc, E)` to discharge (`live-pcs` from `pc`); each ready entry contributes its wait's state as a wait does, its value through the value walk and its cursor update through the census\". Delete \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set\" from the `canonical-task` paragraph: \"a kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` until 6e, then a `run` state through `lift-running`\".\n\n**§7.2**, restore: \"`:running`: `:control {:segment local :pc pc} :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K) :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set entries`; every kind: `:ready-queue` from the ready rows in order, each entry its wait plus `:value`, `:status` and `:resource-updates` re-keyed to the receiver's cells; nothing runs; `vm/run` or `run-fuel` continues from `pc`, or sweeps and resumes the first ready entry.\"\n\n**New §7.3 \"Custody and lifecycle of a running root\"** = plan v2 §3.3 in design voice, including the four outcomes, the sentence \"progress since the last durable checkpoint is lost on expiry; fuel makes the computation interruptible, exporting before the bound is what preserves it\", the gate guard, and \"a completed `:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed but not a retained write\".\n\n**New §7.4 \"Fuel\"** = plan v2 §3.4 including the honest guarantee verbatim.\n\n**§8.3**, item 12: \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel pc outside every body; a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts as a `run` state (6e; until then the backend refusal is pinned as a limit); a `:push` pc relabels\". Add §8.6 (6d-a), §8.7 (6d-b), §8.8 (6e) = plan v2 §3.9.\n\n**§9**: add \"**Claims that are checks**: `L(p)` is sufficient at every boundary (law 1 at every step); injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc (item 3); the ordered stack equation at every pc (item 5); the dead-prefix rule (law 1 after a tail transition); the refusal oracle equality (6e). None is a theorem about all programs; each is a gate on the corpus.\"\n\n**§11 Q6**: live, answered in 6d-a: keep \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"; a lift at the pc **before** a constant-dead branch refuses `ghost` as `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, pinned as a documented false refusal (derive, don't persist; the linker's conservative discharge).\n\n**Sites the review listed**: `:368-371` and `:403-408`: \"An exclusive **running**, blocked or parked root carries policy, occurrence, arbitration and next-op-seq, with origin on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries origin only.\" `:433-435`: contexts \"`:wait`, `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running`\". `:1249-1252`: \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a register kernel stepped one instruction past a `:const`, asked to lift) — **historical (phase 6); from 6e a stepped kernel lifts as a `run` state and only a pc outside every body refuses**\". `:2419-2425`: mark the paragraph \"historical amendment text (phase 6)\" and point to §5.6/§5.7 as the one normative conformance statement, adding the running-state check: \"and, at every boundary of the stepping driver, the run window's key set with `L(pc)`\". §4 and §5 inventories (static/runtime refusal lists): add `:ready-reason`, `:inconsistent-state`, the `:running` kind and context, the `:run`/`:ready` rows, and delete `:not-quiescent`.\n\n#### 3.7.2 `yin.vm.semantic.md` (frozen design)\n\n**§4.1 first paragraph**, replace \"A safepoint is a transition at which the machine parks. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** The machine's task state between any two instructions, `⟨seg, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` beside its ordered waits and completed wakes, is canonical and lifts (continuation design §1.3): preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm` (owner, 2026-10-17). A **safepoint** is a boundary at which the program stops by itself: a park with a pending wait, an explicit park with none, or a halt with a result. The table below enumerates those parking transitions; it is not the set of liftable pcs, which is every pc of every body. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n\n**§4.1 `:680-689`**: keep the explicit-park and reified-continuation sentences; replace \"Whole-task quiescence (empty ready queue, UCF §7.4.1) and the existing refusal conditions (unsupported observation states, pending close) are unchanged.\" with \"A non-empty ready queue is carried as ordered completed wakes (continuation design §2.3); the holds (observation in flight, pending close) remain refusals of the lift as driver seams mid-step.\"\n\n**§4.2**: add the run state (§3.7.1 keyed form); \"(for an `act`, `pc` is a safepoint successor …; for a `run`, `pc` is any pc of a body and the window is exactly `L(pc)`)\"; row carrier sentence: add \"`:run [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref]`, `:ready [wait-cref value status cursor-cells-cref]`\".\n\n**§4.3**, after the act and tail clauses:\n\n> and for a `run` state at pc `p`, produce the segment `A`, `p`, every register of `L(p)`, `E`, `K` with each frame's validated window and `rd`, and no delivery record or pending; a physical location outside `L(p)`'s image is never read, and an engine that cannot hold a value of `L(p)`, or of any carried frame's window or binding, refuses before attachment (the register kernel's `:continuation-registers`). `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` holds for admitted canonical frames of all three states; a stack image's `:push` pc relabels to its successor.\n\n**§10 phase 6**: append \"*(Amended 2026-10-16: the phase-6 harness proved the delivery states only; slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e extend the gate to every instruction boundary and to the scheduler's products under the owner's any-pc invariant.)*\"\n\n#### 3.7.3 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\n\n**Blocker 1 (`:17`)**: append \"**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc):** the parking transitions are where the program stops by itself; the set of liftable configurations is every instruction boundary of the register-shaped machine together with its scheduler state (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1), and the safepoint table enumerates the delivery states within it.\"\n\n**§7.2.1 (`:264-266`)**: prefix \"(versions 0-2; version 3 admits `run` states at every pc and completed wakes, continuation design §1.3)\".\n\n**§7.4.1 title and rule.** Title → \"7.4.1 Every instruction boundary is representable; the safepoint table is the machine's parking transitions\". Replace \"UCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be lifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the reference machine at which the machine parks.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17).** The register-shaped machine has no unserializable interior: its task state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` with its ordered waits and completed wakes, is canonical (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1; continuation design §1.3). **A continuation is representable at every instruction boundary**; the lift refuses a value with no wire form, a dependency the receiver cannot satisfy, or an observation in flight, never a pc. A *safepoint* is a boundary at which the program stops by itself: a park with a pending wait, an explicit park with none, or a halt with a result. The paragraph above describes the stack-shaped machine, which left at phase 8.\n\nKeyed-form block: add the `run` state. **Quiescence** paragraph (`:762-765`): replace with \"There is no quiescence requirement: a completed wake in the ready queue travels as a `:ready` row with its value, status and cursor update, and the receiver resumes it before any newly woken wait, in carried order.\"\n\n**§7.4.2** (`:800-825`): the conformance sentence (`:802`) → \"by a conformance test that runs the segment to **every boundary of the stepping driver** and compares the saved window's key set with `live − {rd}` at parks, the run window's key set with `live` between instructions, and the E-reads\"; rules → \"`lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on **every pc of A** the engine reaches; a pc that is no pc of A relabels (a `:push` of the stack image) or refuses (outside every body)\". Replace the `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` bullet with:\n\n> - A request to lift at a pc outside every body returns `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc; the name is historical, the meaning is \"no state of A exists here\". A stack image's `:push` pc is relabelled to its successor's A pc without running anything. An engine may not invent states a record does not have, and two engines that disagree about the state set of one address are not both conforming. An engine that cannot hold a value of the canonical frame refuses before attachment with its own kind (the register backend's `:continuation-registers`); that is a limit of the engine, not of the address.\n\nLast paragraph (`:823-825`): \"a debugger's breakpoint set is any subset of the pcs of A; the safepoint set is where the program will stop by itself\".\n\n**§7.7.4** step 3 (`:1901-1903`) → \"Neutralize control: a parked or blocked machine has no control; a machine between instructions (root kind `:running`) keeps its `:control`, `:window`, `:env` and `:k` as the state the lift reads, and the kernel refuses to step a machine whose gate is `:exporting` or `:ended` (`yin.vm.semantic/vm-hot`, one check at entry, outside the instruction loop), so the exporting machine is pure data in both cases; direct `:resume` …\". `:1912-1913` \"pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained export record — no engine change, no callback, no timer\" → \"pure data with its wait set, ready queue and parked records moved into the retained export record — one engine change at the kernel's entry (the gate guard), no change to the instruction loop, no callback, no timer\". `:1941-1942` \"A task is not at a liftable safepoint while it holds any of these\" → \"A task is not export-ready while it holds any of these; they are the driver's own seams and resolve in its next cycle without guest execution.\" In the \"(M-next D8/D9, as built.)\" paragraph, after \"under `:running` internal computation proceeds\" add \"for the holder's fuel budget per program tick (`driver/run-cycle`, `:fuel`), so a long computation is preempted at an instruction boundary and may be exported there; a holder whose tenure ends keeps the newest completed pure state for inspection and offers nothing; the occurrence is regrantable from its last durable checkpoint and progress since it is lost (continuation design §7.3)\".\n\n**§7.7.6** (`:2161`) \"runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\" → \"runs to its next park, halt, or fuel boundary\".\n\n**§7.8 Lift** step 1 (`:2621-2623`) → \"The machine is parked, blocked, halted or between instructions (`:running`), with a local record `r`, and holds no observation in flight, else `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`.\" Step 3 (`:2627-2628`) → \"`lift(export-record)`: confirm every pc is a pc of A (§7.4.1), else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; …\". Step 9 (`:2673`) → \"At the next park, halt, or fuel boundary, produce the successor occurrence …\".\n\n**§7.9** (`:2690`): `| :yin.k/not-at-safepoint | lift | :yin.k/segment, :yin.k/pc: the pc is outside every body of A |`; delete the `:yin.k/not-quiescent` row.\n\n**§7.10** (`:2839`): \"Resume happens only at an instruction boundary the canonical vector defines (a parking transition, or the boundary a holder's fuel budget stopped at); the successor chain …\". **`:2842` No layer collapsing** → \"The engine's instruction loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the machine's value at any boundary, the one engine change is a gate check at the kernel's entry, and the exporting state is that value with its waits, queue and parked records moved into a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery.\"\n\n**§7.11** (`:2931-2942`): rename \"Continuation conformance harness\"; \"to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table — effect-producing calls included —\" → \"to **every boundary of the stepping driver** (continuation design §5.7), the complete §7.4.1 table's parking transitions included,\"; the cross-engine sentence \"one program at one aligned instruction boundary\". **§7.11.1** (`:3100-3130`): Invariant → \"At every instruction boundary, lifting and lowering preserve the next observable step (and, at a parking transition, its wait; with a non-empty queue, its order); a pc outside every body refuses.\" Setup: \"a nonsafepoint pc\" → \"a `:push` pc of the stack image (relabels) and a body-start pc (lifts)\"; expected: \"An undeclared pc gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`\" → \"A pc outside every body gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; every pc of A lifts as a `run` state when the machine is between instructions; queued work travels as `:ready` rows and resumes in order\".\n\n#### 3.7.4 `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`\n\n**§1 stance item 1** (`:45-49`): append \"Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc): a *safepoint* is a parking `(segment-address, pc)`; the carrier expresses every `(segment-address, pc)` of A as a `:run` state when the machine is between instructions, and the scheduler's completed wakes as `:ready` rows.\" **§5.2**: `:run` and `:ready` rows; `:wait`'s note. **§5.5** (`:455-460`): the kind-rules paragraph = plan v2 §3.1 table; keep \"Frames restore in carried order and are never collapsed to one; a task may hold several ordered waits **and several ordered completed wakes**\". Custody table (`:466-473`): rows `root | running / blocked / parked | exclusive | the four keys plus origin on a successor` and `root | running / blocked / parked | fork | nil`; add \"a completed `:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed, not retained\". **§8 step 7** (`:797-801`): contexts \"(`:wait`, `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running`)\" and \"the root's `:run` and each `:ready`'s wait\". **§9 Lift** (`:880`): \"the merged order is holds, kind, free environment …\" (quiescence deleted); the canonical round-trip law (`:913-917`) unchanged (\"no execution between lower and lift\" is the law the harness now follows literally); **Cross-engine** (`:929`): \"Running the corpus to each aligned instruction boundary\". **§11** (`:1027`): \"Code identity (A), the parking transitions,\".\n\n#### 3.7.5 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md` §7 (`:560-579`)\n\nReplace \"Only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks at the existing engine export boundary are eligible. Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth kind.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** For the stack-shaped machine of versions 0-2, only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks were eligible and running snapshots were not a fourth kind. For the register-shaped machine and body version 3 this is reversed by the owner's invariant: a task between instructions is the fourth kind, `:running`, carried with its ordered waits and completed wakes (continuation design §1.3, §2.6), and is eligible for export and custody.\n\n#### 3.7.6 `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` §14.1\n\nTable 14.1.1 (`:3777-3788`): add `| Between instructions (:running) | Active control; ordered waits and completed wakes travel; the window is exactly L(pc); continue at pc. |`. `:3798` \"Static `:yin.safepoint/kinds` checks pc eligibility only\" → \"…checks a parking pc's kinds; a `run` pc is any pc of a body\". `:3827` → \"Validate the instruction boundary (§7.4.1 as amended); enter exporting before …\". `:3882` \"continue to the next safepoint\" → \"continue to the next park, halt or fuel boundary\". `:3900-3901` → \"A pc outside every body yields `not-at-safepoint`; queued work travels as `:ready` rows.\" `:4145` as UCF §7.8 step 9.\n\n#### 3.7.7 `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`\n\nNew dated entry I-9: \"2026-10-16, any-pc. The lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`) and a non-empty ready queue (`:1652-1653`), the holder runs to a park (`driver.cljc:2120`), and every custody kind table lists three kinds, in violation of the owner's primary invariant (2026-10-17). Slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e add the `:run` and `:ready` rows, root kind `:running`, validator rules R1-R2 and the extended S1/C2/C3/P1, custody for running roots, fuel, expiry semantics, control-flow stack layouts and the harness at every boundary. Section 8's 'check the pc against the static safepoint set' (`:602`) and 'the safepoint harness must run each 7.4.1 row' (`:622`) describe the stack-shaped machine and are historical.\" No compatibility promise.\n\n#### 3.7.8 The assessment's §F and the gap list\n\nAssessment §F disagreement 3 → \"Safepoints are the parking transitions, a proper subset of the representable boundaries, by the owner's invariant; its 'one necessity kept' (the ready-queue refusal) was wrong: the queue is carried.\" Its verdict line is superseded by this report. Gap list G7 (\"capability gap only\") superseded; the orchestrator log's next seat entry records the reclassification as a P1 defect against a primary invariant and cites this report. `docs/agents/known-gaps.md` gains the row:\n\n> | the lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`) and a queued completed wake (`:1652-1653`), the holder runs to a park (`driver.cljc:2120`) and loses a computed boundary on tenure loss (`:2252-2253`), the custody kind tables list three kinds, and the stack layout is undefined at jump pcs (`layout.cljc:118-131`): the carrier cannot express, and the holder cannot pause at, an arbitrary pc | owner invariant 2026-10-17; Architect plan v2 `collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md` | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | 6d-a, 6d-b, 6e | P1 |\n\n### 3.8 Complete inventory of safepoint-only assertions\n\nMethod: `grep -rn safepoint docs/ src/ test/` on `7c4dff32`, every hit classified. **Excluded as a different concept**: guest-visible event-delivery safepoints (`yang.safepoint`, `yang.stage`, `yang.python.antlr.safepoint`/`lower`/`prelude`, their tests `test/yang/**`, `test/yin/vm/semantic/scan_test.cljc:15,30`, `derive_test.cljc:9,637`, `stream_poll_test.cljc:2`, `docs/design/yang.antlr.md` (48 hits), `yin.vm.semantic.derivations.md:653,985,989`, `test.subsystems.md:78`, `src/dev/subsystems.edn:55`, `build-n-test.md:44`): delivery at an arbitrary pc would be implicit control flow visible to the guest; migration at an arbitrary pc is invisible. One footnote at `yang.antlr.md:1340-1341` makes the distinction. **Historical logs** (`docs/orchestrator-log.md`, 103 hits; `docs/cesk-space-optimization.md:85,117`): append-only; the next seat entry records supersession; no rewrite. **The driver's `:safepoint` phase name** (`driver.cljc:74,100,1122,1130,1796,2041,2147-2179,2309,2359`; `driver_test.cljc` 20 hits; `compose_test.cljc:509-551`; `compose_rows_test.cljc` 19 hits; `stage_e_scenarios.cljc` `[:until \"h1\" :safepoint n]` 14 hits): the phase means \"the machine has nothing left to drive, or the exit is armed\"; keep the name, fix the docstring at `:74-77` (\"halted, no wait, or the composition armed the exit over a machine at a park or an instruction boundary\"), and `at-safepoint?` (`:2147-2154`) keeps detecting the halt with a docstring saying so.\n\n**Rule statements to replace** (the sentence says \"only at a safepoint\" or an equivalent):\n\n| File:line | Text | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| `universal-continuation-format.md:17` | \"The safepoint set must be exactly the machine's parking transitions\" | §3.7.3 |\n| `:127`, `:151` | \"the safepoints of §4.1\"; \"the canonical CESK at a safepoint\" | \"the parking transitions\"; \"the canonical CESK at an instruction boundary\" |\n| `:264-266` | §7.2.1 resume pc is a static safepoint | historical prefix, §3.7.3 |\n| `:656`, `:663-664`, `:668-674` | \"Only safepoints lift\"; \"a continuation may be lifted only at a safepoint\" | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:692` | \"`:current-continuation` is not a safepoint: it does not park\" | keep (true) |\n| `:728-734` | linker safepoints (M4) | keep; \"safepoint\" here names the `:module/require` parking transitions |\n| `:762-765` | Quiescence paragraph | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:787-794` | the static safepoint map | keep the map; rename nothing (`:yin.safepoint/*` keys are the published wire names); add \"`def`/`use`/`live` are defined at every pc\" |\n| `:800-805` | conformance \"to each safepoint\" | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:807-825` | layout memo; \"total on safepoints\"; `:not-at-safepoint` bullet; breakpoint sentence | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:929` | wait entry's safepoint of the same name | keep (names a parking transition) |\n| `:1901-1903` | \"control and K are already absent\" | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:1912-1913` | \"no engine change\" | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:1941-1942` | \"not at a liftable safepoint while it holds any of these\" | reword, §3.7.3 |\n| `:2161` | \"runs to its next safepoint\" | amend, §3.7.3 |\n| `:2621-2628`, `:2673` | §7.8 steps 1, 3, 9 | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:2690` | outcome rows | redefine; delete `:not-quiescent`, §3.7.3 |\n| `:2839` | \"Resume happens only at a safepoint\" | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:2842` | \"hot loop untouched … over the blocked machine's value\" | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:2931-2942`, `:3100-3130` | harness blocker; 7.11.1 | replace, §3.7.3 |\n| `:3186`, `:3252` | \"the safepoint row\"; \"The safepoint block adds obligations\" | keep (\"row\" names the 7.11.1 row; \"block\" the section) |\n| `yin.vm.semantic.md:356` | \"κ is a captured value, never a safepoint\" | keep |\n| `:657-659` | §4.1 title and first sentence | replace, §3.7.2 |\n| `:680-689` | explicit park; reified; quiescence | keep the first two; replace the quiescence sentence, §3.7.2 |\n| `:733` | \"pc is a safepoint successor\" | act only, §3.7.2 |\n| `:765-768` | foreign engine's static safepoint map | keep; add the every-pc sentence of §3.7.2 §4.3 |\n| `:912` | \"windows are saved(p, rd) at reachable safepoints\" | \"at reachable parking pcs, and `L(p)` at every other reachable pc\" |\n| `:1030`, `:1065`, `:1135` | §10 records naming the harness and table | keep; `:1135` gets the phase-6 note, §3.7.2 |\n| `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:12`, `:97`, `:1116`, `:1122-1144`, `:1182`, `:1284`, `:2015` | the harness by name and its §5 description \"lift at the safepoint\" | file name and ns stay; `:1122` → \"lift at every boundary of the stepping driver, the parking boundaries included\"; the rest name the harness |\n| `:142-147` (§0.2), `:178-233` (§1.1-1.2), `:293-305`, `:381-392` (§2.3, §2.6) | shapes, rows, kinds | §3.7.1 |\n| `:368-371`, `:403-408` | custody prose, three kinds | §3.7.1 |\n| `:433-435` | `frame/defect` contexts | §3.7.1 |\n| `:473`, `:483-484`, `:528` | S1, C2, C3, P1 | §3.7.1 |\n| `:568` | \"frame from its state at a safepoint\" | \"at any instruction boundary\" |\n| `:805-810`, `:812-847` | §4.3 checked equation; K slices; tail lift discards | replace §4.3, §3.7.1 |\n| `:1020-1025` | §4.5 \"never invents a safepoint\" | replace, §3.7.1 |\n| `:1202`, `:1220`, `:1229`, `:1234` | row 11 synthetic `:running` phase; \"neither is an engine state at a quiescent safepoint\"; \"before any safepoint\" | row 11 becomes the engine's real state after 6d-a (a fuelled child); amend the three sentences to say so |\n| `:1249-1252` | refusal rows | historical note, §3.7.1 |\n| `:1333-1335` | law 4 \"one program at one safepoint\" | §3.7.1 |\n| `:1424-1450` | §5.6 items 2, 3, 5, 6 (\"at every safepoint reached\"), 9 (\"every non-tail safepoint\") | generalise, §3.7.1 |\n| `:1590`, `:1613`, `:1626-1630`, `:1651-1658` | §7.1 \"refuse unless quiescent\"; `:not-at-safepoint` of `canonical-task`; steps 1-2 | replace, §3.7.1 |\n| `:2148-2157` | §8.3 item 12 | replace, §3.7.1 |\n| `:2263-2268` | §9 checks | add, §3.7.1 |\n| `:2409-2425` | second conformance text | historical label, §3.7.1 |\n| `:2443` | cross-engine equality \"at one safepoint\" | §3.7.1 |\n| `:2492`, `:2566-2567` | amendment records | keep (records of earlier edits) |\n| `:2738-2740` | install child \"at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked`\" | \"a carried child is `:blocked` (wire phase `:parked`) or `:running` (wire phase `:running`)\" |\n| `:2792-2797` | §11 Q6 | answer, §3.7.1 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:45-49` | stance item 1 | §3.7.4 |\n| `:402-404` | install child \"retains at a safepoint\" | \"retains at a park, halt or fuel boundary\" |\n| `:455-460`, `:466-473` | kind rules; custody table | §3.7.4 |\n| `:797-801` | step 7 contexts | §3.7.4 |\n| `:880` | \"quiescence and safepoint, kind\" | §3.7.4 |\n| `:929` | cross-engine \"to each safepoint\" | §3.7.4 |\n| `:970` | \"safepoint reconstruction obligations\" | \"reconstruction obligations at every boundary\" |\n| `:1027` | §11 | §3.7.4 |\n| `universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md:560-579` | §7 eligibility | §3.7.5 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:4`, `:227-228`, `:312-314`, `:467`, `:542-544`, `:573-597`, `:655-659` | history of the static safepoint API | historical; entry I-9 points to them, §3.7.7 |\n| `:602-603`, `:622` | \"must check the pc against the static safepoint set\"; \"harness must run each 7.4.1 row\" | historical; I-9 |\n| `yin.vm.linker.dht.md:3742`, `:3759`, `:3777-3798`, `:3827`, `:3882`, `:3900-3901`, `:4145` | 14.1 | §3.7.6 |\n| `yin.vm.linker.md:47`, `:2460`; `code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:74`, `:780`; `semantic.evaluator.md:1544` | name the concept | keep |\n| `known-gaps.md:27`, `:36` | records | keep; add the new row |\n\n**Code: docstrings, comments, refusals.**\n\n| File:line | Text | Slice and disposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| `rows.cljc:1323`, `:1741` | \"walks a quiescent semantic machine\"; \"quiescent semantic machine m\" | 6d-a: \"a machine at any boundary: running, blocked, parked or halted\" |\n| `rows.cljc:1652-1653` | `:yin.k/not-quiescent` | 6d-a: deleted; ready rows |\n| `rows.cljc:1656-1657`, `:1663` | the two `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` refusals | 6d-a: the first becomes the `:running` kind; the second becomes `:inconsistent-state` |\n| `layout.cljc:33` | \"never invents a safepoint (§4.5)\" | 6e: \"never invents a state (§4.5)\" |\n| `layout.cljc:313-315`, `:318-330`, `:333-336` | `parking-ops` \"A1 …\"; `safepoint!`; `h->a` \"a :push has none and is no safepoint\" | 6e: `safepoint!` gains the run mode; `h->a` relabels a `:push` to its successor; docstrings say \"delivery state\" where they say \"safepoint\" |\n| `layout.cljc:469`, `:480`, `:528`, `:532` | `safepoint!` call sites | 6e: run mode |\n| `layout.cljc:715-725` | `canonical-task` \"A running kernel is :yin.k/not-at-safepoint\" | 6e: \"a running kernel lifts as :running\" |\n| `layout.cljc:118-131`, `:501-507` | `stack-layouts`; `slice-window` count equality | 6d-a: jump/terminator layouts; 6e: dead-prefix `≥` |\n| `driver.cljc:74-77`, `:318-321`, `:1119-1123`, `:2147-2154` | phase docstring; \"liftable safepoint (parked, blocked or halted)\"; `hand-off`; `at-safepoint?` | 6d-b: docstrings |\n| `export.cljc:274-282`, header | `kind-of` \"else :halted\"; `enter` queue refusal (`:107-114`, `:138`) | 6d-b: `:running` first; `first-refusal` deleted |\n| `compose.cljc:798-799`, `:842` | \"liftable safepoint, holding no custody\"; \"arms over the safepoint\" | 6d-b: \"at a park, halt or instruction boundary\" |\n| `ledger.cljc:277`; `authority/grant.cljc:74-75` | `#{:blocked :parked}` | 6d-b: add `:running` |\n| `rows.cljc:1261`, `:1297`, `:1704-1716`, `:1862`, `:1877-1882`, `:2264-2267` | kind tables, child check, restore arms | 6d-a |\n| `frame.cljc:96`, `:122`, `:124-127`, `:162-164` | `#{:act :tail}`; `(pos? n)`; C3; P1 | 6d-a |\n| `semantic.cljc:2`, `:137`, `:331-333` | kernel docstring; `vm-hot` entry; `step`/`run` | 6d-a adds `run-fuel`; 6d-b adds the gate guard; docstring names both |\n| `engine.cljc:1458-1491` | `advance-install` \"`running` runs it until it halts or parks\" | 6d-a: budgeted; docstring |\n| `safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1-20`, `:1012-1025` | docstring; \"not at a safepoint\" refusal on a stepped machine | 6d-a: docstring says what it now proves; the refusal row becomes the positive `running-kind` case |\n| `:1057-1066` | `a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent` | 6d-a: becomes `a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-carried` (law 1/2 on the F1 and F2 states) |\n| `:1886-1904` | `layout-refusals` \"a kernel stepped to a non-safepoint pc\" | 6e: positive; a hand-built entry outside every body still refuses; a `:push` entry relabels |\n| `stage_e_scenarios.cljc:184-186` | \"no liftable safepoint, UCF 7.4.1\" | 6d-b: \"a hold, UCF 7.7.4\" |\n\n### 3.9 Slices, order, parallelism, effort\n\nHosts: JVM, Node, Dart for every slice. Verification per `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`: iterate on `bb test:clj`; the three-lane quick tier at landing (`bb test:sub yin.vm`; confirm \"Testing yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test\" and every touched namespace in the Node log); the slow tier once at 6d-a landing and once at 6e. Every refusal test asserts zero serves (lift) or zero attaches (lower). Fresh worktree: `mise trust`, `npm ci`. Dart: `loop`/`reduce` over thousands of points (never `for`), frames and windows from `{}`, no floats in the driver. Node: `[:lit nil]` survives `select-keys`; the C2 float tamper is the canary.\n\n**Order and parallelism.**\n\n```\nmodule-pins commit 2 (merging now)  ──►  6d-a (branch srvm-6d-a from master after the merge)\n                                              │\n                                              ├──►  6d-b (srvm-6d-b)   ┐  in parallel; both review-gated\n                                              └──►  6e   (srvm-6e)     ┘  independently; 6d-b merges first\n                                                                            if both are green (the holder\n                                                                            closes the owner's invariant)\n```\n\n- Nothing of 6d runs in parallel with module-pins' merge except writing the 6d-a brief: 6d-a's `rows.cljc` hunks (`lift-task*`, `check-pendings!`, `check-install!`, `restore-task`, `lower-task` opts) are adjacent to or inside commit 2's (`discharge!`/`requires` `:1603-1700`, `check-modules!` `:1883-1963`, `lower-task` `:2273-2350`), and both re-mint `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt`. Branching after the merge re-mints once.\n- 6d-b and 6e are independent of each other and both depend on 6d-a's grammar. The owner's invariant is **not** closed by 6d-a alone (fork-only); 6d-b closes it for the holder.\n- After integration: re-run module-store/free-name discharge and receiver admission at mid-body contexts (module-pins' Q5 host-profile re-discharge meets `run` contexts there).\n\n#### 6d-a: the reference lane\n\n**Change.**\n- `frame.cljc`: S1 (`:96-98`) admits `:run` with its exact key set; C2 (`:117-123`) `(pos? n)` for act and return frames only; C3 (`:124-127`) skips the frame itself for a run; `window-defect` (`:49-61`) takes the wanted set and omits `:window-self` for a run; context `:running` in P1 (`:162-164`); A1-A6, T1 skipped for a run.\n- `rows.cljc`: `row-table` (`:49-69`) `:run`, `:ready`, task slots 17/18; `pending-table` unchanged; `lift-task*` (`:1650-1698`) steps 1-2 and nodes per §3.2; `state-node` (`:1508-1518`) run arm; `validate-frames` (`:1109-1144`) slot 17 in `:running`, slot 18's waits in `:wait`; `check-pendings!` (`:1877-1882`) the kind table; `check-install!` (`:1862`) child kinds by phase; `task-ops` (`:1261`, `:1263-1277`) and `custody-baseline` (`:1297`) kind set and completed-op rule; `protect!` unchanged (slot 13 only); `header-custody` (`:1704-1716`); `restore-task` (`:2253-2268`) running arm and ready restore; `lower-task` docstring/opts; `rows->frame`/`frame->rows` (`:1023-1058`, `:1082`) the run shape; `rows/checkpoint` inspector follows `custody-baseline`.\n- `layout.cljc`: `stack-layouts` (`:118-131`) jump and terminator layouts; `state-prim`/`lower-defect` (`:863-874`, `:916-953`) `:run` arm and slots 17/18 (so a primitive in a running window refuses into the register backend before attach even before 6e).\n- `engine.cljc`: `advance-install` takes a budget (`:1458-1491`); `scheduler-round-fuel` beside `scheduler-round` (`:2315-2326`).\n- `semantic.cljc`: public `run-fuel` (§3.4); no change to `vm-hot`'s loop (the gate guard is 6d-b).\n- Tests: `frame_test`, `rows_test`, `safepoint_harness_test` (stepping driver, laws at every boundary, quick sample, tampers, ready/product cases), `layout_test` (static checks), `semantic_vm_test` (`run-fuel`), `engine_test` (budgeted children).\n- Docs: §3.7.1 (continuation §0.1, §0.2, §1.1-1.3, §2.3, §2.6, §3.2-3.3, §4.3, §5.4, §5.6-5.7, §7.1-7.2, §7.4, §8.3, §8.6, §9, §11, the six sites), §3.7.2, §3.7.3 UCF §7.4.1-7.4.2, §7.9; §3.7.4; §3.7.5; §3.7.7; the known-gaps row.\n\n**Depends on.** Module-pins commit 2 on master.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n1. `frame-test/run-state-rules`: a `:run` built from `run-fuel` at every boundary of `:worked-example` validates in context `:running`; one tamper per rule: C2 (a float pc → `:pc-kind`; `pc = (count v)` → `:pc-bounds`); a **body-start pc validates**; R1 (a key `≥ k(body)` → `:window-scope`); R2 (one live key removed → `:window-missing`; one dead register added → `:window-extra`); S1 (a `deliver` key, or a `pending` key, on a `run` → `:frame-shape`); P1 (a `run` in context `:wait` → `:pending-presence`; an `act` in context `:running` → `:pending-presence`); an `act` at `p+1` and a `run` at `p+1` with `rd` present both validate and each is refused under the other's kind.\n2. `rows-test/run-and-ready-rows`: `frame->rows ∘ rows->frame` identity on every `:run` of item 1; `:run` is five slots (a sixth is `:row-shape` at decode step 2 by raw edit with rehash); a `:wait` whose state cref names a `:run` is `:undecodable` at step 3; a `:ready` whose wait cref names a `:run` row, or whose status is not a keyword, is `:undecodable`; a `:ready` with reason other than `:next`/`:put` is refused at lift `:yin.k/non-portable :ready-reason` with zero serves.\n3. `rows-test/task-product-kinds`: (a) `run-fuel` mid-body → kind `:running`, one `:run`, no waits, no ready; lower restores `:control` at the pc with the trimmed window and runs nothing (zero steps observed through the toy channel); `vm/run` reaches the reference result and trace; relift equals the lift under `canonical-under` in a fresh receiver. (b) **F2 state** (two-callers, one response, sweep, resume): kind `:running` with one `:run` and one `:wait`; law 1 (the remaining waiter completes after the receiver's own response), law 2. (c) **F1 state** (both responses, sweep, resume, `run-fuel 1`): kind `:running` with one `:run` and one `:ready`; law 1 (the queued wake resumes before any new wake, its value delivered once, nothing re-appended: the receiver's call-out stream receives no second request), law 2. (d) the state after `check-wait-set` alone (control nil, waits 1, ready 1): kind `:blocked` with one wait and one ready. (e) a halted activation beside a queued wake (hand-built per §3.1): kind `:blocked`, restored flags `blocked? true`, law 1. (f) negative controls: a `:running` root with two `:run` crefs, with none, with a parked-id, with a result; a `:halted` root with a `:ready`; a `:blocked` root with neither waits nor ready: each `:yin.k/undecodable :kind` (or `:parked-id`) before any attach.\n4. `harness/every-boundary-matches-the-reference` (`^:slow`, `slow/guard`): the stepping driver per §3.6 over the 89 cases (`run-fuel 1`, not `vm/step`); at every boundary with `:control` set: lift, lower into an isolated world, immediate relift (law 2), law 6 with the two window tampers, run to the end (law 1); boundary counts to the park and to the halt pinned per case per host (re-measure once; v1's numbers are the assessment's estimate); the coverage classes of §3.6 each reached; the point count asserted.\n5. `harness/sampled-boundaries-match-the-reference` (quick tier): per bare row (22) five structural boundaries (first body-start reached, the pc after the first primitive `:var`, the pc before the parking instruction, the first `:jump` or `:branch-false` reached, the last `:return` before the halt) plus every boundary of rows 1 and 20; laws 1, 2, 6; about 150 points.\n6. `harness/products-and-refusals`: items 3(b)-(e) through the harness's lift/lower path; a `:run` window holding a cursor-ref, a reified continuation and a nil-valued live register round-trips (rows 14, 16, 22 at the boundary after the capture or the nil load); the `ghost` case lifted at the pc **before** the constant-dead branch refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{ghost}}` (Q6 pinned); a held `:observe` still refuses `:reason-mismatch` with zero serves.\n7. `layout-test/static-checks-at-every-pc`: on `layout-corpus` and the matrix's vectors, at every reachable pc: `virt` single-valued over `L(p)`'s physical image; the ordered region form has `|layout(p)| + |uses(p)| = |L(p)|` with duplicates counted, and the set equations of §3.5; **the jump pc of every conditional and every terminator has a layout** (the F5 vectors pinned); sizes asserted per segment.\n8. `engine-test/budgeted-install-children`: a child whose initializer loops for thousands of instructions is stopped by `run-fuel n` at a boundary (phase `:running`, `:control` set), lifted as a `:running` child, lowered under receiver admission, continued to `linked`; a nested initializer; a tick over a tree of `t` tasks executes at most `n × t` instructions (counted through a probe primitive).\n9. `semantic-vm-test/run-fuel`: `run-fuel n` on an active machine equals `n` applications of `vm-hot … 1` (record equality minus telemetry fields); on a blocked machine it is one budgeted round; `maybe-run`'s bridge path is exercised once.\n10. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log shows every touched namespace; the slow tier once at landing on three hosts.\n\n**Days.** 3 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = 4.5; docs 1 beside it.\n\n#### 6d-b: holder, custody, expiry, recovery\n\n**Change.**\n1. `driver.cljc` `run-cycle` (`:2111-2144`): `:fuel` config key (checked in `check-common!` `:177-207`, positive integer or nil); `(if fuel (sr/run-fuel m0 fuel) (vm/run m0))` under the existing gate and halted guards; **each stage's machine assoc'd into the state before the next tenure check**; `cycle-result` adds `:yin.k/at-boundary true`; `step-active`'s catch ends the run on the state it was handed and records `:yin.k/retained-boundary`; `end-run` keeps the retained machine on the ended state (gated `:ended`, never offered).\n2. `export.cljc`: `kind-of` (`:274-282`) `:running` first; `enter` (`:121-149`) drops `first-refusal` and moves the ready queue into the record (`:ready []` on the machine; `:yin.k/recovery-ready` on fenced restore); `recovery-metadata`/`valid-state?` count ready entries; `abort` unchanged (its conditions are right).\n3. `semantic.cljc` `vm-hot` entry gate guard (§3.3 outcome 4); `run-fuel` inherits it.\n4. `ledger.cljc:277`, `authority/grant.cljc:74-75`: admit `:running`.\n5. Docstrings per §3.8 (driver, compose, export header).\n6. Tests: `driver_test`: a fuelled run preempts a long pure loop at a boundary, the phase stays `:running`, the next tick continues; `hand-off` mints, journals, freezes and offers a `:running` pack; a candidate lowers it (awaiting-grant → grant → `activate`) and reaches the reference result; the five clock-controlled cuts of §3.3 with their assertions; `rehydrate-fenced` byte equality for a running root with a wait and with a ready entry, and the rehydrated machine refuses `vm/step` (\"Gate is closed\"); two competing candidates; stale-source stepping refused; uncertain-offer abort refused; expired-grant admission refused; a halted exclusive root still restores without a grant (F6 regression). `ledger_test`/`grant_test`: a `:running` baseline is offerable; `:halted` is not. `compose_rows_test`: an exclusive world handed off at a boundary completes on the successor; one with a running install child. `stage_e_scenarios`: `[:until \"h1\" :running n] [:hand-off \"h1\"]` with fuel so the hand-off lands mid-body; recovery cut before and after the offer; a tenure-expiry cut between slices asserting the last checkpoint is what recovers.\n7. Docs: §3.7.1 §7.3; §3.7.3 UCF §7.7.4, §7.7.6, §7.8, §7.10, §7.11, §7.11.1, `:2842`; §3.7.6.\n\n**Depends on.** 6d-a. **Days.** 2.5 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 4.\n\n#### 6e: the cross-engine half\n\n**Change.** `layout.cljc`: `lift-running`/`lower-running` over the kernels' live fields (register `{:pc :registers :frames :continuation :store-of}`, stack `{:pc :stack :frames :continuation :store-of}`); `virt` over a given virtual set (`L(p)` for a run); `register-window` parameterised; nil fill outside `L(p)`; `h->a` relabels `:push`; `slice-window`/`lift-stack` dead-prefix rule (`≥`, prefix dropped, diagnostic); `lower-stack`/`stack-k` unchanged in shape; `safepoint!` run mode (any pc inside a body); `canonical-task` lifts an active kernel as `:running` (with waits and ready as the semantic lift does; backends still refuse installs); `kernel-task` lowers a `:running` machine into a running kernel. No kernel edit. `layout_test`, the harness: laws 4 and 5 at every aligned boundary on the 61 lane cases.\n\n**Acceptance.** Law 4: one root id on three lanes at aligned boundary `i` (stack index after `:push` relabelling), taken and untaken branches both pinned, ordered operand multiplicity pinned; law 5: six ordered pairs at every boundary, each continuing to the reference result, with the **full-payload refusal oracle** of §3.6 (exact refusals and exact successful transfers per case and direction); the `:push` relabelling pinned on one row; **item 6, tail residuals**: either a lane-fragment case with a non-empty ambient layout at a tail call exercises the dead-prefix rule (lift drops, lower omits, law 1 and law 4 hold, the diagnostic reports the count) or the static check `layout(p) = []` at every tail-call pc of the fragment proves none exists and the rule is pinned on a hand-built vector; `layout-refusals` rewritten: a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts on both lanes with zero serves refused; a hand-built entry outside every body refuses `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; the fragment is not widened (rows 7-12 and 17 excluded; row 4 not lowered).\n\n**Depends on.** 6d-a; independent of 6d-b. **Days.** 3.5 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 5.\n\n#### Effort\n\n| Slice | Implementation days | Review rounds | Days |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6d-a reference lane, grammar, fuel, static layouts | 3 | 3 | 4.5 |\n| 6d-b holder, custody, expiry, recovery | 2.5 | 3 | 4 |\n| 6e cross-engine | 3.5 | 3 | 5 |\n| docs (§3.7, §3.8), split across the three | 1.5 | 1 | 1.5 |\n| **Total** | **10.5** | | **15**, or 10 without 6e |\n\nWhat the review said to keep: targeted counterexamples (F1, F2, F5 states), the all-pc static gates, the quick structural sample, one full three-host run at landing, every custody race and scheduler product. What may be cut during iteration: repetitive broad end-to-end cases. No wall-time is forecast; the slow tier's duration is measured once at 6d-a landing and recorded in the ledger row.\n\nRouting: codex (gpt-6.1-sol, workspace-write, stdin closed), one brief per slice, foreground lanes only, report inside the worktree's `collab/`; Fable reviews at every gate under the Q6 rule; glm/cmd as non-author fallback; commit only on Architect sign-off; never commit `collab/` or `docs/agents/routing-status.md`.\n\n### 3.10 Decisions for the owner (defaults stated so 6d-a can start)\n\n1. **Task slots 17 (`run`) and 18 (`ready`)**, re-minting every pinned root once right after module-pins commit 2. Alternative: a mixed slot 13 `[:refs :wait :run :ready]`, no re-mint, every slot-13 traversal audited for three row kinds. **Recommend 17/18**: it removes F7's misread class by construction, and the re-mint coincides with one already happening.\n2. **Fuel as a holder config key `:fuel`**, default nil (today's behaviour; nothing changes until a composition opts in). **Recommend as stated.**\n3. **No automatic export at the bound in 6d-b**; `hand-off` stays explicit; `:yin.k/at-boundary` is the signal. A later \"arm when remaining tenure < one renewal interval\" policy belongs in `compose`, needs no VM clock. **Recommend as stated.**\n4. **No adoption of a retained boundary**: an ended holder keeps `:yin.k/retained-boundary` for inspection only; recovery is the last durable checkpoint; the documents state the loss. A future ledger rule \"a regrant to the same holder may continue from its retained boundary when the input prefix and next-op-seq agree\" is possible but is new authority and not in 6d. **Recommend as stated.**\n5. **Q6**: keep edge-reachability liveness; pin the pre-branch `ghost` refusal. A \"no\" means a constant-propagation pass in `live-pcs`, a separate slice. **Recommend keep.**\n6. **Completed `:put` wakes are completed operations**: op-id well-formed, excluded from retained writes and `protect!`, never re-appended. **Recommend as stated** (the alternative, counting them as retained, would make the receiver re-append a datom that is already on the stream).\n7. **`:push` relabelled to its successor**, not refused. **Recommend relabel** (no guest semantics run; the stack content is already its successor's).\n8. **Holds stay refusals** (`:observe`, held, unminted cursor, pending close, link cursor not installed), documented as driver seams mid-step that the driver resolves without guest execution; 6d-b verifies each resolves within one cycle and gives any that does not a known-gaps row. **Recommend as stated.**\n\n### 3.11 Risks\n\n1. **A liveness bug surfaces** at a pc no park looked at: law 1 fails there. A finding, not a slice risk; the main reason to run the every-boundary laws.\n2. **The ready carrier meets a wake reason I did not enumerate** (a link wake entering the queue): pinned as `:ready-reason` refusal in 6d-a and reported; widening is a small follow-up, not a redesign.\n3. **Re-mint churn**: two re-mints in a week (module-pins, 6d-a). Mitigation: order (§3.9); 6d's own acceptance pins boundary counts and refusal kinds, never root ids.\n4. **Dead-prefix rule untested on a real case** if the fragment has none: 6e item 6 pins it on a hand-built vector; the rule is then a documented check awaiting a natural case.\n5. **Slow-tier time on Dart**: slow tier only; measured once.\n6. **Gate guard cost**: one map read per `run-fuel`/`vm-hot` call; a reviewer confirms it is outside the loop.\n7. **Fuel too small or too large**: a composition knob; default nil.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Verification honesty\n\n- Every `file:line` above was read in `7c4dff32` today. The review's citations held in every case I checked; where it cited a function by name I read the body.\n- **Run**: one throwaway JVM test namespace reproducing F1, F2 and F5 (transcript excerpts in §1); deleted; `git status` shows only `collab/`. **Not run**: the harness, the slow tier, any Node or Dart lane, any driver or Stage E scenario, any performance probe. Boundary counts and point counts are to be measured once and pinned by 6d-a.\n- **Claims that are checks** (not theorems): `L(p)` sufficient at every boundary; injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc; the ordered stack equation with the jump/terminator layouts at every pc; the dead-prefix rule; the refusal-oracle equality; cross-host boundary-count equality; the per-tree fuel bound.\n- **Not claimed**: that the de Bruijn kernels reconstruct a `run` state today (6e); that the holder can preempt today (`vm/run` at `driver.cljc:2120`); that fuel bounds wall-clock time; that fuel repairs expiry loss (only exporting before the bound does); that a tail call with a non-empty ambient layout exists in the fragment (6e decides); that link wakes never enter the queue (6d-a verifies by reading `poll-links`); that module-pins commit 2's final hunks are exactly those of `origin/srvm-module-pins` at `62ed14fa` (they are what is merging now).\n- **Timestamps**: the brief dates itself 2026-10-17 21:00 +07 and the owner's ruling 2026-10-17; the machine clock at completion reads 2026-10-16 14:40 +07. The header uses the clock; the ruling's date is quoted as the brief gives it.\n\n## 5. Readiness\n\n**Implementation-ready**: 6d-a, as specified in §3.9, under the defaults of §3.10, **the moment module-pins commit 2 is on master**; its brief can be written now from §3.1, §3.2, §3.4, §3.5 (static part), §3.6, §3.9 and the §3.7 text it owns. 6d-b and 6e are specified to the same level and are ready to brief when 6d-a merges; they run in parallel. **Start first**: 6d-a. The owner's invariant is closed for the holder only when 6d-b lands; the plan says so and does not call it closed earlier.\n\nPLAN-V2-READY\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat review-inputs/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat review-inputs/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-16 07:19:41 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-16 14:19:41 +07\nCoding-Agent: codex (GPT-6; exact deployment model not exposed to this session)\nSession-ID: 01a14392-2b1d-7081-a47e-76c8cc6832b7\n\n# Independent second architect review: slice 6d\n\n**Verdict: REJECT the plan as implementation-ready.** The `:run` activation is the right extension, and a running exclusive root must be a custody subject. However, this plan still excludes supported scheduler states, leaves an unbounded child-execution path, and specifies a false all-PC stack equation. These are defects in the proposed closure of the owner's invariant, not reasons to reconsider that invariant.\n\nReviewed HEAD: `7c4dff32e8633a53c361bf7f34282d51fcf70363`, rather than the plan's `ce76c846`. Citations below refer to files read in this worktree. `plan` means `collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md`; `assessment` means `collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md`. I read both, the requested architectural documents, and the implementation paths discussed below. The prompt's `docs/design/universal-continuation-format.md` does not exist here; its actual path is `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (abbreviated `UCF` below). The clock is 2026-10-16; the owner's 2026-10-17 ruling is treated as supplied instruction, without inventing a corrected date.\n\nThis is a source-based review. No VM, test suite, performance probe, network operation, or build was run. The counterexamples are deductions from the cited transitions; their runtime reproduction remains an implementation acceptance requirement. Only this report was written.\n\n## Findings\n\nEach entry gives **severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | recommended correction**. P1 blocks the proposed architectural acceptance; P2 is an implementation or specification gap that must be resolved in the affected slice.\n\n### F1 — The ready-queue exception contradicts arbitrary-PC migration\n\n**P1, architectural defect | plan:148; src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2205; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc:107 | any-PC continuation completeness | carry scheduler state or establish a semantics-preserving, bounded normalization at the same guest PC.**\n\nThe plan explicitly admits an active activation with another completed wake queued, yet requires running the active activation to a park before export. `run-loop` prioritizes the active activation (`engine.cljc:2211`) over the queue (`:2216`); `vm-hot`'s active path never drains that queue (`semantic.cljc:139`). A recursively calling pure activation can run indefinitely. Retrying `hand-off` after another fuel slice can therefore refuse forever, or until the lease expires. The fact that the machine began with multiple waits does not make it malformed: the existing transport expressly preserves ordered multiple waits (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:455`), and the harness transports `two-callers` (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1069`, `:1093`).\n\nThe result cannot be discarded or reclassified as a pending wait: the wake carries an already observed value/status and cursor resource updates (`engine.cljc:443`). Repeating a completed write can duplicate it; dropping a completed read loses it. The assessment already recognized representability (assessment:101, section B.1); its decision to refuse is no longer compatible with the owner's ruling.\n\nSpecify an ordered ready-outcome carrier, including terminal outcomes as deferred scheduler outcomes, cursor-cell aliasing, resource updates and custody implications. Alternatively prove a transformation that preserves the current PC, current activation, queued outcomes and scheduling order without executing arbitrary guest work. Do not describe “run until it parks” as such a transformation. Add a migration case with two completed wakes and an unbounded first activation, and prove export without executing that activation further.\n\n### F2 — R0 rejects a state that the scheduler constructs\n\n**P1, architectural defect | plan:96; src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2294; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:116 | no information loss; supported task domain | represent an active activation together with remaining waits.**\n\nClearing control when creating a wait does not prove that control and a wait set can never coexist. Start from the admitted two-waiter machine, complete only one response, then execute `scheduler-round`. `check-wait-set` retains the unresolved wait (`engine.cljc:2194`); `resume-from-run-queue` pops the completed entry, clears blocked/halted flags and leaves the remaining wait set (`:2305`); `register-restore` sets control without clearing those waits (`semantic.cljc:116`). The result is active control plus a nonempty wait set, with no ready queue required. Explicit resume likewise restores into the existing machine (`engine.cljc:2360`).\n\nThis is an admitted blocked carrier resumed by the kernel, even if a single-control program does not initially create two waits on its own. R0 cannot call it inconsistent merely because the new running grammar has no place for it. F1 and F2 require a task-state product (current activation, ordered pending waits, ordered ready outcomes), not just a mutually exclusive fourth root arm. The activation-level `:run` row can remain as proposed. Update the kind rules and distinguish root classification from activation shape. Test one-woken/one-waiting as well as two-woken cases; do not manufacture them only as negative tests.\n\n### F3 — Fuel does not bound an install child or the entire program tick\n\n**P1, architectural defect | plan:113; src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1458; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1862 | preemption at every executing task's boundary | propagate explicit execution budgets through scheduling and child execution, and admit running install children.**\n\nThe proposed `run-fuel` calls an unrestricted `scheduler-round` when the root is inactive. That round polls links and advances installs (`engine.cljc:2168`; `poll-links`/`advance-installs`); `advance-install` calls `vm/run` on the child (`:1475`). A blocked root installing a module whose initializer computes indefinitely still never returns to the holder. A one-instruction root fuel budget does not help. The assessment's “child states are unreachable unless the sweep takes fuel; not needed” conclusion is precisely the gap the new invariant makes necessary to close.\n\nDefine how a budget reaches child tasks, how it is shared or bounded per tree, and how a child remains at a materialized boundary when exhausted. Current lower admits only a blocked child (`rows.cljc:1862`); the plan neither changes that check nor supplies running-child phase acceptance. Include a long initializer, a nested initializer, and handoff/recovery while the child is running. Retain receiver admission checks for each child.\n\nFuel also counts VM instructions, not elapsed time: `:call` invokes a host function synchronously (`semantic.cljc:193`), stream construction invokes the supplied constructor (`engine.cljc:480`), and a scheduler round can scan many entries. State the cooperative guarantee precisely: interruption between completed instructions, with no hard wall-clock bound for one atomic instruction or unbounded host seam. Avoid promising a lease deadline from `n` instructions alone. No clock-to-instruction conversion belongs in the VM.\n\n### F4 — A tenure failure still loses the just-computed boundary\n\n**P1, plan/implementation gap | plan:115; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:2118; :2248 | explicit state retention and exclusive authority | return the new boundary as fenced data on tenure loss, and specify which authority may adopt it.**\n\nAfter `m1` is computed, `require-tenure!` at `driver.cljc:2123` may throw before `m1` is associated with holder state (`:2139`). The catch at `:2253` calls `end-run` on the original argument, not on `m1`. Fuel changes neither this control flow nor the later throws inside replay/emit/read. When tenure dies between completed ticks, the next entry check also ends the run rather than making a successor. `exit-mint` itself requires tenure (`:1144`).\n\nThe plan's sentence that a holder can hand off *before* the bound is useful composition policy, but it does not resolve its claim about tenure dying between slices. Specify separate outcomes for successful preemption while tenure is live, expired-tenure retention, and recovery from the last durable checkpoint. Retain the newest completed pure state before a post-slice check can discard it. An expired holder must not offer a valid successor or resume merely because it still owns bytes: adoption needs authority consistent with the ledger's existing origin/completion rules. If expiry intentionally replays the last committed checkpoint, say that progress since it is lost and do not call the invariant fully repaired by fuel.\n\nAdd clock-controlled cuts immediately before the slice, immediately after it, during the later driver stages, and between slices. The source must remain fenced, with either a durably authorized successor or an explicitly documented recovery checkpoint. Keeping automatic export a composition decision is sound; leaving the expiry semantics unspecified is not.\n\n### F5 — The all-PC stack equation is false with today's layout\n\n**P1 for 6e, specification defect also blocking 6d-a item 7 | plan:322; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc:118; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/code.cljc:349 | every-PC reconstruction | derive layouts at control-flow and terminator PCs, not just expression nodes.**\n\n`stack-layouts` walks `(:expr body)` and its `:children`, assigning only each node's `:pc`. The parser stores a conditional's jump in `:jump`, not as a child (`code.cljc:389`), and excludes the body terminator from the expression (`:413`). Thus the purported existing layout is not defined at every PC.\n\nA concrete structured conditional is:\n\n```clojure\n[[:const 1 true]             ; 0: test\n [:branch-false 1 4]         ; 1\n [:const 0 10]               ; 2: consequent result\n [:jump 5]                   ; 3\n [:const 0 20]               ; 4: alternate result\n [:halt 0]]                  ; 5\n```\n\nAt PC 3, `L(3) = #{0}` by `analysis/successors` and `:halt`'s use, while `stack-layout(3)` is nil and `uses(:jump)` is empty. The planned equality therefore reads `#{} = #{0}` and fails. The stack kernel really holds `[10]`: `:const` pushes it (`debruijn/stack.cljc:689`), and `:jump` preserves it (`:777`). The running lift must identify that value as the conditional's result register. At the halt, the result is also on the stack although there is no expression-node layout entry; its operand use supplies the mapping there.\n\nAdd branch-result layouts at jumps/joins, explicit empty ambient layouts at terminators where appropriate, and path-sensitive dynamic checks of ordered stack contents. Audit all instruction categories rather than checking the set equation alone. Tail-call residuals also require checking **after** entering the callee: the stack closure-call transition retains `stack'` on a tail call (`stack.cljc:737`–`:748`), so dead caller operands can survive below a running callee. Dropping them only at the tail-call PC does not establish the layout at the next body-start boundary. Test a tail closure call in operand position with nonempty ambient operands and live `K`.\n\nThe register-allocation argument is substantially stronger: intervals include `L(p) ∪ def(p)`, and a slot expires only when its end is strictly less than the new PC (`derive.cljc:127`, `:140`). No non-parking-specific register collision was found. This does not validate the separate stack claim.\n\n### F6 — Grant-check instruction contradicts the intended halted behavior\n\n**P2, concrete specification error | plan:336; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:2039 | custody by kind | leave the halted exemption intact.**\n\nThe plan says to “drop `(= :halted kind)` from the skip only; keep `fenced?`.” Current code skips grants only for fenced restoration or a halted root. Running roots already take `accept-grant!` once admitted by the surrounding grammar. Removing the halted test instead requires a grant for an origin-only terminal result, contradicting plan:53 and UCF's result-is-not-a-lease-subject rule (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:473`). Change the brief to “preserve `check-custody!` dispatch; extend admitted kinds elsewhere,” and pin halted restoration without a grant as a regression case.\n\n### F7 — The primitive refusal predicate is broader than a primitive in L(pc)\n\n**P2, 6e acceptance gap | plan:349; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc:853; :947 | refuse unsupported values before attachment | compute the expected refusal set over the complete payload.**\n\nThe existing predicate checks current windows, lexical bindings, nested collections/closure captures, every return frame, and parked states (`state-prim`, `frame-prim`, `value-prim`). A primitive can remain in a lexical binding or saved `K` frame while no primitive is in the current `L(pc)`. Equating every register-target refusal count with only the current primitive-live PC set is therefore not generally correct. The harness already has lexical, captured and nested primitive refusal fixtures (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1931`).\n\nExtend the expected set to all payload roots, independently of the implementation traversal, and assert exact refusals **and successful transfers** per case/direction. Update callers too: `lower-defect` currently feeds `(first (:slots w))` to `state-prim` because slot 13 contains `:wait` rows (`layout.cljc:947`); adding only a `:run` branch to `state-prim` will not make it receive the new direct `:run` row. Audit every slot-13 wait destructuring, including restore's `entry` mapping, for the new sum. Do not claim the full three-obstruction list is complete: existing image collision, free-env, install/link and retained-FFI limits remain applicable.\n\n### F8 — The harness description would compare the wrong state and mutate its fixture\n\n**P2, implementation acceptance gap | plan:319; docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:916 | law 2 has no intervening execution; append-only effect discipline | fork each test point before running it, and compare relift immediately after lower.**\n\nThe listed order says lower, run to the end, then relift for root equality. That produces a halted or parked root rather than the captured running root. The actual law explicitly requires **no execution** between lower and lift. Write the order as lift, lower, immediate relift/law 2, then run/law 1.\n\nAlso isolate each snapshot's resource world. Running the receiver to completion on the stepping source's mutable test streams can append effects or consume scripted outcomes needed by subsequent snapshot points. The existing ring/toy fixtures perform real stream operations. Use independent equivalent worlds or a deterministic input/effect script with fresh identities normalized by the existing correspondence. Preserve negative controls for cursor positions/descriptors, windows and lexical values.\n\n“Every PC” in a dynamic driver means every boundary of the chosen execution, not every CFG-reachable PC or every possible activation. Pin coverage by `(segment, pc, structural state)` and add both branch outcomes, body entries, jumps, returns, tail transitions, resume/capture, scheduler products and install children. The static all-PC pass and dynamic path pass are complementary; neither proves a universal theorem by itself.\n\n### F9 — The new document text overstates liftability and its inventory is incomplete\n\n**P2, documentation defect | plan:254; plan:408; docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:1250 | consistent executable contract | distinguish representable state, portable values, satisfied dependencies and export readiness.**\n\nThe amendments announce “Every instruction boundary lifts,” but retain queues and holds as refusals and intentionally pin the pre-branch `ghost` false refusal (plan:321). UCF's proposed safepoint definition also says it carries a pending wait (plan:256), although explicit park is a no-wait safepoint and halt carries a result (`yin.vm.semantic.md:680`). Define a safepoint as a program-driven park/halt boundary, with delivery/pending only for the appropriate cases. Define any-PC **representability** independently of receiver satisfaction and resource availability. F1/F2 cannot be hidden under an “export readiness” qualifier, since those states are already part of supported machine execution.\n\nThe `:push` wording is also too strong: it says “no canonical state exists here,” while the assessment correctly notes that stack `:push` only advances PC (`stack.cljc:722`). It is a stuttering boundary with the same values as its successor, not an intrinsically unserializable interior. Either canonicalize it to the successor without running guest semantics, or label refusal as an explicit backend limitation. Retaining that limitation is a scope choice under the owner's fixed backend fragment, not proof of arbitrary hardware-PC coverage.\n\nI independently searched `docs/`, `src/`, and `test/` for safepoint restrictions and `not-at-safepoint`. The main live refusal sites in rows, layout and harness are covered by section 6. Additional omissions/contradictions to reconcile:\n\n- `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:1250` still prescribes a stepped-kernel refusal; no disposition in the inventory. Replace it when 6e lands or label its historical scope explicitly.\n- `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2421` still supplies replacement conformance text testing only parking windows. Retain it as a historical amendment or add the running-state liveness check; do not leave two normative replacements.\n- `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:799` (step 7) lists only the old frame kinds and contexts; section 3.4 does not amend that validator list to include `:run`/`:running`.\n- `UCF:802` still specifies only saved-window/parking checks; the proposed section 7.4.2 changes totality but does not add the `L(pc)` running-window check there.\n- `UCF:1912` still says “no engine change,” and `UCF:2842`'s no-layer-collapsing row still says the hot loop is untouched and lifting is over a blocked machine. The proposed kernel-entry gate changes that engine contract even though it leaves the instruction loop intact. Update both.\n- The continuation document's root custody prose at `:368` and `:405`, its context input at `:433`, and its static/runtime inventory in sections 4–5 must agree with the new forms. Adding a later paragraph alone leaves old exhaustive lists misleading.\n\nGuest-event safepoints in `yang.antlr.md` and related code are a separate delivery policy and need not be widened. Historical logs/reports should receive explicit supersession references, not rewritten history. Also reconcile R3/S1 versus plan:316: a `run` with forbidden pending is specified as `:frame-shape` by S1 but expected as `:pending-presence` by acceptance. Pick one ordered diagnostic and test it consistently.\n\n## Answers to the eight review questions\n\n1. **Canonical activation versus complete task.** `put-registers` really materializes segment/PC/window/env/K at fuel exhaustion (`semantic.cljc:50`, `:142`). Those five components are an activation, not a self-contained CESK task. The store is already explicit in the frozen transition relation (`yin.vm.semantic.md:197`); `gensym` depends on `id-counter` (`engine.cljc:2368`); parked continuations, stores, module stores and resource cells are needed too. The existing task wrapper carries the counter, whole root store, parked table and install tree (`rows.cljc:1670`–`:1694`), and module stores reached through environments/closures. A run-state walk must register the current environment and code context before collecting those stores and code. The proposed walk does so. Queues and remaining waits are the missing scheduler components (F1/F2).\n\n   `analysis.cljc:45` computes backward liveness for every body PC, not just parks; the union/difference order at `:64` is correct. This is exact for its declared syntactic CFG fixed point, not path/value-minimal liveness. Branches union both successors, and layout syntax has live sets even when unreachable. No under-approximation was established by reading. Every-PC observational replay plus an independent use/def/CFG oracle is required; checking a validator that uses the same analysis is not independent evidence. For R, keep the all-PC injectivity check; for H, fix F5 first.\n\n   Open cursor cells are explicit resource data, not an implicit stream read position (`rows.cljc:1381`). Seals/owners are reissued by lower, with aliasing through the census. The module registry, primitive implementation/profiles, stream constructors and externally served streams remain composition prerequisites; the five-tuple does not contain them. Telemetry and GC policy are host instrumentation/execution services, not carried semantic registers. `run-fuel` can avoid the public step's telemetry wrapper, but parks/resumes themselves still emit telemetry (`engine.cljc:2352`, `:2363`); “telemetry-free” must mean no per-fuel snapshot, not no possible telemetry emission. Trace replay is deterministic only under equivalent primitives, dependency bindings and external inputs. Ungated effects execute inside steps (`engine.cljc:498`); a host exception is not transactional rollback of an already performed append. The plan should not infer external atomicity merely from losing loop locals.\n\n2. **Custody.** Using the full occurrence/arbitration/next-op-seq/origin arm for a running exclusive root is correct. `header-custody` already chooses it for any non-halted kind (`rows.cljc:1713`), and `with-custody` already chooses gate `:running` for it (`:2058`). Ledger and grantor do need their explicit offerable sets extended (`ledger.cljc:277`, `authority/grant.cljc:75`). Preserve `accept-grant!`, fenced restoration and origin completion; fix F6. The proposed kernel-entry guard correctly closes the new direct pure-step path while exporting/ended. `abort` is not an unconditional return to running: live tenure and proof that no offer may have been accepted are mandatory (`export.cljc:584`, `:590`). Carry that condition into the plan's simplified source paragraph. Add two competing candidates, stale-source stepping, uncertain offer/abort, expired-grant admission, and recovery byte equality for running roots. An exclusive grant authorizes one holder's effects; serializable immutable bytes alone cannot prevent a trusted caller from cloning an object or calling lower twice. Do not claim physical single-instantiation enforcement. F3/F4 remain blockers for lease/preemption completeness.\n\n3. **Quiescence.** Keeping refusal for queues is not compatible with unconditional any-PC migration over the admitted task domain. It is safe refusal, but leaves the named invariant unimplemented. The counterexample need not drain before expiry, and “organically single waiter” cannot exclude carriers already accepted by the system. F1/F2 specify the needed correction.\n\n4. **Foundational invariants.** An explicit fuel argument and holder config add no hidden global, raw callback, shared mutable state, or clock in the kernel. A resumable budget outcome is explicit host scheduling, and migration preserves guest control flow. A new typed row does not require VM knowledge of a dao.space index: it remains stream data interpreted independently. No new graph is assumed if run, K, resource and scheduler references are tabled and validated by the existing graph stage. `:run` is a distinct semantic state, so a row kind is justified; a new root slot need not be invented merely to tag it. The new task product must preserve that separation. Existing local accumulator atoms are construction machinery, not new shared guest state. The plan broadly respects malleability; the phrase “one behavioural change” hides the scheduler and expiry dependencies.\n\n5. **6e.** Register pairing and live-window reconstruction are plausible and grounded in the allocator. Stack mid-expression reconstruction is also plausible, but the claimed existing equation and obstruction list are incomplete (F5/F7). Laws 4/5 are realistic on **aligned semantic boundaries**, after skipping/normalizing H stutters and honoring the documented backend domain. Pin both taken and untaken branches and ordered operand multiplicity; set equality alone loses duplicate-operand information. Keep tail-PC windows distinct from tail *delivery* states. Every-A-instruction step count also needs checking around primitive tail calls and dead suffix syntax, rather than assuming subtraction of `:push` is the only alignment issue. The retained image/free-env/FFI/link limits remain intentionally deferred; no mandate to widen them is inferred here.\n\n6. **Amendments.** The main direction is correct: parking PCs become delivery cases inside a larger state relation, and receiver layout metadata remains derived/static. Replacement text is not ready verbatim because of F1/F2/F9, the malformed-state assertion, and conflicting diagnostics. Section 6 is a good inventory but not complete; concrete additional occurrences are above. Keep the frozen design, continuation row table, tuple validator stages and UCF wording synchronized in the commit that introduces each behavior.\n\n7. **Sequencing/cost.** 6d-a must precede consumers of the new grammar; 6d-b and 6e depend on that grammar but not intrinsically on each other. The asserted total order is therefore not forced (the plan itself says this at :351). Ship kernel fencing with the first exposed running exclusive path, and do not call the owner's invariant closed by fork-only 6d-a. Resolve task/scheduler representation and fuelled install execution before fixing the grammar in a brief. Move the small conditional/jump layout proof ahead of the broad cross-engine matrix; it already falsifies an acceptance gate in 6d-a.\n\n   Module pins and any-PC state are logically independent, but edits to one function and root `requires` are not guaranteed a trivial semantic rebase. Re-run module-store/free-name discharge and receiver admission at mid-body contexts after integration. Existing module pin Q5 (`continuation.md:2776`) is intentionally in-flight and is not a new 6d defect; same-composition harness success does not verify arbitrary receiver equivalence until that slice lands. There is no architecture reason to block initial reference work on a stalled host-profile decision. Land whichever reviewed slice is ready, coordinate rows/fixtures, and revalidate the integrated contract.\n\n   The 11-day estimate is an estimate for a narrower design. It omits scheduler outcomes/remaining waits, running-child carrier and scheduling, expiry retention/adoption decisions, and control-flow stack layouts. Re-estimate those explicitly after the decisions. No measured runtime supports the slow-tier wall-time forecast here. Cut repetitive broad end-to-end cases during iteration; retain targeted counterexamples, all-PC static gates, quick structural samples and a full three-host run at landing. Do not cut custody races or scheduler products to preserve the estimate.\n\n8. **What was verified right; defect/gap/defer distinction.** The core `:run` versus `:act` distinction is correct: current `L(pc)` includes available values, while an `act` excludes the not-yet-delivered destination (`semantic.cljc:64`, `analysis.cljc:92`). The body-start exception to act-only PC/body-continuity rules is necessary (`frame.cljc:122`, `:124`). Retaining nil-valued keys, whole root store, every parked record, source AST associations, fresh receiver seals and existing admission stages is correct. Reusing slot 13 can preserve existing encoded row identities, provided all typed traversals are updated. Grant-gated running custody, fencing before publication, a composition-owned fuel knob, and reference/cross-engine slice separation are all sound foundations checked against the cited code.\n\n   F1/F2/F3 are architectural completeness defects, F5 is a false reconstruction specification, F4 is an unresolved lifecycle design with a concrete implementation loss path, and F6–F9 are implementable contract/acceptance corrections. Existing registry pinning, general correlation/census gaps and the retained backend fragment are deferred work already documented; this review does not silently expand their scope. It does require the plan to stop treating them, or same-composition fixtures, as universal portability evidence.\n\n## Required revision before acceptance\n\n1. **Make the task state complete:** carry active control together with ordered waits and completed scheduler outcomes; remove R0 and the queue exception as purported proofs of arbitrary-PC coverage. Include running install children and explicit tree budgets.\n2. **Complete the custody/preemption lifecycle:** retain the boundary on post-slice tenure failure, specify lawful expiry/recovery behavior, keep halted grant exemption, and verify stale-source/two-candidate/recovery cuts for running roots.\n3. **Repair and prove all-PC reconstruction:** add conditional-jump/join and tail-residual layouts, normalize or explicitly scope stack stutters, fix the full-payload refusal oracle and no-execution law-2 ordering, then synchronize the documents.\n\nThe owner has settled whether to provide preemptive migration. The revision must settle how all already admitted machine states remain expressible and recoverable when preempted. Until these changes are incorporated, the plan's `PLAN-READY` conclusion is unsupported.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
