{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '269,426p' review-inputs/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"### 3.7 Amended document text (exact)\n\nMarker on every clause: \"**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17)**\". The owner's sentence is quoted once (continuation §0.1 ruling 9) and pointed to elsewhere. Four words are defined once and used consistently: **representable** (the carrier has a canonical form for the machine state), **portable** (every value in it has a wire form), **satisfied** (the receiver has the code, primitives, modules and streams it names), **export-ready** (the holder may publish it: custody, tenure, no hold).\n\n#### 3.7.1 `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md`\n\n**§0.1, new ruling 9:**\n\n> 9. **Preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm`** (owner, 2026-10-17: \"preemptive migration, the ability to pause and continue at any PC, is a primary invariant of yin.vm. It is why yin.vm is defined as a continuation VM based on the CESK model. The UCF being only defined at a wait or parked state is a violation of this invariant\"). The carrier expresses the evaluator's complete task state between **any** two instructions, including the scheduler's products (remaining waits, completed wakes), not only its two delivery states; a safepoint is a boundary at which the program stops by itself. Slices 6d-a (carrier, reference lane), 6d-b (holder, custody, expiry) and 6e (backends) land it.\n\n**§0.2, first sentence**, replace \"A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register machine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return` frames, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait (frozen §2.4, §4.2)\" with:\n\n> A continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register machine, which is a `run` state `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` at any instruction boundary (§1.3) or one of its two delivery states, `act` or `tail`, with the saved window, the delivery record and the pending wait the evaluator mints when it parks (frozen §2.4, §4.2); and a task is the product of its activation, its ordered waits and its ordered completed wakes (§2.6)\n\nand after \"`:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and `:parked-table` rows\" insert \", the `:run` and `:ready` rows (6d)\".\n\n**§1.1, fourth shape:**\n\n> ```clojure\n> run  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K}      ; between instructions; no deliver, no pending\n> ```\n> - `run` is the state `vm-hot` materialises when fuel runs out (`put-registers`, `semantic.cljc:142-143`, `:50-55`): `pc` is the instruction **about to execute**, `W` is trimmed by the carrier to `L(pc)` (`analysis/live`), `K` as for `act`. A `run` at `n` differs by kind from an `act` at `n`: the `act` has executed `n−1`, carries `saved(n, rd)` and awaits `rd`; the `run` has not executed `n` and carries `L(n)`, which may contain that `rd`.\n\n**§1.2**, keyed form after the tail state:\n\n> ```clojure\n> ;; run state (6d)\n> {:yin.k/state :run\n>  :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n                ; the instruction about to execute, 0 ≤ n < (count v)\n>  :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n>  :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly L(n); sorted by r on the wire\n>  :yin.k/k [ … ]}                            ; no deliver, no pending\n> ```\n\n**New §1.3 \"States between instructions, and the task product\":**\n\n> **1.3 States between instructions, and the task product.** The two delivery states of §1.1 are the states at which the evaluator parks by itself. They are special cases of its general state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, which `vm-hot` materializes whenever fuel runs out (`semantic.cljc:142-143`) and which the carrier expresses as the `run` state of §1.2. A step is atomic: effects are observed only inside a step or inside the scheduler's sweep, so no state between steps holds a half-issued effect; atomicity of a step is not transactionality of the outside world (an append performed before a host exception is not undone). A task is a **product**: at most one active activation (the `run`), its ordered pending waits, its ordered completed wakes (the ready queue: a woken wait with the value, status and cursor update its effect produced, carried by the `:ready` row of §2.3), its parked table, and its stores. The scheduler constructs every combination: resuming one of several woken waits leaves the others queued and the unresolved ones pending (`engine.cljc:2294-2312`, `:2305`). Every such state is **representable**. The lift refuses only what is not **portable** (a value with no wire form), not **satisfied** (code or dependencies the receiver lacks), or a **hold** (a driver-internal observation in flight: §7.1 step 1), never a pc. The static correspondence of §4 holds at every pc of a body: distinct virtuals in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` hold distinct physical registers (`derive/allocation`), and the stack kernel's operand region at A pc `p` is `stack-layout(p) ++ uses(p)` above a dead prefix (§4.3); both are checks (§5.6), not theorems. A root whose task has an active activation has kind `:running` (§2.6) and is a custody subject like a blocked or parked root (§7.3).\n\n**§2.3 row table**, two rows, one note:\n\n> | `:run` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref` | the between-instructions state; no delivery, no pending; five slots (an `:act` has six) |\n> | `:ready` | `wait-cref  value-V  status-D  cursor-cells-cref` | a completed wake: the wait it completes, the value its effect produced, the status the sweep recorded (a terminal status raises on resume exactly as on the immediate path), and the cursor cells it advanced (a `:next`'s successor cursor; empty for a `:put`) |\n\nand `:wait`: \"`state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row, never a `:run`\".\n\n**§2.6 root**: slots `17 run (crefs to :run rows; exactly one for :running, none otherwise)` and `18 ready (crefs to :ready rows, in queue order; none for :halted)`; `kind` comment `; :running | :blocked | :parked | :halted`; kind rules paragraph = the table of plan v2 §3.1 (also tuples §5.5). Add: \"A machine halted with a non-empty queue is kind `:blocked`: the scheduler resumes the queue before it reads the halt, so the halt value is dead.\"\n\n**§3.2**: S1, C2, C3, R1, R2, P1 as plan v2 §3.1's validator table (R3 is folded into S1: a `run` with a `deliver` or `pending` key is `:frame-shape`). \"`window-defect` is parameterised by the wanted set: `saved(pc, rd)` for an act and a return frame, `live(pc)` for a run; there is no `:window-self` analogue.\"\n\n**§3.3**: step 7 judges \"every wait's state in context `:wait`, the root's `:run` in context `:running`, every `:ready`'s wait in context `:wait`, every parked state in `:parked`, every reified value in `:captured`\".\n\n**§4.3** (stack layouts), replace \"**Checked** (§5.6 item 5) … dead layout syntax).\" with:\n\n> **Defined at every pc of every body.** `stack-layout(p)` is the ancestors' pending pushes for a parse node's pc; for a conditional's `:jump` pc `j` it is `stack-layout(b) ++ [rd(if)]` (`b` the branch pc, `rd(if)` the register both arms define: the consequent's value is on the stack while control jumps); for a body's terminator (`:return r`, `:halt r`) it is `[]`. **Checked** (§5.6 item 5), ordered: the current region is `stack-layout(p) ++ [W[u] for u in uses(p)]` in emitter order (operator then operands; `:stream-put` target then value), and as sets `stack-layout(p) ∪ uses(p) = L(p)` at every reachable non-tail pc, `uses(p) = L(p)` at every reachable tail-call pc. **Dead prefix.** A tail `:call` consumes its operator and operands and keeps the rest of its region without pushing a frame (`stack.cljc:731`, `:747`), so a region entered through a tail call may hold, below the layout, values the ambient layout syntax pushed and nothing reads again (every read is top-relative; a return truncates to a base that includes them). The lift takes the top `|stack-layout(p)| + |uses(p)|` values of the current region and the top `|stack-layout(pc_i − 1)|` of each frame's slice and drops the prefix; the lower produces none and recomputes bases cumulatively; the prefix is unobservable by law 1 and absent from the canonical form by laws 2 and 4. A `:push` pc of H is a stutter: its state equals its successor's but for pc, and the lift relabels it to `h->a(hpc+1)` without running anything.\n\n**§4.5**, last clause → \"never invents a state: a kernel pc outside every body is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2, as amended; the name is historical); every other kernel pc lifts, as a delivery state when the kernel is parked and as a `run` state when it is between instructions (6e); a `:push` pc of H is relabelled to its successor. Until 6e lands, a de Bruijn kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` as a documented limit of the backends, not of the carrier.\"\n\n**§5.4 law 4**: \"one program at one **aligned instruction boundary** (its parking boundaries included; the stack lane's `:push` stutters relabelled) lifts to row sets whose root ids are equal …\". Laws 1, 2, 6: \"at every boundary of the stepping driver (§5.7)\".\n\n**§5.6**, items 3, 5, 10:\n\n> 3. … at every reachable pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is single-valued on the physical image of `L(p)`; the `live-out*(p)` clause stays for parked entries.\n> 5. … the ordered region check and the set equations of §4.3 at every reachable pc; the parked-boundary form `(set (stack-layout p)) = saved(p+1, rd)` stays; duplicate operands are two positions holding one value and the lift asserts they agree.\n> 10. Stepping: for every case the reference lane's boundary count to the first park and to the halt is pinned per host; the three lanes agree on it after the stack lane's `:push` relabelling (`correspondence`).\n\n**New §5.7 \"The stepping driver\"** = plan v2 §3.6 (order, isolated worlds, coverage classes, \"every pc\" definition) in design voice.\n\n**§7.1 steps 1-2**, replace:\n\n> 1. **Holds.** Refuse `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch` on an observation in flight (an `:observe` wait, a held entry, an unminted cursor, a pending close, a link request without its cursor): these are the holder's own seams mid-step, resolved by the driver's next cycle without executing guest code; they are not task states at rest. There is no quiescence refusal: a non-empty ready queue is carried (§2.3 `:ready`).\n> 2. **Kind.** `:running` when `:control` is set; else `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked table holds; else `:blocked` when the wait set or the ready queue is non-empty; else `:halted` with empty wait set and queue; anything else is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` (unreachable by construction).\n\nand in step 4: \"a `:running` task's state node is the control's `run` state with the window trimmed to `L(pc)` (`select-keys`), contributing the context `(A, pc, E)` to discharge (`live-pcs` from `pc`); each ready entry contributes its wait's state as a wait does, its value through the value walk and its cursor update through the census\". Delete \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set\" from the `canonical-task` paragraph: \"a kernel with an active continuation is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` until 6e, then a `run` state through `lift-running`\".\n\n**§7.2**, restore: \"`:running`: `:control {:segment local :pc pc} :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K) :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil :wait-set entries`; every kind: `:ready-queue` from the ready rows in order, each entry its wait plus `:value`, `:status` and `:resource-updates` re-keyed to the receiver's cells; nothing runs; `vm/run` or `run-fuel` continues from `pc`, or sweeps and resumes the first ready entry.\"\n\n**New §7.3 \"Custody and lifecycle of a running root\"** = plan v2 §3.3 in design voice, including the four outcomes, the sentence \"progress since the last durable checkpoint is lost on expiry; fuel makes the computation interruptible, exporting before the bound is what preserves it\", the gate guard, and \"a completed `:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed but not a retained write\".\n\n**New §7.4 \"Fuel\"** = plan v2 §3.4 including the honest guarantee verbatim.\n\n**§8.3**, item 12: \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel pc outside every body; a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts as a `run` state (6e; until then the backend refusal is pinned as a limit); a `:push` pc relabels\". Add §8.6 (6d-a), §8.7 (6d-b), §8.8 (6e) = plan v2 §3.9.\n\n**§9**: add \"**Claims that are checks**: `L(p)` is sufficient at every boundary (law 1 at every step); injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc (item 3); the ordered stack equation at every pc (item 5); the dead-prefix rule (law 1 after a tail transition); the refusal oracle equality (6e). None is a theorem about all programs; each is a gate on the corpus.\"\n\n**§11 Q6**: live, answered in 6d-a: keep \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"; a lift at the pc **before** a constant-dead branch refuses `ghost` as `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, pinned as a documented false refusal (derive, don't persist; the linker's conservative discharge).\n\n**Sites the review listed**: `:368-371` and `:403-408`: \"An exclusive **running**, blocked or parked root carries policy, occurrence, arbitration and next-op-seq, with origin on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries origin only.\" `:433-435`: contexts \"`:wait`, `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running`\". `:1249-1252`: \"`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a register kernel stepped one instruction past a `:const`, asked to lift) — **historical (phase 6); from 6e a stepped kernel lifts as a `run` state and only a pc outside every body refuses**\". `:2419-2425`: mark the paragraph \"historical amendment text (phase 6)\" and point to §5.6/§5.7 as the one normative conformance statement, adding the running-state check: \"and, at every boundary of the stepping driver, the run window's key set with `L(pc)`\". §4 and §5 inventories (static/runtime refusal lists): add `:ready-reason`, `:inconsistent-state`, the `:running` kind and context, the `:run`/`:ready` rows, and delete `:not-quiescent`.\n\n#### 3.7.2 `yin.vm.semantic.md` (frozen design)\n\n**§4.1 first paragraph**, replace \"A safepoint is a transition at which the machine parks. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** The machine's task state between any two instructions, `⟨seg, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` beside its ordered waits and completed wakes, is canonical and lifts (continuation design §1.3): preemptive migration at any pc is a primary invariant of `yin.vm` (owner, 2026-10-17). A **safepoint** is a boundary at which the program stops by itself: a park with a pending wait, an explicit park with none, or a halt with a result. The table below enumerates those parking transitions; it is not the set of liftable pcs, which is every pc of every body. The semantic kinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n\n**§4.1 `:680-689`**: keep the explicit-park and reified-continuation sentences; replace \"Whole-task quiescence (empty ready queue, UCF §7.4.1) and the existing refusal conditions (unsupported observation states, pending close) are unchanged.\" with \"A non-empty ready queue is carried as ordered completed wakes (continuation design §2.3); the holds (observation in flight, pending close) remain refusals of the lift as driver seams mid-step.\"\n\n**§4.2**: add the run state (§3.7.1 keyed form); \"(for an `act`, `pc` is a safepoint successor …; for a `run`, `pc` is any pc of a body and the window is exactly `L(pc)`)\"; row carrier sentence: add \"`:run [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref]`, `:ready [wait-cref value status cursor-cells-cref]`\".\n\n**§4.3**, after the act and tail clauses:\n\n> and for a `run` state at pc `p`, produce the segment `A`, `p`, every register of `L(p)`, `E`, `K` with each frame's validated window and `rd`, and no delivery record or pending; a physical location outside `L(p)`'s image is never read, and an engine that cannot hold a value of `L(p)`, or of any carried frame's window or binding, refuses before attachment (the register kernel's `:continuation-registers`). `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` holds for admitted canonical frames of all three states; a stack image's `:push` pc relabels to its successor.\n\n**§10 phase 6**: append \"*(Amended 2026-10-16: the phase-6 harness proved the delivery states only; slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e extend the gate to every instruction boundary and to the scheduler's products under the owner's any-pc invariant.)*\"\n\n#### 3.7.3 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\n\n**Blocker 1 (`:17`)**: append \"**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc):** the parking transitions are where the program stops by itself; the set of liftable configurations is every instruction boundary of the register-shaped machine together with its scheduler state (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1), and the safepoint table enumerates the delivery states within it.\"\n\n**§7.2.1 (`:264-266`)**: prefix \"(versions 0-2; version 3 admits `run` states at every pc and completed wakes, continuation design §1.3)\".\n\n**§7.4.1 title and rule.** Title → \"7.4.1 Every instruction boundary is representable; the safepoint table is the machine's parking transitions\". Replace \"UCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be lifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the reference machine at which the machine parks.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17).** The register-shaped machine has no unserializable interior: its task state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` with its ordered waits and completed wakes, is canonical (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1; continuation design §1.3). **A continuation is representable at every instruction boundary**; the lift refuses a value with no wire form, a dependency the receiver cannot satisfy, or an observation in flight, never a pc. A *safepoint* is a boundary at which the program stops by itself: a park with a pending wait, an explicit park with none, or a halt with a result. The paragraph above describes the stack-shaped machine, which left at phase 8.\n\nKeyed-form block: add the `run` state. **Quiescence** paragraph (`:762-765`): replace with \"There is no quiescence requirement: a completed wake in the ready queue travels as a `:ready` row with its value, status and cursor update, and the receiver resumes it before any newly woken wait, in carried order.\"\n\n**§7.4.2** (`:800-825`): the conformance sentence (`:802`) → \"by a conformance test that runs the segment to **every boundary of the stepping driver** and compares the saved window's key set with `live − {rd}` at parks, the run window's key set with `live` between instructions, and the E-reads\"; rules → \"`lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on **every pc of A** the engine reaches; a pc that is no pc of A relabels (a `:push` of the stack image) or refuses (outside every body)\". Replace the `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` bullet with:\n\n> - A request to lift at a pc outside every body returns `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc; the name is historical, the meaning is \"no state of A exists here\". A stack image's `:push` pc is relabelled to its successor's A pc without running anything. An engine may not invent states a record does not have, and two engines that disagree about the state set of one address are not both conforming. An engine that cannot hold a value of the canonical frame refuses before attachment with its own kind (the register backend's `:continuation-registers`); that is a limit of the engine, not of the address.\n\nLast paragraph (`:823-825`): \"a debugger's breakpoint set is any subset of the pcs of A; the safepoint set is where the program will stop by itself\".\n\n**§7.7.4** step 3 (`:1901-1903`) → \"Neutralize control: a parked or blocked machine has no control; a machine between instructions (root kind `:running`) keeps its `:control`, `:window`, `:env` and `:k` as the state the lift reads, and the kernel refuses to step a machine whose gate is `:exporting` or `:ended` (`yin.vm.semantic/vm-hot`, one check at entry, outside the instruction loop), so the exporting machine is pure data in both cases; direct `:resume` …\". `:1912-1913` \"pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained export record — no engine change, no callback, no timer\" → \"pure data with its wait set, ready queue and parked records moved into the retained export record — one engine change at the kernel's entry (the gate guard), no change to the instruction loop, no callback, no timer\". `:1941-1942` \"A task is not at a liftable safepoint while it holds any of these\" → \"A task is not export-ready while it holds any of these; they are the driver's own seams and resolve in its next cycle without guest execution.\" In the \"(M-next D8/D9, as built.)\" paragraph, after \"under `:running` internal computation proceeds\" add \"for the holder's fuel budget per program tick (`driver/run-cycle`, `:fuel`), so a long computation is preempted at an instruction boundary and may be exported there; a holder whose tenure ends keeps the newest completed pure state for inspection and offers nothing; the occurrence is regrantable from its last durable checkpoint and progress since it is lost (continuation design §7.3)\".\n\n**§7.7.6** (`:2161`) \"runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\" → \"runs to its next park, halt, or fuel boundary\".\n\n**§7.8 Lift** step 1 (`:2621-2623`) → \"The machine is parked, blocked, halted or between instructions (`:running`), with a local record `r`, and holds no observation in flight, else `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`.\" Step 3 (`:2627-2628`) → \"`lift(export-record)`: confirm every pc is a pc of A (§7.4.1), else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; …\". Step 9 (`:2673`) → \"At the next park, halt, or fuel boundary, produce the successor occurrence …\".\n\n**§7.9** (`:2690`): `| :yin.k/not-at-safepoint | lift | :yin.k/segment, :yin.k/pc: the pc is outside every body of A |`; delete the `:yin.k/not-quiescent` row.\n\n**§7.10** (`:2839`): \"Resume happens only at an instruction boundary the canonical vector defines (a parking transition, or the boundary a holder's fuel budget stopped at); the successor chain …\". **`:2842` No layer collapsing** → \"The engine's instruction loop is untouched: lifting is a pure function over the machine's value at any boundary, the one engine change is a gate check at the kernel's entry, and the exporting state is that value with its waits, queue and parked records moved into a retained record — driver discipline, not engine machinery.\"\n\n**§7.11** (`:2931-2942`): rename \"Continuation conformance harness\"; \"to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table — effect-producing calls included —\" → \"to **every boundary of the stepping driver** (continuation design §5.7), the complete §7.4.1 table's parking transitions included,\"; the cross-engine sentence \"one program at one aligned instruction boundary\". **§7.11.1** (`:3100-3130`): Invariant → \"At every instruction boundary, lifting and lowering preserve the next observable step (and, at a parking transition, its wait; with a non-empty queue, its order); a pc outside every body refuses.\" Setup: \"a nonsafepoint pc\" → \"a `:push` pc of the stack image (relabels) and a body-start pc (lifts)\"; expected: \"An undeclared pc gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`\" → \"A pc outside every body gives `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; every pc of A lifts as a `run` state when the machine is between instructions; queued work travels as `:ready` rows and resumes in order\".\n\n#### 3.7.4 `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`\n\n**§1 stance item 1** (`:45-49`): append \"Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc): a *safepoint* is a parking `(segment-address, pc)`; the carrier expresses every `(segment-address, pc)` of A as a `:run` state when the machine is between instructions, and the scheduler's completed wakes as `:ready` rows.\" **§5.2**: `:run` and `:ready` rows; `:wait`'s note. **§5.5** (`:455-460`): the kind-rules paragraph = plan v2 §3.1 table; keep \"Frames restore in carried order and are never collapsed to one; a task may hold several ordered waits **and several ordered completed wakes**\". Custody table (`:466-473`): rows `root | running / blocked / parked | exclusive | the four keys plus origin on a successor` and `root | running / blocked / parked | fork | nil`; add \"a completed `:put` wake is a completed operation: its op-id is well-formed, not retained\". **§8 step 7** (`:797-801`): contexts \"(`:wait`, `:captured`, `:parked`, `:running`)\" and \"the root's `:run` and each `:ready`'s wait\". **§9 Lift** (`:880`): \"the merged order is holds, kind, free environment …\" (quiescence deleted); the canonical round-trip law (`:913-917`) unchanged (\"no execution between lower and lift\" is the law the harness now follows literally); **Cross-engine** (`:929`): \"Running the corpus to each aligned instruction boundary\". **§11** (`:1027`): \"Code identity (A), the parking transitions,\".\n\n#### 3.7.5 `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md` §7 (`:560-579`)\n\nReplace \"Only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks at the existing engine export boundary are eligible. Running CESK/PC snapshots are not a fourth kind.\" with:\n\n> **Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc).** For the stack-shaped machine of versions 0-2, only blocked, explicitly parked or halted tasks were eligible and running snapshots were not a fourth kind. For the register-shaped machine and body version 3 this is reversed by the owner's invariant: a task between instructions is the fourth kind, `:running`, carried with its ordered waits and completed wakes (continuation design §1.3, §2.6), and is eligible for export and custody.\n\n#### 3.7.6 `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` §14.1\n\nTable 14.1.1 (`:3777-3788`): add `| Between instructions (:running) | Active control; ordered waits and completed wakes travel; the window is exactly L(pc); continue at pc. |`. `:3798` \"Static `:yin.safepoint/kinds` checks pc eligibility only\" → \"…checks a parking pc's kinds; a `run` pc is any pc of a body\". `:3827` → \"Validate the instruction boundary (§7.4.1 as amended); enter exporting before …\". `:3882` \"continue to the next safepoint\" → \"continue to the next park, halt or fuel boundary\". `:3900-3901` → \"A pc outside every body yields `not-at-safepoint`; queued work travels as `:ready` rows.\" `:4145` as UCF §7.8 step 9.\n\n#### 3.7.7 `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`\n\nNew dated entry I-9: \"2026-10-16, any-pc. The lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`) and a non-empty ready queue (`:1652-1653`), the holder runs to a park (`driver.cljc:2120`), and every custody kind table lists three kinds, in violation of the owner's primary invariant (2026-10-17). Slices 6d-a, 6d-b and 6e add the `:run` and `:ready` rows, root kind `:running`, validator rules R1-R2 and the extended S1/C2/C3/P1, custody for running roots, fuel, expiry semantics, control-flow stack layouts and the harness at every boundary. Section 8's 'check the pc against the static safepoint set' (`:602`) and 'the safepoint harness must run each 7.4.1 row' (`:622`) describe the stack-shaped machine and are historical.\" No compatibility promise.\n\n#### 3.7.8 The assessment's §F and the gap list\n\nAssessment §F disagreement 3 → \"Safepoints are the parking transitions, a proper subset of the representable boundaries, by the owner's invariant; its 'one necessity kept' (the ready-queue refusal) was wrong: the queue is carried.\" Its verdict line is superseded by this report. Gap list G7 (\"capability gap only\") superseded; the orchestrator log's next seat entry records the reclassification as a P1 defect against a primary invariant and cites this report. `docs/agents/known-gaps.md` gains the row:\n\n> | the lift refuses a machine between instructions (`rows.cljc:1656-1657`) and a queued completed wake (`:1652-1653`), the holder runs to a park (`driver.cljc:2120`) and loses a computed boundary on tenure loss (`:2252-2253`), the custody kind tables list three kinds, and the stack layout is undefined at jump pcs (`layout.cljc:118-131`): the carrier cannot express, and the holder cannot pause at, an arbitrary pc | owner invariant 2026-10-17; Architect plan v2 `collab/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md` | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | 6d-a, 6d-b, 6e | P1 |\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
