oint\") | generalise, §3.7.1 |\n| `:1590`, `:1613`, `:1626-1630`, `:1651-1658` | §7.1 \"refuse unless quiescent\"; `:not-at-safepoint` of `canonical-task`; steps 1-2 | replace, §3.7.1 |\n| `:2148-2157` | §8.3 item 12 | replace, §3.7.1 |\n| `:2263-2268` | §9 checks | add, §3.7.1 |\n| `:2409-2425` | second conformance text | historical label, §3.7.1 |\n| `:2443` | cross-engine equality \"at one safepoint\" | §3.7.1 |\n| `:2492`, `:2566-2567` | amendment records | keep (records of earlier edits) |\n| `:2738-2740` | install child \"at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked`\" | \"a carried child is `:blocked` (wire phase `:parked`) or `:running` (wire phase `:running`)\" |\n| `:2792-2797` | §11 Q6 | answer, §3.7.1 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:45-49` | stance item 1 | §3.7.4 |\n| `:402-404` | install child \"retains at a safepoint\" | \"retains at a park, halt or fuel boundary\" |\n| `:455-460`, `:466-473` | kind rules; custody table | §3.7.4 |\n| `:797-801` | step 7 contexts | §3.7.4 |\n| `:880` | \"quiescence and safepoint, kind\" | §3.7.4 |\n| `:929` | cross-engine \"to each safepoint\" | §3.7.4 |\n| `:970` | \"safepoint reconstruction obligations\" | \"reconstruction obligations at every boundary\" |\n| `:1027` | §11 | §3.7.4 |\n| `universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md:560-579` | §7 eligibility | §3.7.5 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:4`, `:227-228`, `:312-314`, `:467`, `:542-544`, `:573-597`, `:655-659` | history of the static safepoint API | historical; entry I-9 points to them, §3.7.7 |\n| `:602-603`, `:622` | \"must check the pc against the static safepoint set\"; \"harness must run each 7.4.1 row\" | historical; I-9 |\n| `yin.vm.linker.dht.md:3742`, `:3759`, `:3777-3798`, `:3827`, `:3882`, `:3900-3901`, `:4145` | 14.1 | §3.7.6 |\n| `yin.vm.linker.md:47`, `:2460`; `code-as-tuples.implementation-plan.md:74`, `:780`; `semantic.evaluator.md:1544` | name the concept | keep |\n| `known-gaps.md:27`, `:36` | records | keep; add the new row |\n\n**Code: docstrings, comments, refusals.**\n\n| File:line | Text | Slice and disposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| `rows.cljc:1323`, `:1741` | \"walks a quiescent semantic machine\"; \"quiescent semantic machine m\" | 6d-a: \"a machine at any boundary: running, blocked, parked or halted\" |\n| `rows.cljc:1652-1653` | `:yin.k/not-quiescent` | 6d-a: deleted; ready rows |\n| `rows.cljc:1656-1657`, `:1663` | the two `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` refusals | 6d-a: the first becomes the `:running` kind; the second becomes `:inconsistent-state` |\n| `layout.cljc:33` | \"never invents a safepoint (§4.5)\" | 6e: \"never invents a state (§4.5)\" |\n| `layout.cljc:313-315`, `:318-330`, `:333-336` | `parking-ops` \"A1 …\"; `safepoint!`; `h->a` \"a :push has none and is no safepoint\" | 6e: `safepoint!` gains the run mode; `h->a` relabels a `:push` to its successor; docstrings say \"delivery state\" where they say \"safepoint\" |\n| `layout.cljc:469`, `:480`, `:528`, `:532` | `safepoint!` call sites | 6e: run mode |\n| `layout.cljc:715-725` | `canonical-task` \"A running kernel is :yin.k/not-at-safepoint\" | 6e: \"a running kernel lifts as :running\" |\n| `layout.cljc:118-131`, `:501-507` | `stack-layouts`; `slice-window` count equality | 6d-a: jump/terminator layouts; 6e: dead-prefix `≥` |\n| `driver.cljc:74-77`, `:318-321`, `:1119-1123`, `:2147-2154` | phase docstring; \"liftable safepoint (parked, blocked or halted)\"; `hand-off`; `at-safepoint?` | 6d-b: docstrings |\n| `export.cljc:274-282`, header | `kind-of` \"else :halted\"; `enter` queue refusal (`:107-114`, `:138`) | 6d-b: `:running` first; `first-refusal` deleted |\n| `compose.cljc:798-799`, `:842` | \"liftable safepoint, holding no custody\"; \"arms over the safepoint\" | 6d-b: \"at a park, halt or instruction boundary\" |\n| `ledger.cljc:277`; `authority/grant.cljc:74-75` | `#{:blocked :parked}` | 6d-b: add `:running` |\n| `rows.cljc:1261`, `:1297`, `:1704-1716`, `:1862`, `:1877-1882`, `:2264-2267` | kind tables, child check, restore arms | 6d-a |\n| `frame.cljc:96`, `:122`, `:124-127`, `:162-164` | `#{:act :tail}`; `(pos? n)`; C3; P1 | 6d-a |\n| `semantic.cljc:2`, `:137`, `:331-333` | kernel docstring; `vm-hot` entry; `step`/`run` | 6d-a adds `run-fuel`; 6d-b adds the gate guard; docstring names both |\n| `engine.cljc:1458-1491` | `advance-install` \"`running` runs it until it halts or parks\" | 6d-a: budgeted; docstring |\n| `safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1-20`, `:1012-1025` | docstring; \"not at a safepoint\" refusal on a stepped machine | 6d-a: docstring says what it now proves; the refusal row becomes the positive `running-kind` case |\n| `:1057-1066` | `a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent` | 6d-a: becomes `a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-carried` (law 1/2 on the F1 and F2 states) |\n| `:1886-1904` | `layout-refusals` \"a kernel stepped to a non-safepoint pc\" | 6e: positive; a hand-built entry outside every body still refuses; a `:push` entry relabels |\n| `stage_e_scenarios.cljc:184-186` | \"no liftable safepoint, UCF 7.4.1\" | 6d-b: \"a hold, UCF 7.7.4\" |\n\n### 3.9 Slices, order, parallelism, effort\n\nHosts: JVM, Node, Dart for every slice. Verification per `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`: iterate on `bb test:clj`; the three-lane quick tier at landing (`bb test:sub yin.vm`; confirm \"Testing yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test\" and every touched namespace in the Node log); the slow tier once at 6d-a landing and once at 6e. Every refusal test asserts zero serves (lift) or zero attaches (lower). Fresh worktree: `mise trust`, `npm ci`. Dart: `loop`/`reduce` over thousands of points (never `for`), frames and windows from `{}`, no floats in the driver. Node: `[:lit nil]` survives `select-keys`; the C2 float tamper is the canary.\n\n**Order and parallelism.**\n\n```\nmodule-pins commit 2 (merging now)  ──►  6d-a (branch srvm-6d-a from master after the merge)\n                                              │\n                                              ├──►  6d-b (srvm-6d-b)   ┐  in parallel; both review-gated\n                                              └──►  6e   (srvm-6e)     ┘  independently; 6d-b merges first\n                                                                            if both are green (the holder\n                                                                            closes the owner's invariant)\n```\n\n- Nothing of 6d runs in parallel with module-pins' merge except writing the 6d-a brief: 6d-a's `rows.cljc` hunks (`lift-task*`, `check-pendings!`, `check-install!`, `restore-task`, `lower-task` opts) are adjacent to or inside commit 2's (`discharge!`/`requires` `:1603-1700`, `check-modules!` `:1883-1963`, `lower-task` `:2273-2350`), and both re-mint `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt`. Branching after the merge re-mints once.\n- 6d-b and 6e are independent of each other and both depend on 6d-a's grammar. The owner's invariant is **not** closed by 6d-a alone (fork-only); 6d-b closes it for the holder.\n- After integration: re-run module-store/free-name discharge and receiver admission at mid-body contexts (module-pins' Q5 host-profile re-discharge meets `run` contexts there).\n\n#### 6d-a: the reference lane\n\n**Change.**\n- `frame.cljc`: S1 (`:96-98`) admits `:run` with its exact key set; C2 (`:117-123`) `(pos? n)` for act and return frames only; C3 (`:124-127`) skips the frame itself for a run; `window-defect` (`:49-61`) takes the wanted set and omits `:window-self` for a run; context `:running` in P1 (`:162-164`); A1-A6, T1 skipped for a run.\n- `rows.cljc`: `row-table` (`:49-69`) `:run`, `:ready`, task slots 17/18; `pending-table` unchanged; `lift-task*` (`:1650-1698`) steps 1-2 and nodes per §3.2; `state-node` (`:1508-1518`) run arm; `validate-frames` (`:1109-1144`) slot 17 in `:running`, slot 18's waits in `:wait`; `check-pendings!` (`:1877-1882`) the kind table; `check-install!` (`:1862`) child kinds by phase; `task-ops` (`:1261`, `:1263-1277`) and `custody-baseline` (`:1297`) kind set and completed-op rule; `protect!` unchanged (slot 13 only); `header-custody` (`:1704-1716`); `restore-task` (`:2253-2268`) running arm and ready restore; `lower-task` docstring/opts; `rows->frame`/`frame->rows` (`:1023-1058`, `:1082`) the run shape; `rows/checkpoint` inspector follows `custody-baseline`.\n- `layout.cljc`: `stack-layouts` (`:118-131`) jump and terminator layouts; `state-prim`/`lower-defect` (`:863-874`, `:916-953`) `:run` arm and slots 17/18 (so a primitive in a running window refuses into the register backend before attach even before 6e).\n- `engine.cljc`: `advance-install` takes a budget (`:1458-1491`); `scheduler-round-fuel` beside `scheduler-round` (`:2315-2326`).\n- `semantic.cljc`: public `run-fuel` (§3.4); no change to `vm-hot`'s loop (the gate guard is 6d-b).\n- Tests: `frame_test`, `rows_test`, `safepoint_harness_test` (stepping driver, laws at every boundary, quick sample, tampers, ready/product cases), `layout_test` (static checks), `semantic_vm_test` (`run-fuel`), `engine_test` (budgeted children).\n- Docs: §3.7.1 (continuation §0.1, §0.2, §1.1-1.3, §2.3, §2.6, §3.2-3.3, §4.3, §5.4, §5.6-5.7, §7.1-7.2, §7.4, §8.3, §8.6, §9, §11, the six sites), §3.7.2, §3.7.3 UCF §7.4.1-7.4.2, §7.9; §3.7.4; §3.7.5; §3.7.7; the known-gaps row.\n\n**Depends on.** Module-pins commit 2 on master.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n1. `frame-test/run-state-rules`: a `:run` built from `run-fuel` at every boundary of `:worked-example` validates in context `:running`; one tamper per rule: C2 (a float pc → `:pc-kind`; `pc = (count v)` → `:pc-bounds`); a **body-start pc validates**; R1 (a key `≥ k(body)` → `:window-scope`); R2 (one live key removed → `:window-missing`; one dead register added → `:window-extra`); S1 (a `deliver` key, or a `pending` key, on a `run` → `:frame-shape`); P1 (a `run` in context `:wait` → `:pending-presence`; an `act` in context `:running` → `:pending-presence`); an `act` at `p+1` and a `run` at `p+1` with `rd` present both validate and each is refused under the other's kind.\n2. `rows-test/run-and-ready-rows`: `frame->rows ∘ rows->frame` identity on every `:run` of item 1; `:run` is five slots (a sixth is `:row-shape` at decode step 2 by raw edit with rehash); a `:wait` whose state cref names a `:run` is `:undecodable` at step 3; a `:ready` whose wait cref names a `:run` row, or whose status is not a keyword, is `:undecodable`; a `:ready` with reason other than `:next`/`:put` is refused at lift `:yin.k/non-portable :ready-reason` with zero serves.\n3. `rows-test/task-product-kinds`: (a) `run-fuel` mid-body → kind `:running`, one `:run`, no waits, no ready; lower restores `:control` at the pc with the trimmed window and runs nothing (zero steps observed through the toy channel); `vm/run` reaches the reference result and trace; relift equals the lift under `canonical-under` in a fresh receiver. (b) **F2 state** (two-callers, one response, sweep, resume): kind `:running` with one `:run` and one `:wait`; law 1 (the remaining waiter completes after the receiver's own response), law 2. (c) **F1 state** (both responses, sweep, resume, `run-fuel 1`): kind `:running` with one `:run` and one `:ready`; law 1 (the queued wake resumes before any new wake, its value delivered once, nothing re-appended: the receiver's call-out stream receives no second request), law 2. (d) the state after `check-wait-set` alone (control nil, waits 1, ready 1): kind `:blocked` with one wait and one ready. (e) a halted activation beside a queued wake (hand-built per §3.1): kind `:blocked`, restored flags `blocked? true`, law 1. (f) negative controls: a `:running` root with two `:run` crefs, with none, with a parked-id, with a result; a `:halted` root with a `:ready`; a `:blocked` root with neither waits nor ready: each `:yin.k/undecodable :kind` (or `:parked-id`) before any attach.\n4. `harness/every-boundary-matches-the-reference` (`^:slow`, `slow/guard`): the stepping driver per §3.6 over the 89 cases (`run-fuel 1`, not `vm/step`); at every boundary with `:control` set: lift, lower into an isolated world, immediate relift (law 2), law 6 with the two window tampers, run to the end (law 1); boundary counts to the park and to the halt pinned per case per host (re-measure once; v1's numbers are the assessment's estimate); the coverage classes of §3.6 each reached; the point count asserted.\n5. `harness/sampled-boundaries-match-the-reference` (quick tier): per bare row (22) five structural boundaries (first body-start reached, the pc after the first primitive `:var`, the pc before the parking instruction, the first `:jump` or `:branch-false` reached, the last `:return` before the halt) plus every boundary of rows 1 and 20; laws 1, 2, 6; about 150 points.\n6. `harness/products-and-refusals`: items 3(b)-(e) through the harness's lift/lower path; a `:run` window holding a cursor-ref, a reified continuation and a nil-valued live register round-trips (rows 14, 16, 22 at the boundary after the capture or the nil load); the `ghost` case lifted at the pc **before** the constant-dead branch refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{ghost}}` (Q6 pinned); a held `:observe` still refuses `:reason-mismatch` with zero serves.\n7. `layout-test/static-checks-at-every-pc`: on `layout-corpus` and the matrix's vectors, at every reachable pc: `virt` single-valued over `L(p)`'s physical image; the ordered region form has `|layout(p)| + |uses(p)| = |L(p)|` with duplicates counted, and the set equations of §3.5; **the jump pc of every conditional and every terminator has a layout** (the F5 vectors pinned); sizes asserted per segment.\n8. `engine-test/budgeted-install-children`: a child whose initializer loops for thousands of instructions is stopped by `run-fuel n` at a boundary (phase `:running`, `:control` set), lifted as a `:running` child, lowered under receiver admission, continued to `linked`; a nested initializer; a tick over a tree of `t` tasks executes at most `n × t` instructions (counted through a probe primitive).\n9. `semantic-vm-test/run-fuel`: `run-fuel n` on an active machine equals `n` applications of `vm-hot … 1` (record equality minus telemetry fields); on a blocked machine it is one budgeted round; `maybe-run`'s bridge path is exercised once.\n10. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts; Node log shows every touched namespace; the slow tier once at landing on three hosts.\n\n**Days.** 3 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = 4.5; docs 1 beside it.\n\n#### 6d-b: holder, custody, expiry, recovery\n\n**Change.**\n1. `driver.cljc` `run-cycle` (`:2111-2144`): `:fuel` config key (checked in `check-common!` `:177-207`, positive integer or nil); `(if fuel (sr/run-fuel m0 fuel) (vm/run m0))` under the existing gate and halted guards; **each stage's machine assoc'd into the state before the next tenure check**; `cycle-result` adds `:yin.k/at-boundary true`; `step-active`'s catch ends the run on the state it was handed and records `:yin.k/retained-boundary`; `end-run` keeps the retained machine on the ended state (gated `:ended`, never offered).\n2. `export.cljc`: `kind-of` (`:274-282`) `:running` first; `enter` (`:121-149`) drops `first-refusal` and moves the ready queue into the record (`:ready []` on the machine; `:yin.k/recovery-ready` on fenced restore); `recovery-metadata`/`valid-state?` count ready entries; `abort` unchanged (its conditions are right).\n3. `semantic.cljc` `vm-hot` entry gate guard (§3.3 outcome 4); `run-fuel` inherits it.\n4. `ledger.cljc:277`, `authority/grant.cljc:74-75`: admit `:running`.\n5. Docstrings per §3.8 (driver, compose, export header).\n6. Tests: `driver_test`: a fuelled run preempts a long pure loop at a boundary, the phase stays `:running`, the next tick continues; `hand-off` mints, journals, freezes and offers a `:running` pack; a candidate lowers it (awaiting-grant → grant → `activate`) and reaches the reference result; the five clock-controlled cuts of §3.3 with their assertions; `rehydrate-fenced` byte equality for a running root with a wait and with a ready entry, and the rehydrated machine refuses `vm/step` (\"Gate is closed\"); two competing candidates; stale-source stepping refused; uncertain-offer abort refused; expired-grant admission refused; a halted exclusive root still restores without a grant (F6 regression). `ledger_test`/`grant_test`: a `:running` baseline is offerable; `:halted` is not. `compose_rows_test`: an exclusive world handed off at a boundary completes on the successor; one with a running install child. `stage_e_scenarios`: `[:until \"h1\" :running n] [:hand-off \"h1\"]` with fuel so the hand-off lands mid-body; recovery cut before and after the offer; a tenure-expiry cut between slices asserting the last checkpoint is what recovers.\n7. Docs: §3.7.1 §7.3; §3.7.3 UCF §7.7.4, §7.7.6, §7.8, §7.10, §7.11, §7.11.1, `:2842`; §3.7.6.\n\n**Depends on.** 6d-a. **Days.** 2.5 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 4.\n\n#### 6e: the cross-engine half\n\n**Change.** `layout.cljc`: `lift-running`/`lower-running` over the kernels' live fields (register `{:pc :registers :frames :continuation :store-of}`, stack `{:pc :stack :frames :continuation :store-of}`); `virt` over a given virtual set (`L(p)` for a run); `register-window` parameterised; nil fill outside `L(p)`; `h->a` relabels `:push`; `slice-window`/`lift-stack` dead-prefix rule (`≥`, prefix dropped, diagnostic); `lower-stack`/`stack-k` unchanged in shape; `safepoint!` run mode (any pc inside a body); `canonical-task` lifts an active kernel as `:running` (with waits and ready as the semantic lift does; backends still refuse installs); `kernel-task` lowers a `:running` machine into a running kernel. No kernel edit. `layout_test`, the harness: laws 4 and 5 at every aligned boundary on the 61 lane cases.\n\n**Acceptance.** Law 4: one root id on three lanes at aligned boundary `i` (stack index after `:push` relabelling), taken and untaken branches both pinned, ordered operand multiplicity pinned; law 5: six ordered pairs at every boundary, each continuing to the reference result, with the **full-payload refusal oracle** of §3.6 (exact refusals and exact successful transfers per case and direction); the `:push` relabelling pinned on one row; **item 6, tail residuals**: either a lane-fragment case with a non-empty ambient layout at a tail call exercises the dead-prefix rule (lift drops, lower omits, law 1 and law 4 hold, the diagnostic reports the count) or the static check `layout(p) = []` at every tail-call pc of the fragment proves none exists and the rule is pinned on a hand-built vector; `layout-refusals` rewritten: a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts on both lanes with zero serves refused; a hand-built entry outside every body refuses `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; the fragment is not widened (rows 7-12 and 17 excluded; row 4 not lowered).\n\n**Depends on.** 6d-a; independent of 6d-b. **Days.** 3.5 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 5.\n\n#### Effort\n\n| Slice | Implementation days | Review rounds | Days |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6d-a reference lane, grammar, fuel, static layouts | 3 | 3 | 4.5 |\n| 6d-b holder, custody, expiry, recovery | 2.5 | 3 | 4 |\n| 6e cross-engine | 3.5 | 3 | 5 |\n| docs (§3.7, §3.8), split across the three | 1.5 | 1 | 1.5 |\n| **Total** | **10.5** | | **15**, or 10 without 6e |\n\nWhat the review said to keep: targeted counterexamples (F1, F2, F5 states), the all-pc static gates, the quick structural sample, one full three-host run at landing, every custody race and scheduler product. What may be cut during iteration: repetitive broad end-to-end cases. No wall-time is forecast; the slow tier's duration is measured once at 6d-a landing and recorded in the ledger row.\n\nRouting: codex (gpt-6.1-sol, workspace-write, stdin closed), one brief per slice, foreground lanes only, report inside the worktree's `collab/`; Fable reviews at every gate under the Q6 rule; glm/cmd as non-author fallback; commit only on Architect sign-off; never commit `collab/` or `docs/agents/routing-status.md`.\n\n### 3.10 Decisions for the owner (defaults stated so 6d-a can start)\n\n1. **Task slots 17 (`run`) and 18 (`ready`)**, re-minting every pinned root once right after module-pins commit 2. Alternative: a mixed slot 13 `[:refs :wait :run :ready]`, no re-mint, every slot-13 traversal audited for three row kinds. **Recommend 17/18**: it removes F7's misread class by construction, and the re-mint coincides with one already happening.\n2. **Fuel as a holder config key `:fuel`**, default nil (today's behaviour; nothing changes until a composition opts in). **Recommend as stated.**\n3. **No automatic export at the bound in 6d-b**; `hand-off` stays explicit; `:yin.k/at-boundary` is the signal. A later \"arm when remaining tenure < one renewal interval\" policy belongs in `compose`, needs no VM clock. **Recommend as stated.**\n4. **No adoption of a retained boundary**: an ended holder keeps `:yin.k/retained-boundary` for inspection only; recovery is the last durable checkpoint; the documents state the loss. A future ledger rule \"a regrant to the same holder may continue from its retained boundary when the input prefix and next-op-seq agree\" is possible but is new authority and not in 6d. **Recommend as stated.**\n5. **Q6**: keep edge-reachability liveness; pin the pre-branch `ghost` refusal. A \"no\" means a constant-propagation pass in `live-pcs`, a separate slice. **Recommend keep.**\n6. **Completed `:put` wakes are completed operations**: op-id well-formed, excluded from retained writes and `protect!`, never re-appended. **Recommend as stated** (the alternative, counting them as retained, would make the receiver re-append a datom that is already on the stream).\n7. **`:push` relabelled to its successor**, not refused. **Recommend relabel** (no guest semantics run; the stack content is already its successor's).\n8. **Holds stay refusals** (`:observe`, held, unminted cursor, pending close, link cursor not installed), documented as driver seams mid-step that the driver resolves without guest execution; 6d-b verifies each resolves within one cycle and gives any that does not a known-gaps row. **Recommend as stated.**\n\n### 3.11 Risks\n\n1. **A liveness bug surfaces** at a pc no park looked at: law 1 fails there. A finding, not a slice risk; the main reason to run the every-boundary laws.\n2. **The ready carrier meets a wake reason I did not enumerate** (a link wake entering the queue): pinned as `:ready-reason` refusal in 6d-a and reported; widening is a small follow-up, not a redesign.\n3. **Re-mint churn**: two re-mints in a week (module-pins, 6d-a). Mitigation: order (§3.9); 6d's own acceptance pins boundary counts and refusal kinds, never root ids.\n4. **Dead-prefix rule untested on a real case** if the fragment has none: 6e item 6 pins it on a hand-built vector; the rule is then a documented check awaiting a natural case.\n5. **Slow-tier time on Dart**: slow tier only; measured once.\n6. **Gate guard cost**: one map read per `run-fuel`/`vm-hot` call; a reviewer confirms it is outside the loop.\n7. **Fuel too small or too large**: a composition knob; default nil.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Verification honesty\n\n- Every `file:line` above was read in `7c4dff32` today. The review's citations held in every case I checked; where it cited a function by name I read the body.\n- **Run**: one throwaway JVM test namespace reproducing F1, F2 and F5 (transcript excerpts in §1); deleted; `git status` shows only `collab/`. **Not run**: the harness, the slow tier, any Node or Dart lane, any driver or Stage E scenario, any performance probe. Boundary counts and point counts are to be measured once and pinned by 6d-a.\n- **Claims that are checks** (not theorems): `L(p)` sufficient at every boundary; injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc; the ordered stack equation with the jump/terminator layouts at every pc; the dead-prefix rule; the refusal-oracle equality; cross-host boundary-count equality; the per-tree fuel bound.\n- **Not claimed**: that the de Bruijn kernels reconstruct a `run` state today (6e); that the holder can preempt today (`vm/run` at `driver.cljc:2120`); that fuel bounds wall-clock time; that fuel repairs expiry loss (only exporting before the bound does); that a tail call with a non-empty ambient layout exists in the fragment (6e decides); that link wakes never enter the queue (6d-a verifies by reading `poll-links`); that module-pins commit 2's final hunks are exactly those of `origin/srvm-module-pins` at `62ed14fa` (they are what is merging now).\n- **Timestamps**: the brief dates itself 2026-10-17 21:00 +07 and the owner's ruling 2026-10-17; the machine clock at completion reads 2026-10-16 14:40 +07. The header uses the clock; the ruling's date is quoted as the brief gives it.\n\n## 5. Readiness\n\n**Implementation-ready**: 6d-a, as specified in §3.9, under the defaults of §3.10, **the moment module-pins commit 2 is on master**; its brief can be written now from §3.1, §3.2, §3.4, §3.5 (static part), §3.6, §3.9 and the §3.7 text it owns. 6d-b and 6e are specified to the same level and are ready to brief when 6d-a merges; they run in parallel. **Start first**: 6d-a. The owner's invariant is closed for the holder only when 6d-b lands; the plan says so and does not call it closed earlier.\n\nPLAN-V2-READY\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat review-inputs/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat review-inputs/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-16 07:19:41 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-16 14:19:41 +07\nCoding-Agent: codex (GPT-6; exact deployment model not exposed to this session)\nSession-ID: 01a14392-2b1d-7081-a47e-76c8cc6832b7\n\n# Independent second architect review: slice 6d\n\n**Verdict: REJECT the plan as implementation-ready.** The `:run` activation is the right extension, and a running exclusive root must be a custody subject. However, this plan still excludes supported scheduler states, leaves an unbounded child-execution path, and specifies a false all-PC stack equation. These are defects in the proposed closure of the owner's invariant, not reasons to reconsider that invariant.\n\nReviewed HEAD: `7c4dff32e8633a53c361bf7f34282d51fcf70363`, rather than the plan's `ce76c846`. Citations below refer to files read in this worktree. `plan` means `collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md`; `assessment` means `collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md`. I read both, the requested architectural documents, and the implementation paths discussed below. The prompt's `docs/design/universal-continuation-format.md` does not exist here; its actual path is `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (abbreviated `UCF` below). The clock is 2026-10-16; the owner's 2026-10-17 ruling is treated as supplied instruction, without inventing a corrected date.\n\nThis is a source-based review. No VM, test suite, performance probe, network operation, or build was run. The counterexamples are deductions from the cited transitions; their runtime reproduction remains an implementation acceptance requirement. Only this report was written.\n\n## Findings\n\nEach entry gives **severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | recommended correction**. P1 blocks the proposed architectural acceptance; P2 is an implementation or specification gap that must be resolved in the affected slice.\n\n### F1 — The ready-queue exception contradicts arbitrary-PC migration\n\n**P1, architectural defect | plan:148; src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2205; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc:107 | any-PC continuation completeness | carry scheduler state or establish a semantics-preserving, bounded normalization at the same guest PC.**\n\nThe plan explicitly admits an active activation with another completed wake queued, yet requires running the active activation to a park before export. `run-loop` prioritizes the active activation (`engine.cljc:2211`) over the queue (`:2216`); `vm-hot`'s active path never drains that queue (`semantic.cljc:139`). A recursively calling pure activation can run indefinitely. Retrying `hand-off` after another fuel slice can therefore refuse forever, or until the lease expires. The fact that the machine began with multiple waits does not make it malformed: the existing transport expressly preserves ordered multiple waits (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:455`), and the harness transports `two-callers` (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1069`, `:1093`).\n\nThe result cannot be discarded or reclassified as a pending wait: the wake carries an already observed value/status and cursor resource updates (`engine.cljc:443`). Repeating a completed write can duplicate it; dropping a completed read loses it. The assessment already recognized representability (assessment:101, section B.1); its decision to refuse is no longer compatible with the owner's ruling.\n\nSpecify an ordered ready-outcome carrier, including terminal outcomes as deferred scheduler outcomes, cursor-cell aliasing, resource updates and custody implications. Alternatively prove a transformation that preserves the current PC, current activation, queued outcomes and scheduling order without executing arbitrary guest work. Do not describe “run until it parks” as such a transformation. Add a migration case with two completed wakes and an unbounded first activation, and prove export without executing that activation further.\n\n### F2 — R0 rejects a state that the scheduler constructs\n\n**P1, architectural defect | plan:96; src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2294; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:116 | no information loss; supported task domain | represent an active activation together with remaining waits.**\n\nClearing control when creating a wait does not prove that control and a wait set can never coexist. Start from the admitted two-waiter machine, complete only one response, then execute `scheduler-round`. `check-wait-set` retains the unresolved wait (`engine.cljc:2194`); `resume-from-run-queue` pops the completed entry, clears blocked/halted flags and leaves the remaining wait set (`:2305`); `register-restore` sets control without clearing those waits (`semantic.cljc:116`). The result is active control plus a nonempty wait set, with no ready queue required. Explicit resume likewise restores into the existing machine (`engine.cljc:2360`).\n\nThis is an admitted blocked carrier resumed by the kernel, even if a single-control program does not initially create two waits on its own. R0 cannot call it inconsistent merely because the new running grammar has no place for it. F1 and F2 require a task-state product (current activation, ordered pending waits, ordered ready outcomes), not just a mutually exclusive fourth root arm. The activation-level `:run` row can remain as proposed. Update the kind rules and distinguish root classification from activation shape. Test one-woken/one-waiting as well as two-woken cases; do not manufacture them only as negative tests.\n\n### F3 — Fuel does not bound an install child or the entire program tick\n\n**P1, architectural defect | plan:113; src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1458; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1862 | preemption at every executing task's boundary | propagate explicit execution budgets through scheduling and child execution, and admit running install children.**\n\nThe proposed `run-fuel` calls an unrestricted `scheduler-round` when the root is inactive. That round polls links and advances installs (`engine.cljc:2168`; `poll-links`/`advance-installs`); `advance-install` calls `vm/run` on the child (`:1475`). A blocked root installing a module whose initializer computes indefinitely still never returns to the holder. A one-instruction root fuel budget does not help. The assessment's “child states are unreachable unless the sweep takes fuel; not needed” conclusion is precisely the gap the new invariant makes necessary to close.\n\nDefine how a budget reaches child tasks, how it is shared or bounded per tree, and how a child remains at a materialized boundary when exhausted. Current lower admits only a blocked child (`rows.cljc:1862`); the plan neither changes that check nor supplies running-child phase acceptance. Include a long initializer, a nested initializer, and handoff/recovery while the child is running. Retain receiver admission checks for each child.\n\nFuel also counts VM instructions, not elapsed time: `:call` invokes a host function synchronously (`semantic.cljc:193`), stream construction invokes the supplied constructor (`engine.cljc:480`), and a scheduler round can scan many entries. State the cooperative guarantee precisely: interruption between completed instructions, with no hard wall-clock bound for one atomic instruction or unbounded host seam. Avoid promising a lease deadline from `n` instructions alone. No clock-to-instruction conversion belongs in the VM.\n\n### F4 — A tenure failure still loses the just-computed boundary\n\n**P1, plan/implementation gap | plan:115; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:2118; :2248 | explicit state retention and exclusive authority | return the new boundary as fenced data on tenure loss, and specify which authority may adopt it.**\n\nAfter `m1` is computed, `require-tenure!` at `driver.cljc:2123` may throw before `m1` is associated with holder state (`:2139`). The catch at `:2253` calls `end-run` on the original argument, not on `m1`. Fuel changes neither this control flow nor the later throws inside replay/emit/read. When tenure dies between completed ticks, the next entry check also ends the run rather than making a successor. `exit-mint` itself requires tenure (`:1144`).\n\nThe plan's sentence that a holder can hand off *before* the bound is useful composition policy, but it does not resolve its claim about tenure dying between slices. Specify separate outcomes for successful preemption while tenure is live, expired-tenure retention, and recovery from the last durable checkpoint. Retain the newest completed pure state before a post-slice check can discard it. An expired holder must not offer a valid successor or resume merely because it still owns bytes: adoption needs authority consistent with the ledger's existing origin/completion rules. If expiry intentionally replays the last committed checkpoint, say that progress since it is lost and do not call the invariant fully repaired by fuel.\n\nAdd clock-controlled cuts immediately before the slice, immediately after it, during the later driver stages, and between slices. The source must remain fenced, with either a durably authorized successor or an explicitly documented recovery checkpoint. Keeping automatic export a composition decision is sound; leaving the expiry semantics unspecified is not.\n\n### F5 — The all-PC stack equation is false with today's layout\n\n**P1 for 6e, specification defect also blocking 6d-a item 7 | plan:322; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc:118; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/code.cljc:349 | every-PC reconstruction | derive layouts at control-flow and terminator PCs, not just expression nodes.**\n\n`stack-layouts` walks `(:expr body)` and its `:children`, assigning only each node's `:pc`. The parser stores a conditional's jump in `:jump`, not as a child (`code.cljc:389`), and excludes the body terminator from the expression (`:413`). Thus the purported existing layout is not defined at every PC.\n\nA concrete structured conditional is:\n\n```clojure\n[[:const 1 true]             ; 0: test\n [:branch-false 1 4]         ; 1\n [:const 0 10]               ; 2: consequent result\n [:jump 5]                   ; 3\n [:const 0 20]               ; 4: alternate result\n [:halt 0]]                  ; 5\n```\n\nAt PC 3, `L(3) = #{0}` by `analysis/successors` and `:halt`'s use, while `stack-layout(3)` is nil and `uses(:jump)` is empty. The planned equality therefore reads `#{} = #{0}` and fails. The stack kernel really holds `[10]`: `:const` pushes it (`debruijn/stack.cljc:689`), and `:jump` preserves it (`:777`). The running lift must identify that value as the conditional's result register. At the halt, the result is also on the stack although there is no expression-node layout entry; its operand use supplies the mapping there.\n\nAdd branch-result layouts at jumps/joins, explicit empty ambient layouts at terminators where appropriate, and path-sensitive dynamic checks of ordered stack contents. Audit all instruction categories rather than checking the set equation alone. Tail-call residuals also require checking **after** entering the callee: the stack closure-call transition retains `stack'` on a tail call (`stack.cljc:737`–`:748`), so dead caller operands can survive below a running callee. Dropping them only at the tail-call PC does not establish the layout at the next body-start boundary. Test a tail closure call in operand position with nonempty ambient operands and live `K`.\n\nThe register-allocation argument is substantially stronger: intervals include `L(p) ∪ def(p)`, and a slot expires only when its end is strictly less than the new PC (`derive.cljc:127`, `:140`). No non-parking-specific register collision was found. This does not validate the separate stack claim.\n\n### F6 — Grant-check instruction contradicts the intended halted behavior\n\n**P2, concrete specification error | plan:336; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:2039 | custody by kind | leave the halted exemption intact.**\n\nThe plan says to “drop `(= :halted kind)` from the skip only; keep `fenced?`.” Current code skips grants only for fenced restoration or a halted root. Running roots already take `accept-grant!` once admitted by the surrounding grammar. Removing the halted test instead requires a grant for an origin-only terminal result, contradicting plan:53 and UCF's result-is-not-a-lease-subject rule (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:473`). Change the brief to “preserve `check-custody!` dispatch; extend admitted kinds elsewhere,” and pin halted restoration without a grant as a regression case.\n\n### F7 — The primitive refusal predicate is broader than a primitive in L(pc)\n\n**P2, 6e acceptance gap | plan:349; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc:853; :947 | refuse unsupported values before attachment | compute the expected refusal set over the complete payload.**\n\nThe existing predicate checks current windows, lexical bindings, nested collections/closure captures, every return frame, and parked states (`state-prim`, `frame-prim`, `value-prim`). A primitive can remain in a lexical binding or saved `K` frame while no primitive is in the current `L(pc)`. Equating every register-target refusal count with only the current primitive-live PC set is therefore not generally correct. The harness already has lexical, captured and nested primitive refusal fixtures (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1931`).\n\nExtend the expected set to all payload roots, independently of the implementation traversal, and assert exact refusals **and successful transfers** per case/direction. Update callers too: `lower-defect` currently feeds `(first (:slots w))` to `state-prim` because slot 13 contains `:wait` rows (`layout.cljc:947`); adding only a `:run` branch to `state-prim` will not make it receive the new direct `:run` row. Audit every slot-13 wait destructuring, including restore's `entry` mapping, for the new sum. Do not claim the full three-obstruction list is complete: existing image collision, free-env, install/link and retained-FFI limits remain applicable.\n\n### F8 — The harness description would compare the wrong state and mutate its fixture\n\n**P2, implementation acceptance gap | plan:319; docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:916 | law 2 has no intervening execution; append-only effect discipline | fork each test point before running it, and compare relift immediately after lower.**\n\nThe listed order says lower, run to the end, then relift for root equality. That produces a halted or parked root rather than the captured running root. The actual law explicitly requires **no execution** between lower and lift. Write the order as lift, lower, immediate relift/law 2, then run/law 1.\n\nAlso isolate each snapshot's resource world. Running the receiver to completion on the stepping source's mutable test streams can append effects or consume scripted outcomes needed by subsequent snapshot points. The existing ring/toy fixtures perform real stream operations. Use independent equivalent worlds or a deterministic input/effect script with fresh identities normalized by the existing correspondence. Preserve negative controls for cursor positions/descriptors, windows and lexical values.\n\n“Every PC” in a dynamic driver means every boundary of the chosen execution, not every CFG-reachable PC or every possible activation. Pin coverage by `(segment, pc, structural state)` and add both branch outcomes, body entries, jumps, returns, tail transitions, resume/capture, scheduler products and install children. The static all-PC pass and dynamic path pass are complementary; neither proves a universal theorem by itself.\n\n### F9 — The new document text overstates liftability and its inventory is incomplete\n\n**P2, documentation defect | plan:254; plan:408; docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:1250 | consistent executable contract | distinguish representable state, portable values, satisfied dependencies and export readiness.**\n\nThe amendments announce “Every instruction boundary lifts,” but retain queues and holds as refusals and intentionally pin the pre-branch `ghost` false refusal (plan:321). UCF's proposed safepoint definition also says it carries a pending wait (plan:256), although explicit park is a no-wait safepoint and halt carries a result (`yin.vm.semantic.md:680`). Define a safepoint as a program-driven park/halt boundary, with delivery/pending only for the appropriate cases. Define any-PC **representability** independently of receiver satisfaction and resource availability. F1/F2 cannot be hidden under an “export readiness” qualifier, since those states are already part of supported machine execution.\n\nThe `:push` wording is also too strong: it says “no canonical state exists here,” while the assessment correctly notes that stack `:push` only advances PC (`stack.cljc:722`). It is a stuttering boundary with the same values as its successor, not an intrinsically unserializable interior. Either canonicalize it to the successor without running guest semantics, or label refusal as an explicit backend limitation. Retaining that limitation is a scope choice under the owner's fixed backend fragment, not proof of arbitrary hardware-PC coverage.\n\nI independently searched `docs/`, `src/`, and `test/` for safepoint restrictions and `not-at-safepoint`. The main live refusal sites in rows, layout and harness are covered by section 6. Additional omissions/contradictions to reconcile:\n\n- `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:1250` still prescribes a stepped-kernel refusal; no disposition in the inventory. Replace it when 6e lands or label its historical scope explicitly.\n- `yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:2421` still supplies replacement conformance text testing only parking windows. Retain it as a historical amendment or add the running-state liveness check; do not leave two normative replacements.\n- `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:799` (step 7) lists only the old frame kinds and contexts; section 3.4 does not amend that validator list to include `:run`/`:running`.\n- `UCF:802` still specifies only saved-window/parking checks; the proposed section 7.4.2 changes totality but does not add the `L(pc)` running-window check there.\n- `UCF:1912` still says “no engine change,” and `UCF:2842`'s no-layer-collapsing row still says the hot loop is untouched and lifting is over a blocked machine. The proposed kernel-entry gate changes that engine contract even though it leaves the instruction loop intact. Update both.\n- The continuation document's root custody prose at `:368` and `:405`, its context input at `:433`, and its static/runtime inventory in sections 4–5 must agree with the new forms. Adding a later paragraph alone leaves old exhaustive lists misleading.\n\nGuest-event safepoints in `yang.antlr.md` and related code are a separate delivery policy and need not be widened. Historical logs/reports should receive explicit supersession references, not rewritten history. Also reconcile R3/S1 versus plan:316: a `run` with forbidden pending is specified as `:frame-shape` by S1 but expected as `:pending-presence` by acceptance. Pick one ordered diagnostic and test it consistently.\n\n## Answers to the eight review questions\n\n1. **Canonical activation versus complete task.** `put-registers` really materializes segment/PC/window/env/K at fuel exhaustion (`semantic.cljc:50`, `:142`). Those five components are an activation, not a self-contained CESK task. The store is already explicit in the frozen transition relation (`yin.vm.semantic.md:197`); `gensym` depends on `id-counter` (`engine.cljc:2368`); parked continuations, stores, module stores and resource cells are needed too. The existing task wrapper carries the counter, whole root store, parked table and install tree (`rows.cljc:1670`–`:1694`), and module stores reached through environments/closures. A run-state walk must register the current environment and code context before collecting those stores and code. The proposed walk does so. Queues and remaining waits are the missing scheduler components (F1/F2).\n\n   `analysis.cljc:45` computes backward liveness for every body PC, not just parks; the union/difference order at `:64` is correct. This is exact for its declared syntactic CFG fixed point, not path/value-minimal liveness. Branches union both successors, and layout syntax has live sets even when unreachable. No under-approximation was established by reading. Every-PC observational replay plus an independent use/def/CFG oracle is required; checking a validator that uses the same analysis is not independent evidence. For R, keep the all-PC injectivity check; for H, fix F5 first.\n\n   Open cursor cells are explicit resource data, not an implicit stream read position (`rows.cljc:1381`). Seals/owners are reissued by lower, with aliasing through the census. The module registry, primitive implementation/profiles, stream constructors and externally served streams remain composition prerequisites; the five-tuple does not contain them. Telemetry and GC policy are host instrumentation/execution services, not carried semantic registers. `run-fuel` can avoid the public step's telemetry wrapper, but parks/resumes themselves still emit telemetry (`engine.cljc:2352`, `:2363`); “telemetry-free” must mean no per-fuel snapshot, not no possible telemetry emission. Trace replay is deterministic only under equivalent primitives, dependency bindings and external inputs. Ungated effects execute inside steps (`engine.cljc:498`); a host exception is not transactional rollback of an already performed append. The plan should not infer external atomicity merely from losing loop locals.\n\n2. **Custody.** Using the full occurrence/arbitration/next-op-seq/origin arm for a running exclusive root is correct. `header-custody` already chooses it for any non-halted kind (`rows.cljc:1713`), and `with-custody` already chooses gate `:running` for it (`:2058`). Ledger and grantor do need their explicit offerable sets extended (`ledger.cljc:277`, `authority/grant.cljc:75`). Preserve `accept-grant!`, fenced restoration and origin completion; fix F6. The proposed kernel-entry guard correctly closes the new direct pure-step path while exporting/ended. `abort` is not an unconditional return to running: live tenure and proof that no offer may have been accepted are mandatory (`export.cljc:584`, `:590`). Carry that condition into the plan's simplified source paragraph. Add two competing candidates, stale-source stepping, uncertain offer/abort, expired-grant admission, and recovery byte equality for running roots. An exclusive grant authorizes one holder's effects; serializable immutable bytes alone cannot prevent a trusted caller from cloning an object or calling lower twice. Do not claim physical single-instantiation enforcement. F3/F4 remain blockers for lease/preemption completeness.\n\n3. **Quiescence.** Keeping refusal for queues is not compatible with unconditional any-PC migration over the admitted task domain. It is safe refusal, but leaves the named invariant unimplemented. The counterexample need not drain before expiry, and “organically single waiter” cannot exclude carriers already accepted by the system. F1/F2 specify the needed correction.\n\n4. **Foundational invariants.** An explicit fuel argument and holder config add no hidden global, raw callback, shared mutable state, or clock in the kernel. A resumable budget outcome is explicit host scheduling, and migration preserves guest control flow. A new typed row does not require VM knowledge of a dao.space index: it remains stream data interpreted independently. No new graph is assumed if run, K, resource and scheduler references are tabled and validated by the existing graph stage. `:run` is a distinct semantic state, so a row kind is justified; a new root slot need not be invented merely to tag it. The new task product must preserve that separation. Existing local accumulator atoms are construction machinery, not new shared guest state. The plan broadly respects malleability; the phrase “one behavioural change” hides the scheduler and expiry dependencies.\n\n5. **6e.** Register pairing and live-window reconstruction are plausible and grounded in the allocator. Stack mid-expression reconstruction is also plausible, but the claimed existing equation and obstruction list are incomplete (F5/F7). Laws 4/5 are realistic on **aligned semantic boundaries**, after skipping/normalizing H stutters and honoring the documented backend domain. Pin both taken and untaken branches and ordered operand multiplicity; set equality alone loses duplicate-operand information. Keep tail-PC windows distinct from tail *delivery* states. Every-A-instruction step count also needs checking around primitive tail calls and dead suffix syntax, rather than assuming subtraction of `:push` is the only alignment issue. The retained image/free-env/FFI/link limits remain intentionally deferred; no mandate to widen them is inferred here.\n\n6. **Amendments.** The main direction is correct: parking PCs become delivery cases inside a larger state relation, and receiver layout metadata remains derived/static. Replacement text is not ready verbatim because of F1/F2/F9, the malformed-state assertion, and conflicting diagnostics. Section 6 is a good inventory but not complete; concrete additional occurrences are above. Keep the frozen design, continuation row table, tuple validator stages and UCF wording synchronized in the commit that introduces each behavior.\n\n7. **Sequencing/cost.** 6d-a must precede consumers of the new grammar; 6d-b and 6e depend on that grammar but not intrinsically on each other. The asserted total order is therefore not forced (the plan itself says this at :351). Ship kernel fencing with the first exposed running exclusive path, and do not call the owner's invariant closed by fork-only 6d-a. Resolve task/scheduler representation and fuelled install execution before fixing the grammar in a brief. Move the small conditional/jump layout proof ahead of the broad cross-engine matrix; it already falsifies an acceptance gate in 6d-a.\n\n   Module pins and any-PC state are logically independent, but edits to one function and root `requires` are not guaranteed a trivial semantic rebase. Re-run module-store/free-name discharge and receiver admission at mid-body contexts after integration. Existing module pin Q5 (`continuation.md:2776`) is intentionally in-flight and is not a new 6d defect; same-composition harness success does not verify arbitrary receiver equivalence until that slice lands. There is no architecture reason to block initial reference work on a stalled host-profile decision. Land whichever reviewed slice is ready, coordinate rows/fixtures, and revalidate the integrated contract.\n\n   The 11-day estimate is an estimate for a narrower design. It omits scheduler outcomes/remaining waits, running-child carrier and scheduling, expiry retention/adoption decisions, and control-flow stack layouts. Re-estimate those explicitly after the decisions. No measured runtime supports the slow-tier wall-time forecast here. Cut repetitive broad end-to-end cases during iteration; retain targeted counterexamples, all-PC static gates, quick structural samples and a full three-host run at landing. Do not cut custody races or scheduler products to preserve the estimate.\n\n8. **What was verified right; defect/gap/defer distinction.** The core `:run` versus `:act` distinction is correct: current `L(pc)` includes available values, while an `act` excludes the not-yet-delivered destination (`semantic.cljc:64`, `analysis.cljc:92`). The body-start exception to act-only PC/body-continuity rules is necessary (`frame.cljc:122`, `:124`). Retaining nil-valued keys, whole root store, every parked record, source AST associations, fresh receiver seals and existing admission stages is correct. Reusing slot 13 can preserve existing encoded row identities, provided all typed traversals are updated. Grant-gated running custody, fencing before publication, a composition-owned fuel knob, and reference/cross-engine slice separation are all sound foundations checked against the cited code.\n\n   F1/F2/F3 are architectural completeness defects, F5 is a false reconstruction specification, F4 is an unresolved lifecycle design with a concrete implementation loss path, and F6–F9 are implementable contract/acceptance corrections. Existing registry pinning, general correlation/census gaps and the retained backend fragment are deferred work already documented; this review does not silently expand their scope. It does require the plan to stop treating them, or same-composition fixtures, as universal portability evidence.\n\n## Required revision before acceptance\n\n1. **Make the task state complete:** carry active control together with ordered waits and completed scheduler outcomes; remove R0 and the queue exception as purported proofs of arbitrary-PC coverage. Include running install children and explicit tree budgets.\n2. **Complete the custody/preemption lifecycle:** retain the boundary on post-slice tenure failure, specify lawful expiry/recovery behavior, keep halted grant exemption, and verify stale-source/two-candidate/recovery cuts for running roots.\n3. **Repair and prove all-PC reconstruction:** add conditional-jump/join and tail-residual layouts, normalize or explicitly scope stack stutters, fix the full-payload refusal oracle and no-execution law-2 ordering, then synchronize the documents.\n\nThe owner has settled whether to provide preemptive migration. The revision must settle how all already admitted machine states remain expressible and recoverable when preempted. Until these changes are incorporated, the plan's `PLAN-READY` conclusion is unsupported.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '569,639p' review-inputs/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; sed -n '1,47p' review-inputs/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md; rg --files src | rg '(rows|frame|layout|engine|semantic|driver|export|ffi)\\\\.cljc\"'$'\"'; git rev-parse HEAD\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '569,639p' review-inputs/1793650000000-architect-6d-plan-v2.report.md; sed -n '1,47p' review-inputs/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md; rg --files src | rg '(rows|frame|layout|engine|semantic|driver|export|ffi)\\\\.cljc\"'$'\"'; git rev-parse HEAD\"","aggregated_output":"#### 6d-b: holder, custody, expiry, recovery\n\n**Change.**\n1. `driver.cljc` `run-cycle` (`:2111-2144`): `:fuel` config key (checked in `check-common!` `:177-207`, positive integer or nil); `(if fuel (sr/run-fuel m0 fuel) (vm/run m0))` under the existing gate and halted guards; **each stage's machine assoc'd into the state before the next tenure check**; `cycle-result` adds `:yin.k/at-boundary true`; `step-active`'s catch ends the run on the state it was handed and records `:yin.k/retained-boundary`; `end-run` keeps the retained machine on the ended state (gated `:ended`, never offered).\n2. `export.cljc`: `kind-of` (`:274-282`) `:running` first; `enter` (`:121-149`) drops `first-refusal` and moves the ready queue into the record (`:ready []` on the machine; `:yin.k/recovery-ready` on fenced restore); `recovery-metadata`/`valid-state?` count ready entries; `abort` unchanged (its conditions are right).\n3. `semantic.cljc` `vm-hot` entry gate guard (§3.3 outcome 4); `run-fuel` inherits it.\n4. `ledger.cljc:277`, `authority/grant.cljc:74-75`: admit `:running`.\n5. Docstrings per §3.8 (driver, compose, export header).\n6. Tests: `driver_test`: a fuelled run preempts a long pure loop at a boundary, the phase stays `:running`, the next tick continues; `hand-off` mints, journals, freezes and offers a `:running` pack; a candidate lowers it (awaiting-grant → grant → `activate`) and reaches the reference result; the five clock-controlled cuts of §3.3 with their assertions; `rehydrate-fenced` byte equality for a running root with a wait and with a ready entry, and the rehydrated machine refuses `vm/step` (\"Gate is closed\"); two competing candidates; stale-source stepping refused; uncertain-offer abort refused; expired-grant admission refused; a halted exclusive root still restores without a grant (F6 regression). `ledger_test`/`grant_test`: a `:running` baseline is offerable; `:halted` is not. `compose_rows_test`: an exclusive world handed off at a boundary completes on the successor; one with a running install child. `stage_e_scenarios`: `[:until \"h1\" :running n] [:hand-off \"h1\"]` with fuel so the hand-off lands mid-body; recovery cut before and after the offer; a tenure-expiry cut between slices asserting the last checkpoint is what recovers.\n7. Docs: §3.7.1 §7.3; §3.7.3 UCF §7.7.4, §7.7.6, §7.8, §7.10, §7.11, §7.11.1, `:2842`; §3.7.6.\n\n**Depends on.** 6d-a. **Days.** 2.5 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 4.\n\n#### 6e: the cross-engine half\n\n**Change.** `layout.cljc`: `lift-running`/`lower-running` over the kernels' live fields (register `{:pc :registers :frames :continuation :store-of}`, stack `{:pc :stack :frames :continuation :store-of}`); `virt` over a given virtual set (`L(p)` for a run); `register-window` parameterised; nil fill outside `L(p)`; `h->a` relabels `:push`; `slice-window`/`lift-stack` dead-prefix rule (`≥`, prefix dropped, diagnostic); `lower-stack`/`stack-k` unchanged in shape; `safepoint!` run mode (any pc inside a body); `canonical-task` lifts an active kernel as `:running` (with waits and ready as the semantic lift does; backends still refuse installs); `kernel-task` lowers a `:running` machine into a running kernel. No kernel edit. `layout_test`, the harness: laws 4 and 5 at every aligned boundary on the 61 lane cases.\n\n**Acceptance.** Law 4: one root id on three lanes at aligned boundary `i` (stack index after `:push` relabelling), taken and untaken branches both pinned, ordered operand multiplicity pinned; law 5: six ordered pairs at every boundary, each continuing to the reference result, with the **full-payload refusal oracle** of §3.6 (exact refusals and exact successful transfers per case and direction); the `:push` relabelling pinned on one row; **item 6, tail residuals**: either a lane-fragment case with a non-empty ambient layout at a tail call exercises the dead-prefix rule (lift drops, lower omits, law 1 and law 4 hold, the diagnostic reports the count) or the static check `layout(p) = []` at every tail-call pc of the fragment proves none exists and the rule is pinned on a hand-built vector; `layout-refusals` rewritten: a kernel stepped to a non-parking pc lifts on both lanes with zero serves refused; a hand-built entry outside every body refuses `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; the fragment is not widened (rows 7-12 and 17 excluded; row 4 not lowered).\n\n**Depends on.** 6d-a; independent of 6d-b. **Days.** 3.5 + 1.5 (3 rounds) = 5.\n\n#### Effort\n\n| Slice | Implementation days | Review rounds | Days |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6d-a reference lane, grammar, fuel, static layouts | 3 | 3 | 4.5 |\n| 6d-b holder, custody, expiry, recovery | 2.5 | 3 | 4 |\n| 6e cross-engine | 3.5 | 3 | 5 |\n| docs (§3.7, §3.8), split across the three | 1.5 | 1 | 1.5 |\n| **Total** | **10.5** | | **15**, or 10 without 6e |\n\nWhat the review said to keep: targeted counterexamples (F1, F2, F5 states), the all-pc static gates, the quick structural sample, one full three-host run at landing, every custody race and scheduler product. What may be cut during iteration: repetitive broad end-to-end cases. No wall-time is forecast; the slow tier's duration is measured once at 6d-a landing and recorded in the ledger row.\n\nRouting: codex (gpt-6.1-sol, workspace-write, stdin closed), one brief per slice, foreground lanes only, report inside the worktree's `collab/`; Fable reviews at every gate under the Q6 rule; glm/cmd as non-author fallback; commit only on Architect sign-off; never commit `collab/` or `docs/agents/routing-status.md`.\n\n### 3.10 Decisions for the owner (defaults stated so 6d-a can start)\n\n1. **Task slots 17 (`run`) and 18 (`ready`)**, re-minting every pinned root once right after module-pins commit 2. Alternative: a mixed slot 13 `[:refs :wait :run :ready]`, no re-mint, every slot-13 traversal audited for three row kinds. **Recommend 17/18**: it removes F7's misread class by construction, and the re-mint coincides with one already happening.\n2. **Fuel as a holder config key `:fuel`**, default nil (today's behaviour; nothing changes until a composition opts in). **Recommend as stated.**\n3. **No automatic export at the bound in 6d-b**; `hand-off` stays explicit; `:yin.k/at-boundary` is the signal. A later \"arm when remaining tenure < one renewal interval\" policy belongs in `compose`, needs no VM clock. **Recommend as stated.**\n4. **No adoption of a retained boundary**: an ended holder keeps `:yin.k/retained-boundary` for inspection only; recovery is the last durable checkpoint; the documents state the loss. A future ledger rule \"a regrant to the same holder may continue from its retained boundary when the input prefix and next-op-seq agree\" is possible but is new authority and not in 6d. **Recommend as stated.**\n5. **Q6**: keep edge-reachability liveness; pin the pre-branch `ghost` refusal. A \"no\" means a constant-propagation pass in `live-pcs`, a separate slice. **Recommend keep.**\n6. **Completed `:put` wakes are completed operations**: op-id well-formed, excluded from retained writes and `protect!`, never re-appended. **Recommend as stated** (the alternative, counting them as retained, would make the receiver re-append a datom that is already on the stream).\n7. **`:push` relabelled to its successor**, not refused. **Recommend relabel** (no guest semantics run; the stack content is already its successor's).\n8. **Holds stay refusals** (`:observe`, held, unminted cursor, pending close, link cursor not installed), documented as driver seams mid-step that the driver resolves without guest execution; 6d-b verifies each resolves within one cycle and gives any that does not a known-gaps row. **Recommend as stated.**\n\n### 3.11 Risks\n\n1. **A liveness bug surfaces** at a pc no park looked at: law 1 fails there. A finding, not a slice risk; the main reason to run the every-boundary laws.\n2. **The ready carrier meets a wake reason I did not enumerate** (a link wake entering the queue): pinned as `:ready-reason` refusal in 6d-a and reported; widening is a small follow-up, not a redesign.\n3. **Re-mint churn**: two re-mints in a week (module-pins, 6d-a). Mitigation: order (§3.9); 6d's own acceptance pins boundary counts and refusal kinds, never root ids.\n4. **Dead-prefix rule untested on a real case** if the fragment has none: 6e item 6 pins it on a hand-built vector; the rule is then a documented check awaiting a natural case.\n5. **Slow-tier time on Dart**: slow tier only; measured once.\n6. **Gate guard cost**: one map read per `run-fuel`/`vm-hot` call; a reviewer confirms it is outside the loop.\n7. **Fuel too small or too large**: a composition knob; default nil.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Verification honesty\n\n- Every `file:line` above was read in `7c4dff32` today. The review's citations held in every case I checked; where it cited a function by name I read the body.\n- **Run**: one throwaway JVM test namespace reproducing F1, F2 and F5 (transcript excerpts in §1); deleted; `git status` shows only `collab/`. **Not run**: the harness, the slow tier, any Node or Dart lane, any driver or Stage E scenario, any performance probe. Boundary counts and point counts are to be measured once and pinned by 6d-a.\n- **Claims that are checks** (not theorems): `L(p)` sufficient at every boundary; injectivity over `L(p)` at every pc; the ordered stack equation with the jump/terminator layouts at every pc; the dead-prefix rule; the refusal-oracle equality; cross-host boundary-count equality; the per-tree fuel bound.\n- **Not claimed**: that the de Bruijn kernels reconstruct a `run` state today (6e); that the holder can preempt today (`vm/run` at `driver.cljc:2120`); that fuel bounds wall-clock time; that fuel repairs expiry loss (only exporting before the bound does); that a tail call with a non-empty ambient layout exists in the fragment (6e decides); that link wakes never enter the queue (6d-a verifies by reading `poll-links`); that module-pins commit 2's final hunks are exactly those of `origin/srvm-module-pins` at `62ed14fa` (they are what is merging now).\n- **Timestamps**: the brief dates itself 2026-10-17 21:00 +07 and the owner's ruling 2026-10-17; the machine clock at completion reads 2026-10-16 14:40 +07. The header uses the clock; the ruling's date is quoted as the brief gives it.\n\n## 5. Readiness\n\n**Implementation-ready**: 6d-a, as specified in §3.9, under the defaults of §3.10, **the moment module-pins commit 2 is on master**; its brief can be written now from §3.1, §3.2, §3.4, §3.5 (static part), §3.6, §3.9 and the §3.7 text it owns. 6d-b and 6e are specified to the same level and are ready to brief when 6d-a merges; they run in parallel. **Start first**: 6d-a. The owner's invariant is closed for the holder only when 6d-b lands; the plan says so and does not call it closed earlier.\n\nPLAN-V2-READY\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-16 07:19:41 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-16 14:19:41 +07\nCoding-Agent: codex (GPT-6; exact deployment model not exposed to this session)\nSession-ID: 01a14392-2b1d-7081-a47e-76c8cc6832b7\n\n# Independent second architect review: slice 6d\n\n**Verdict: REJECT the plan as implementation-ready.** The `:run` activation is the right extension, and a running exclusive root must be a custody subject. However, this plan still excludes supported scheduler states, leaves an unbounded child-execution path, and specifies a false all-PC stack equation. These are defects in the proposed closure of the owner's invariant, not reasons to reconsider that invariant.\n\nReviewed HEAD: `7c4dff32e8633a53c361bf7f34282d51fcf70363`, rather than the plan's `ce76c846`. Citations below refer to files read in this worktree. `plan` means `collab/1793610000000-architect-6d-plan.report.md`; `assessment` means `collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md`. I read both, the requested architectural documents, and the implementation paths discussed below. The prompt's `docs/design/universal-continuation-format.md` does not exist here; its actual path is `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (abbreviated `UCF` below). The clock is 2026-10-16; the owner's 2026-10-17 ruling is treated as supplied instruction, without inventing a corrected date.\n\nThis is a source-based review. No VM, test suite, performance probe, network operation, or build was run. The counterexamples are deductions from the cited transitions; their runtime reproduction remains an implementation acceptance requirement. Only this report was written.\n\n## Findings\n\nEach entry gives **severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | recommended correction**. P1 blocks the proposed architectural acceptance; P2 is an implementation or specification gap that must be resolved in the affected slice.\n\n### F1 — The ready-queue exception contradicts arbitrary-PC migration\n\n**P1, architectural defect | plan:148; src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2205; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc:107 | any-PC continuation completeness | carry scheduler state or establish a semantics-preserving, bounded normalization at the same guest PC.**\n\nThe plan explicitly admits an active activation with another completed wake queued, yet requires running the active activation to a park before export. `run-loop` prioritizes the active activation (`engine.cljc:2211`) over the queue (`:2216`); `vm-hot`'s active path never drains that queue (`semantic.cljc:139`). A recursively calling pure activation can run indefinitely. Retrying `hand-off` after another fuel slice can therefore refuse forever, or until the lease expires. The fact that the machine began with multiple waits does not make it malformed: the existing transport expressly preserves ordered multiple waits (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:455`), and the harness transports `two-callers` (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1069`, `:1093`).\n\nThe result cannot be discarded or reclassified as a pending wait: the wake carries an already observed value/status and cursor resource updates (`engine.cljc:443`). Repeating a completed write can duplicate it; dropping a completed read loses it. The assessment already recognized representability (assessment:101, section B.1); its decision to refuse is no longer compatible with the owner's ruling.\n\nSpecify an ordered ready-outcome carrier, including terminal outcomes as deferred scheduler outcomes, cursor-cell aliasing, resource updates and custody implications. Alternatively prove a transformation that preserves the current PC, current activation, queued outcomes and scheduling order without executing arbitrary guest work. Do not describe “run until it parks” as such a transformation. Add a migration case with two completed wakes and an unbounded first activation, and prove export without executing that activation further.\n\n### F2 — R0 rejects a state that the scheduler constructs\n\n**P1, architectural defect | plan:96; src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2294; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:116 | no information loss; supported task domain | represent an active activation together with remaining waits.**\n\nClearing control when creating a wait does not prove that control and a wait set can never coexist. Start from the admitted two-waiter machine, complete only one response, then execute `scheduler-round`. `check-wait-set` retains the unresolved wait (`engine.cljc:2194`); `resume-from-run-queue` pops the completed entry, clears blocked/halted flags and leaves the remaining wait set (`:2305`); `register-restore` sets control without clearing those waits (`semantic.cljc:116`). The result is active control plus a nonempty wait set, with no ready queue required. Explicit resume likewise restores into the existing machine (`engine.cljc:2360`).\n\nThis is an admitted blocked carrier resumed by the kernel, even if a single-control program does not initially create two waits on its own. R0 cannot call it inconsistent merely because the new running grammar has no place for it. F1 and F2 require a task-state product (current activation, ordered pending waits, ordered ready outcomes), not just a mutually exclusive fourth root arm. The activation-level `:run` row can remain as proposed. Update the kind rules and distinguish root classification from activation shape. Test one-woken/one-waiting as well as two-woken cases; do not manufacture them only as negative tests.\n\n### F3 — Fuel does not bound an install child or the entire program tick\n\n**P1, architectural defect | plan:113; src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1458; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1862 | preemption at every executing task's boundary | propagate explicit execution budgets through scheduling and child execution, and admit running install children.**\n\nThe proposed `run-fuel` calls an unrestricted `scheduler-round` when the root is inactive. That round polls links and advances installs (`engine.cljc:2168`; `poll-links`/`advance-installs`); `advance-install` calls `vm/run` on the child (`:1475`). A blocked root installing a module whose initializer computes indefinitely still never returns to the holder. A one-instruction root fuel budget does not help. The assessment's “child states are unreachable unless the sweep takes fuel; not needed” conclusion is precisely the gap the new invariant makes necessary to close.\n\nDefine how a budget reaches child tasks, how it is shared or bounded per tree, and how a child remains at a materialized boundary when exhausted. Current lower admits only a blocked child (`rows.cljc:1862`); the plan neither changes that check nor supplies running-child phase acceptance. Include a long initializer, a nested initializer, and handoff/recovery while the child is running. Retain receiver admission checks for each child.\n\nFuel also counts VM instructions, not elapsed time: `:call` invokes a host function synchronously (`semantic.cljc:193`), stream construction invokes the supplied constructor (`engine.cljc:480`), and a scheduler round can scan many entries. State the cooperative guarantee precisely: interruption between completed instructions, with no hard wall-clock bound for one atomic instruction or unbounded host seam. Avoid promising a lease deadline from `n` instructions alone. No clock-to-instruction conversion belongs in the VM.\n\n### F4 — A tenure failure still loses the just-computed boundary\n\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-YHPCj8Au' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-16 16:07:42.902 xcodebuild[7199:68222486]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-16 16:07:43.322 xcodebuild[7199:68222485] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-a5oLE6Ka' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-10-16 16:07:44.089 xcodebuild[7223:68222584]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-10-16 16:07:44.360 xcodebuild[7223:68222583] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\nc6bc91f7aaa7efa4fff5694fd41384cb943b6404\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"V2 fixes the central task-state model by carrying active control, pending waits, and completed wakes together. I’m checking whether the proposed encoding and fuel path preserve that model, especially cursor updates and repeated FFI bridge execution.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '320,355p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc; sed -n '430,465p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '1450,1500p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '2160,2330p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '45,70p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '110,150p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '320,355p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc; sed -n '430,465p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '1450,1500p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '2160,2330p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '45,70p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '110,150p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"                                    (or (apply2/request-args request) []))})\n                response\n                successor\n                (apply2/request-id request))))\n          :dao.stream/blocked {:handled? false, :vm vm, :outcome o}\n          :dao.stream/gap\n          (throw (ex-info \"Gap at the FFI bridge cursor: a request was evicted and the call that made it can never be answered\"\n                          {:vm-model (:vm-model vm)}))\n          ;; :end, :cursor-mismatch, :invalid-cursor, :transport-error\n          {:handled? false,\n           :terminal o,\n           :vm (assoc-in vm [:bridge :terminal] o)})))))\n\n\n(defn maybe-run\n  \"Run a VM with bridge dispatch if explicit bridge handlers are installed.\n\n   run-fn must be the VM-specific raw runner that advances the VM until halt\n   or block without recursively calling vm/run again.\"\n  [vm run-fn]\n  (if-not (installed? vm)\n    (run-fn vm)\n    (loop [v (run-fn vm)]\n      (if (:blocked? v)\n        (let [{:keys [handled? diagnostic vm]} (bridge-step v)]\n          (cond handled? (recur (run-fn vm))\n                ;; A malformed request consumed as a diagnostic advanced the\n                ;; cursor, so there may be a real request behind it.\n                diagnostic (recur vm)\n                :else vm))\n        v))))\n   terminal-resume-outcome reads it.\n\n   A woken status the immediate `handle-put`/`handle-next` answers as the\n   shaped refusal -- `:dao.stream/refused`, or an outcome outside this\n   contract version -- is shaped into that same refusal value, so whether\n   the first attempt blocked does not change what the program sees. The\n   declared error outcomes stay raw, for terminal-resume-outcome to raise.\n\n   The ready entry stays pure data: any `:stream` handle a poll resolved\n   is dropped -- the resource updates carry the successor cursor, and\n   handles are re-resolved from ids when needed again -- so an entry\n   neither waits nor runs holding a host object.\"\n  [state woken]\n  (mapv (fn [{:keys [entry value cursor status], :as woken-entry}]\n          (let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)\n                value (if (wake-refusal? (:reason entry) status)\n                        (cond-> {:status :refused\n                                 :outcome status\n                                 :stream-id (:stream-id entry)}\n                          cursor-ref (assoc :cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)))\n                        value)\n                updates\n                (or (:resource-updates woken-entry)\n                    (when (and cursor-ref cursor)\n                      (let [cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)\n                            cursor-data (get (:resources state) cursor-id)]\n                        {cursor-id (assoc cursor-data :cursor cursor)})))]\n            (dissoc (assoc entry\n                           :value value\n                           :resource-updates updates\n                           :status status\n                           :cursor cursor)\n                    :stream)))\n        woken))\n\n\n                                   (receive-module s m (dissoc dep :bindings))))\n                               received\n                               (linked-entries (:modules vm)))]\n                (-> received\n                    (update :installs dissoc module-name)\n                    (wake-installed module-name module-name nil))))))))))\n\n\n(defn- advance-install\n  \"Step the install child of `module-name` in this round, beside every\n   other task (section 7.3): `running` runs it until it halts or parks.\n   A parked child stays `parked`; a halted one goes on to `validated`; an\n   error refuses the install. An error while publishing -- attaching an\n   origin image, lowering the slice or a store snapshot, or receiving a\n   dependency -- refuses it too, at phase `:linked`, from the state the\n   round started with: nothing half-published survives, every waiter is\n   restored with the refusal, and no throw leaves the round.\n\n   Under a custody gate the child is stamped with the root's mode before\n   it runs; in `:exporting` and `:ended` it does not run.\"\n  [state module-name]\n  (let [inst (get-in state [:installs module-name])\n        mode (vm/gate-mode state)\n        [child refusal]\n        (when-not (contains? #{:exporting :ended} mode)\n          (try [(vm/run (stamp-gate (:vm inst) mode)) nil]\n               (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n                 [nil (error-refusal module-name e)])))]\n    (cond\n      (and (nil? child) (nil? refusal)) state\n      refusal (refuse-install state module-name refusal)\n      (:blocked? child)\n      (assoc-in state [:installs module-name]\n                (assoc inst :vm child :phase :parked))\n      (:halted? child)\n      (try (link-install state module-name (assoc inst :vm child))\n           (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n             (refuse-install state module-name\n                             (assoc (error-refusal module-name e)\n                                    :phase :linked))))\n      :else (assoc-in state [:installs module-name]\n                      (assoc inst :vm child :phase :running)))))\n\n\n(defn- advance-installs\n  [state]\n  (reduce advance-install state (sort-by str (keys (:installs state)))))\n\n\n(defn- start-install\n  \"`loading`: spawn the child, or refuse on a loader defect. Returns\n\n   A gated machine (any `:yin.k/gate`) observes nothing here: no link\n   retry or scan, no FFI response read, no put or next retry. Its install\n   children are stamped and stepped in `:running` only.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n    (if (and (empty? wait-set) (empty? (:installs state)))\n      state\n      (let [state (poll-links state\n                              (filterv link-entry? wait-set)\n                              (remove link-entry? wait-set))\n            wait-set (:wait-set state)\n            gated? (some? (vm/gate-mode state))\n            state (if gated?\n                    state\n                    (poll-ffi-responses state\n                                        (filterv ffi-response-entry? wait-set)\n                                        (remove ffi-response-entry?\n                                                wait-set)))\n            wait-set (:wait-set state)\n            engine-polled? (some-fn link-entry?\n                                    ffi-response-entry?\n                                    observe-entry?\n                                    (fn [e]\n                                      (and gated?\n                                           (contains? #{:put :next}\n                                                      (:reason e)))))\n            streams (filterv (complement engine-polled?) wait-set)\n            {:keys [woken store], :as result}\n            (waitset/check {:waiting streams}\n                           waitset-resolver\n                           (:resources state))\n            v (assoc state\n                     :resources store\n                     :wait-set (into (:waiting (:waitset result))\n                                     (filter engine-polled?)\n                                     wait-set))]\n        (update v\n                :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n                (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken))))))\n\n\n(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n\n\n(defn run-loop\n  \"Generic eval loop with scheduler support. Ready entries are pure data;\n   `resume-fn` pops and restores them.\"\n  [state active? step-fn resume-fn]\n  (loop [v state]\n    (let [q (or (:ready-queue v) [])]\n      (cond (active? v) (recur (step-fn v))\n            (:blocked? v) (let [v' (check-wait-set v)]\n                            (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v')]\n                              (recur resumed)\n                              (telemetry/emit-snapshot v' :blocked)))\n            (seq q) (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v)]\n                      (recur resumed)\n                      v)\n            :else (if (:halted? v) (telemetry/emit-snapshot v :halt) v)))))\n\n\n(def ^:private waitset-diagnostics\n  \"The statuses `dao.stream.waitset/check` wakes an entry it could not poll\n   at all with: an unsupported reason, an unresolvable entry, or an answer —\n   or transport — it could not interpret. No park site in `yin.vm` produces\n   an entry that earns one, and an entry that earns one must not reach a\n   restore function as a value.\"\n  #{:dao.stream.waitset/unsupported-reason\n    :dao.stream.waitset/unresolved\n    :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer})\n\n\n(defn- terminal-resume-outcome\n  \"The outcome a woken entry resolved with, when it is one the immediate\n   path raises as an error. `handle-put` and `handle-next` raise exactly\n   the declared error outcomes of their operation, and whether the first\n   attempt blocked must not change that. An outcome outside this contract\n   version is the shaped refusal on the immediate path, so it is the\n   shaped refusal here too, never a raise. A waitset diagnostic is\n   terminal whatever the entry's `:reason` -- an unsupported reason is by\n   definition outside the `#{:next :put}` this check otherwise reads --\n   so it is named here rather than falling through to the restore. An\n   entry that was never polled carries no status.\"\n  [entry]\n  (let [status (:status entry)]\n    (cond\n      (contains? waitset-diagnostics status) status\n      (= :link-refused status) status\n      (contains? ffi/response-loss-statuses status) status\n      (contains? #{:next :put} (:reason entry))\n      (when (contains? (get wake-error-outcomes (:reason entry)) status)\n        status))))\n\n\n(defn- throw-terminal-resume!\n  \"Fail a resumed entry exactly as the immediate operation would have. A\n   waitset diagnostic names its status and the entry that earned it: there\n   is no stream operation whose error vocabulary it belongs to.\"\n  [entry o]\n  (cond\n    (contains? waitset-diagnostics o)\n    (fail \"Wait-set entry woke with a diagnostic\" {:status o, :entry entry})\n    ;; a refused or lost link, or a refused install: the effect's error\n    (= :link-refused o)\n    (fail (str \"Module link refused: \"\n               (clojure.core/name (or (:reason (:refusal entry)) :refused)))\n          (assoc (:refusal entry) :link-module (:name entry)))\n    ;; an FFI response stream that can no longer answer this call\n    (contains? ffi/response-loss-statuses o)\n    (ffi/throw-response-lost! (ffi/response-call-id entry) o)\n    (= :next (:reason entry))\n    (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n          {:outcome o,\n           :stream-id (:stream-id entry),\n           :cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))})\n    :else (fail \"Stream append failed\"\n                {:outcome o, :stream-id (:stream-id entry)})))\n\n\n(defn resume-from-run-queue\n  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge its resource updates into\n   the private `:resources` table, and restore VM-specific context.\n\n   Restoration is dispatched here rather than carried on the entry, so a\n   ready entry holds registers, ids, and values — never a closure. The\n   terminal-outcome check runs before the restore: a woken retry that ended\n   in an outcome the immediate operation raises as an error must fail the\n   same way here, not reach Yin code as a value.\n\n   `restore-fn` is called with three arguments, `base entry val`, where\n   `val` is the woken `:value` — the same signature `resume-continuation`\n   uses, so one restore serves both engine call sites\n   (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 2).\"\n  [state restore-fn]\n  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n    (when (seq run-queue)\n      (let [entry (first run-queue)\n            rest-queue (subvec run-queue 1)\n            updates (:resource-updates entry)\n            ;; a woken entry only ever carries engine-minted keyword keys\n            ;; (cursor ids)\n            _ (when-let [k (some #(when-not (keyword? %) %) (keys updates))]\n                (fail \"Ready entry resource update carries a non-minted key\"\n                      {:rule :resource-update-key, :key k}))\n            base (assoc state\n                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n                        :resources (merge (:resources state) updates)\n                        :blocked? false\n                        :halted? false)]\n        (if-let [terminal (terminal-resume-outcome entry)]\n          (throw-terminal-resume! entry terminal)\n          (restore-fn base entry (:value entry)))))))\n\n\n(defn scheduler-round\n  \"One round between continuations: poll the wait set, then resume whatever\n   woke, through `restore-fn` (`base entry val -> state`, the one restore\n   signature `yin.vm.engine.md` §2.1 states). Returns the polled state when\n   nothing woke, so a caller always gets a state back.\n\n   The restore operation is a composition parameter\n   (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 1); every VM was writing this composition\n   by hand.\"\n  [state restore-fn]\n  (let [v' (check-wait-set state)]\n    (or (resume-from-run-queue v' restore-fn) v')))\n\n\n(defn park-id\n  \"The local park id the next `park-continuation` mints: `:parked-N` from\n   code-aliases   ; {address segment-id}; additive, one address one id (UCF §7.3.4)\n   gc])           ; {:since :base :threshold :pinned}: heap reclamation\n\n\n\n(defn put-registers\n  \"Materialize control, window, environment and continuation; value is exit-only.\"\n  [machine seg pc W E K]\n  (assoc machine :control (when seg {:segment seg :pc pc})\n         :window W :env E :k (when (seq K) K)\n         :halted? (and (not (:blocked? machine)) (nil? seg))))\n\n\n(defn- return-to\n  \"The one popped-frame delivery: retain exactly the saved window and write rd.\"\n  [frame value]\n  (assoc frame :window (assoc (:window frame) (:rd frame) value)))\n\n\n(defn- act-payload\n  [seg pc E W K rd facts]\n  {:segment seg :pc (inc pc) :env E\n   :window (select-keys W (analysis/saved facts (inc pc) rd))\n   :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd rd}})\n\n\n       (assoc base :wait-set (conj (vec (:wait-set base)) wait)\n              :control nil :k nil :value :yin/blocked :blocked? true :halted? false))\n     (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n           base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n           value (if call-id (ffi/call-result value call-id) value)]\n       (case (get-in entry [:deliver :deliver])\n         :rd (put-registers base (:segment entry) (:pc entry)\n                            (assoc (:window entry) (get-in entry [:deliver :rd]) value)\n                            (:env entry) (:k entry))\n         :return (if-let [frame (peek (:k entry))]\n                   (put-registers base (:segment frame) (:pc frame)\n                                  (:window (return-to frame value))\n                                  (:env frame) (pop (:k entry)))\n                   (assoc (put-registers base nil nil {}\n                                         (engine/without-store-of (:env base)) nil)\n                          :value value))\n         (throw (ex-info \"Entry carries no delivery record\" {:rule :deliver :entry entry})))))))\n\n\n(defn- scheduler-round\n  [machine]\n  (engine/scheduler-round machine register-restore))\n\n\n(defn vm-hot\n  \"Execute fuel instructions with the activation in locals; nil fuel runs to exit.\"\n  [machine fuel]\n  (if (and (nil? (:control machine)) (nil? (:k machine)))\n    (scheduler-round machine)\n    (let [{:keys [segment pc]} (:control machine)]\n      (loop [machine machine seg segment pc pc W (:window machine)\n             E (:env machine) K (or (:k machine) []) fuel fuel]\n        (if (and fuel (zero? fuel))\n          (put-registers machine seg pc W E K)\n          (let [image (get (:code machine) seg)\n                inst (nth (:vector image) pc)\n                op (nth inst 0)\n                remaining (when fuel (dec fuel))]\n            (case op\n              :const (recur machine seg (inc pc) (assoc W (nth inst 1) (nth inst 2)) E K remaining)\n              :var (recur machine seg (inc pc)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1320,1430p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1490,1535p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1640,1710p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; rg -n 'cell\"'!|cursor-cells|check-op-id|check-pendings|check-install|check-custody|restore-task'\"' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1320,1430p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1490,1535p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1640,1710p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; rg -n 'cell\"'!|cursor-cells|check-op-id|check-pendings|check-install|check-custody|restore-task'\"' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"                      {:seen #{} :ops {}}\n                      (task-ops ctx task :root []))]\n      (cond-> (merge (select-keys c [:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/origin :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq])\n                     {:yin.k/kind kind :yin.k/ops (:ops ops)})\n        fork? (assoc :yin.k/fork true)))))\n\n\n(defn pack-address\n  \"The storage identity of a carrier: the BLAKE3 segment address of its\n   canonical bytes, distinct from the semantic root id it carries (§3.3).\"\n  [wire]\n  (jing/segment-key wire))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Task transport (continuation design §7): lift-task and lower-task\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; The lift walks a quiescent semantic machine into the abstract\n;; task the codec above emits: waits, parked records, the store, module\n;; stores, the free environment, the result and install children, every\n;; value classified as [:lit x], [:row n] or [:cursor-cell source]. A\n;; cursor cell's source is the resource id of a cursor reference, or\n;; [response-resource position] for a link wait's kept response cursor,\n;; so one resource reached twice is one cursor cell. The lower decodes the\n;; carrier (gate to frames, §3.3), checks pendings, dependencies and\n;; custody for every task of the tree, and only then attaches streams and\n;; restores state. Restoration delivers nothing, runs nothing.\n\n(defn- outcome!\n  [status kind data]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Task transport refused\"\n                  (merge data (cond-> {:yin.k/status status}\n                                (some? kind) (assoc :yin.k/kind kind))))))\n\n\n(defn- unliftable-hold\n  \"The first hold of m with no wire pending (UCF 7.4.1, 7.4.3), or nil.\"\n  [m]\n  (let [waits (:wait-set m)\n        cells (filter map? (vals (:resources m)))\n        held? #(and (map? %) (contains? % :yin.k/held))]\n    (cond\n      (some #(= :observe (:reason %)) waits) :observe\n      (or (some held? waits) (some held? cells)) :held\n      (some :yin.k/unminted cells) :unminted-cursor\n      (seq (:yin.k/closes m)) :pending-close\n      (some #(and (= :link-request (:reason %)) (not (contains? % :cursor))) waits)\n      :link-cursor-not-installed\n      :else nil)))\n\n\n(defn- stream-row!\n  \"The :stream row of resource sid, served once per resource: through\n   :serve-keyed with [task-path sid] when the caller supplied it.\"\n  [ctx sid]\n  (or (get-in @(:acc ctx) [:streams sid])\n      (let [h (get (:resources (:m ctx)) sid)\n            keyed (:serve-keyed ctx)]\n        (when (nil? h)\n          (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :forged-resource-reference {:yin.k/hint sid}))\n        (let [marker (remote/lift-marker (if keyed #(keyed [(:path ctx) sid] %) (:serve! ctx)) h)]\n          (when (nil? marker) (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {}))\n          (let [n (node :stream [(:dao.stream/identity marker) (:dao.stream/descriptor marker)])]\n            (swap! (:acc ctx) assoc-in [:streams sid] n)\n            n)))))\n\n\n(defn- cell!\n  [ctx source sid position]\n  (when-not (contains? (:cells @(:acc ctx)) source)\n    (let [stream (stream-row! ctx sid)]\n      (swap! (:acc ctx) assoc-in [:cells source] (node :cursor-cell-body [stream position]))))\n  source)\n\n\n(defn- cell-for!\n  [ctx cid]\n  (let [cell (get (:resources (:m ctx)) cid)]\n    (when-not (and (map? cell) (contains? cell :stream-id))\n      (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :forged-resource-reference {:yin.k/hint cid}))\n    (cell! ctx cid (:stream-id cell) (:cursor cell))))\n\n\n(defn- address!\n  [ctx seg]\n  (if-some [a (get-in (:m ctx) [:code seg :address])]\n    (do (swap! (:acc ctx) update :segments conj a) a)\n    (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment {:yin.k/hint seg})))\n\n\n(defn- scalar-literal?\n  [x]\n  (or (nil? x) (boolean? x) (string? x) (keyword? x) (symbol? x) (number? x)\n      (and (not (cbor/byte-payload? x)) (cbor/numeric? x))))\n\n\n(defn- primitive-name\n  [ctx f]\n  (let [m (:m ctx)\n        n (vm/name-of (:primitives m) (or (:primitive-canonical-names m) {}) f)\n        profile (when (symbol? n) (vm/profile-of (:primitives m) n))]\n    (cond\n      (nil? n) (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :unnamed-function {})\n      (not (symbol? n)) (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-primitive {})\n      (= :host (:yin.k/class profile)) (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :host-state-primitive {:yin.k/name n})\n      :else n)))\n\n\n(declare lift-value state-node)\n\n        :stream-ref (if (engine/authentic-ref? m :stream-ref x)\n                      [:row (stream-row! ctx (:id x))]\n                      (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :forged-resource-reference {:yin.k/hint :stream-ref}))\n        :cursor-ref (if (engine/authentic-ref? m :cursor-ref x)\n                      [:cursor-cell (cell-for! ctx (:id x))]\n                      (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :forged-resource-reference {:yin.k/hint :cursor-ref}))\n        :parked-continuation (if (contains? (:parked m) (:id x))\n                               [:row (node :parked-ref [(:id x)])]\n                               (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state {:yin.k/hint :parked-reference}))\n        :cell-ref (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :cell {:yin.k/hint (:id x)})\n        (collection-value ctx :map (mapcat identity x) x))\n      (vector? x) (collection-value ctx :vec x x)\n      (set? x) (collection-value ctx :set (vec x) x)\n      (seq? x) (collection-value ctx :list (vec x) x)\n      (scalar-literal? x) [:lit x]\n      :else (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :host-object {}))))\n\n\n(defn- window-node\n  [ctx w]\n  (node :window (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [r (lift-value ctx v)]) w)))\n\n\n(defn- k-node\n  [ctx k]\n  (node :k (mapv (fn [f]\n                   (let [a (address! ctx (:segment f))]\n                     (context! ctx a (:pc f) (:env f))\n                     (node :kframe [a (:pc f) (lift-env-node ctx (:env f))\n                                    (window-node ctx (:window f)) (:rd f)])))\n                 (or k []))))\n\n\n(defn- state-node\n  \"An evaluator payload (wait entry, parked record, reified payload) as\n   its :act or :tail row; engine fields are the pending's.\"\n  [ctx p]\n  (if (contains? p :site)\n    ;; a tail state resumes through its K: its own segment never runs again\n    (node :tail [(address! ctx (:segment p)) (:site p) (k-node ctx (:k p))])\n    (let [a (address! ctx (:segment p))]\n      (context! ctx a (:pc p) (:env p))\n      (node :act [a (:pc p) (lift-env-node ctx (:env p))\n                  (window-node ctx (:window p)) (k-node ctx (:k p)) (get-in p [:deliver :rd])]))))\n\n\n                                          (when (nil? profile)\n                                            (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :host-export-profile {:yin.k/name sym}))\n                                          (swap! pins assoc-in [:yin.k/host-exports sym] profile)))\n                                      true))))))))\n        scanned (into {} (map (fn [[a v]] [a (scan/free-occurrences v)])) code)\n        missing (into #{}\n                      (mapcat (fn [{:keys [a at env]}]\n                                (let [live (live-pcs (get code a) at)]\n                                  (keep (fn [o]\n                                          (when (and (contains? live (:at o))\n                                                     (not (answered? (:name o) env)))\n                                            (:name o)))\n                                        (get scanned a)))))\n                      (:contexts @(:acc ctx)))]\n    (when (seq missing)\n      (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/discovery :incomplete :yin.k/missing {:obligations missing}}))\n    @pins))\n\n\n(declare lift-task*)\n\n\n(defn- install-node\n  [ctx mn inst opts]\n  (let [{:keys [phase parent response]} inst\n        response (into {} (remove (fn [[k _]]\n                                    (and (keyword? k) (= \"yin.admit\" (namespace k)))))\n                       response)]\n    (when-not (contains? #{:running :parked} phase)\n      (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :incomplete-install {:yin.k/name mn :yin.k/phase phase}))\n    (when-not (try (cbor/encode response) true\n                   (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _e false))\n      (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :install-response {:yin.k/name mn}))\n    (node :install [mn phase parent response\n                    (lift-task* (:vm inst) (:serve! ctx)\n                                (assoc (dissoc opts :free-env) :path (conj (:path ctx) mn)) :child)])))\n\n\n(defn- lift-task*\n  [m serve! opts role]\n  (when (seq (:ready-queue m))\n    (outcome! :yin.k/not-quiescent nil {:yin.k/ready (count (:ready-queue m))}))\n  (when-some [h (unliftable-hold m)]\n    (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch {:yin.k/hold h}))\n  (when (some? (:control m))\n    (outcome! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc (get-in m [:control :pc])}))\n  (let [value (:value m)\n        active? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? (:parked m) (:id value)))\n        kind (cond active? :parked\n                   (seq (:wait-set m)) :blocked\n                   (engine/halted-with-empty-queue? m) :halted\n                   :else (outcome! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {}))\n        ;; §6.3: the explicit argument overrides the kernel's retained field\n        free-env (cond (some? (:free-env opts)) (:free-env opts)\n                       (some? (:free-env m)) (:free-env m)\n                       :else (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :missing-free-env {}))\n        ctx {:m m :serve! serve! :serve-keyed (:serve-keyed opts) :path (or (:path opts) [])\n             :acc (atom {:streams {} :cells {} :stores #{} :segments #{} :contexts #{}})}\n        free-node (bindings-node ctx free-env)\n        waits (mapv (fn [e] (node :wait [(state-node ctx e) (pending-node ctx e)])) (:wait-set m))\n        parked (node :parked-table (mapcat (fn [[p rec]] [p (state-node ctx rec)]) (:parked m)))\n        store (store-node ctx (:store m))\n        result (when (= :halted kind) (lift-value ctx value))\n        installs (mapv (fn [[mn inst]] (install-node ctx mn inst opts)) (sort-by #(str (key %)) (:installs m)))\n        module-stores (module-store-nodes ctx)\n        acc @(:acc ctx)\n        segments (:segments acc)\n        code (into {} (map (fn [a]\n                             (if-some [v (get-in m [:code (get (:code-aliases m) a) :vector])]\n                               [a v]\n                               (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/segment a}))))\n9:   A task's :cursor-cells row holds alternating source identities and :cursor-cell-body\n66:   :cursor-cells [:pairs :C [:ref :cursor-cell-body]] :cursor-cell-body [[:ref :stream] :D]\n68:          [:refs :module-store] [:ref :parked-table] [:ref :cursor-cells]\n165:    (= kind :cursor-cells) (vec (mapcat identity (sort-by #(census-order (first %)) (partition 2 slots))))\n176:  {:rows 10000 :cursor-cells 64 :depth 256 :work 4194304 :bytes 16777216})\n241:;; :unresolved-cursor-cells.\n397:            (when-not (or (= :cursor-cells (:kind n)) (child-task? ctx n))\n468:      (or (= :cursor-cells kind) (child-task? ctx n)) nil\n564:                     :cursor-cells (vec (sort-by str (distinct (mapcat (fn [g] (mapcat #(unnumbered-cells run (:kind g) %) (:elems g)))\n623:      (= :cursor-cells kind) [:cursor-cells]\n652:   :cursor-cells}} when a tie group fails the tie test. The census's pair\n659:    (spend! counter budget :cursor-cells (quot (count census) 2))\n685:                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable :yin.k/kind :unresolved-cursor-cells\n686:                   :yin.k/path (into prefix (:path refused)) :yin.k/cursor-cells (:cursor-cells refused)})))\n692:   Refuses :yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells when tied members of\n746:    (and (= :cursor-cells kind) (= :C d)) (and (keyword? x) (some? (re-matches census-key (name x))))\n806:            [n path cursor-cells reached]\n817:                    (= :C d) (do (when-not (contains? cursor-cells x) (bad! :missing-cursor-cell p)) (swap! reached conj x))\n819:                    (and (ref? d) (not= :cursor-cells (:kind x))) (visit x p cursor-cells reached)\n821:                                (visit child (conj p j) cursor-cells reached))\n826:                        :cursor-cell (do (when-not (contains? cursor-cells (second x)) (bad! :missing-cursor-cell p))\n828:                        :row (visit (second x) p cursor-cells reached)))\n833:                  cursor-cells (into {} (map vec) (partition 2 (:slots census))) reached (atom #{})]\n834:              (visit task path cursor-cells reached)\n835:              (when-not (= (set (keys cursor-cells)) @reached) (bad! :extra-cursor-cell (conj path 9)))))]\n946:         (when (contains? #{:map :bindings :store :parked-table :window :cursor-cells :set} kind)\n969:                                             (= :unresolved-cursor-cells (:yin.k/kind d)))\n976:             (bad! :noncanonical-cursor-cells [root]))\n1243:(defn- check-op-id!\n1246:   check-op-id!, verbatim).\"\n1290:                            (check-op-id! ctx op-id p)\n1388:(defn- cell!\n1401:    (cell! ctx cid (:stream-id cell) (:cursor cell))))\n1555:      (let [c (cell! ctx [(:response e) (:cursor e)] (:response e) (:cursor e))\n1721:                          (node :cursor-cells (mapcat identity (:cells acc))) requires code installs waits\n1860:(defn- check-install!\n1894:(defn- check-pendings!\n1911:    (run! #(check-install! recv %) installs)))\n2071:(defn- check-custody!\n2151:(defn- restore-task\n2154:   custody check-custody! answered (with-custody).\"\n2286:                                                            :vm (restore-task spawned child handles custody false)})]))\n2324:   custody (check-custody!) and whole-tree module/profile pins plus install\n2349:     (doseq [step [check-stores! check-pendings! check-dependencies!]\n2352:     (let [custody (check-custody! task wire opts)]\n2354:       (restore-task recv task (attach-streams! attach! task) custody true)))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
