{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'ready-queue|wake-installed|wake-link|poll-links|poll-ffi' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '1990,2157p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '47,66p' review-inputs/1792135181000-architect-6d-plan-second-review.report.md; sed -n '2071,2091p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '2250,2266p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"246:  \"Returns true if the VM has halted and its ready-queue is empty.\"\n248:  (and (boolean (:halted? vm)) (empty? (or (:ready-queue vm) []))))\n278:         (empty? (or (:ready-queue vm) []))\n426:  \"Transform woken wait-set entries into ready-queue entries.\n737:          (update :ready-queue\n1327:(defn- wake-installed\n1336:        (update :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n1351:      (wake-installed module-name nil refusal)))\n1455:                    (wake-installed module-name module-name nil))))))))))\n1627:(defn- poll-links\n1642:        (update :ready-queue (fnil into []) woken)\n1716:        (update :ready-queue (fnil into []) (when woken [woken])))))\n1733:          (update :ready-queue (fnil conj [])\n1835:   the readers `waiting` (`woken` so far), as `poll-ffi-cell` applies one\n1869:(defn- poll-ffi-cell\n1904:(defn- poll-ffi-responses\n1914:                        (:ready-queue state))\n1918:                    (let [[state w k] (poll-ffi-cell\n1931:          (update :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n1964:        (update :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n2085:   as `poll-ffi-cell` applies one read. The response wakes the reader\n2109:        pending (into #{} (keep ffi/request-call-id) (:ready-queue state))\n2121:        (update :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n2158:   sweep (`poll-ffi-responses`): a response wakes the reader whose call\n2168:      (let [state (poll-links state\n2175:                    (poll-ffi-responses state\n2198:                :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n2210:    (let [q (or (:ready-queue v) [])]\n2281:  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge its resource updates into\n2295:  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n2306:                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n2534:   `:wait-set` and `:ready-queue` as a kernel position, the store and\n2545:                 (into (:ready-queue vm))),\n2774:        ready-versions (collect-frame-versions (:ready-queue vm))\n                          (woken-answer entry :ok (:datom entry) nil)\n                          (woken-answer entry o o nil))]))))\n\n\n(defn apply-next\n  \"Apply the driver's read `outcome` to the gated parked `:next` entry\n   `entry`, as the ungated sweep applies a retry: `blocked` keeps\n   waiting; `ok` and `gap` advance the cursor cell to the outcome's\n   position and wake it; `end` and every other outcome wake it under\n   their own status. Performs no stream call. Refused as `apply-put` is,\n   for an FFI response reader (`apply-ffi-read`), and for an entry whose\n   cursor cell is still unminted (the driver mints it first).\"\n  [state entry outcome]\n  (require-running! state {})\n  (let [i (wait-slot state entry\n                     #(and (= :next (:reason %))\n                           (map? (:cursor-ref %))\n                           (nil? (ffi/response-call-id %)))\n                     :next)\n        _ (refuse-unminted! state entry)\n        o (:dao.stream/outcome outcome)\n        cursor (:dao.stream/cursor outcome)\n        cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))\n        advance #(assoc-in state [:resources cursor-id :cursor] cursor)]\n    (cond\n      (invalid-answer? :next outcome)\n      (wake-slot state i [{:entry entry,\n                           :status :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer}])\n      (= :dao.stream/blocked o) state\n      (= :dao.stream/ok o)\n      (wake-slot (advance) i\n                 [(woken-answer entry :ok (:dao.stream/value outcome) cursor)])\n      (= :dao.stream/end o)\n      (wake-slot state i [(woken-answer entry :end nil nil)])\n      (= :dao.stream/gap o)\n      (wake-slot (advance) i\n                 [(woken-answer entry :dao.stream/gap :dao.stream/gap\n                                cursor)])\n      :else (wake-slot state i [(woken-answer entry o o nil)]))))\n\n\n(defn- ffi-request-slot\n  \"The wait-set index of the retained FFI request of call `call-id`;\n  refuses an ungated task, the closed gates, and an id no retained\n  request holds.\"\n  [state call-id]\n  (require-running! state {:call-id call-id})\n  (or (first (keep-indexed\n               (fn [n e]\n                 (when (and (= :put (:reason e))\n                            (some? (ffi/request-call-id e))\n                            (= call-id (ffi/request-call-id e)))\n                   n))\n               (:wait-set state)))\n      (fail \"No retained FFI request\" {:call-id call-id})))\n\n\n(defn apply-ffi-sent\n  \"Apply the driver's send of the retained FFI request of call `call-id`:\n   the request writer leaves the wait set and is queued woken by `ok`,\n   so the kernel restores it as the call's response reader, exactly as\n   the ungated sweep's successful retry does. Performs no stream call.\n   Refused as `apply-put` is, and for an id no retained request holds: a\n   second transition finds none.\"\n  [state call-id]\n  (let [i (ffi-request-slot state call-id)\n        entry (get-in state [:wait-set i])]\n    (wake-slot state i [(woken-answer entry :ok (:datom entry) nil)])))\n\n\n(defn apply-ffi-outcome\n  \"Apply the driver's whole append `outcome` for the retained FFI request\n   of call `call-id`, as `apply-put` applies a program put's: `ok` is the\n   sent transition (`apply-ffi-sent`), `full` keeps the request waiting\n   for a retry, and every terminal outcome -- and any answer the append\n   contract cannot vouch for -- wakes the writer under its own status,\n   the ungated sweep's disposition for a put whose append answered\n   terminally. Performs no stream call. Refused as `apply-ffi-sent` is.\"\n  [state call-id outcome]\n  (let [i (ffi-request-slot state call-id)\n        entry (get-in state [:wait-set i])\n        o (:dao.stream/outcome outcome)]\n    (cond\n      (invalid-answer? :append! outcome)\n      (wake-slot state i [{:entry entry,\n                           :status :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer}])\n      (= :dao.stream/full o) state\n      (= :dao.stream/ok o)\n      (wake-slot state i [(woken-answer entry :ok (:datom entry) nil)])\n      :else (wake-slot state i [(woken-answer entry o o nil)]))))\n\n\n(defn apply-ffi-read\n  \"Apply one read `outcome` of the call-out cell to the gated FFI\n   response readers of the cell that call `call-id`'s reader waits on,\n   as `poll-ffi-cell` applies one read. The response wakes the reader\n   whose call id it names -- `call-id`'s or another waiter's on the\n   shared cell -- and the cell advances; a response no live reader owns\n   is discarded with a diagnostic and the cell advances; one belonging\n   to a woken request writer not yet a reader is not consumed; `blocked`\n   keeps waiting; `end` and `gap` wake every reader of the cell as the\n   router does, and any other outcome wakes them under its own status.\n   Performs no stream call. Refused as `apply-put` is, and for a\n   `call-id` no response reader holds.\"\n  [state call-id outcome]\n  (require-running! state {:call-id call-id})\n  (let [reader? #(and (some? (ffi/response-call-id %))\n                      (map? (:cursor-ref %)))\n        target (or (first (filter #(and (reader? %)\n                                        (= call-id (ffi/response-call-id %)))\n                                  (:wait-set state)))\n                   (fail \"No FFI response reader\" {:call-id call-id}))\n        cell-key (:id (:cursor-ref target))\n        cell (get (:resources state) cell-key)\n        _ (when-not (map? cell)\n            (fail \"No response cell\" {:call-id call-id}))\n        readers (filterv #(and (reader? %)\n                               (= cell-key (:id (:cursor-ref %))))\n                         (:wait-set state))\n        pending (into #{} (keep ffi/request-call-id) (:ready-queue state))\n        r (if (invalid-answer? :next outcome)\n            {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer}\n            outcome)\n        [_ state' cursor waiting woken]\n        (ffi-read-step state cell-key (:cursor cell) readers [] pending r)\n        gone (remove (fn [e] (some #(identical? e %) waiting)) readers)]\n    (-> state'\n        (assoc-in [:resources cell-key :cursor] cursor)\n        (assoc :wait-set\n               (filterv (fn [e] (not-any? #(identical? e %) gone))\n                        (:wait-set state')))\n        (update :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n                (make-woken-run-queue-entries state' woken)))))\n\n\n(defn check-wait-set\n  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n\n   The sweep is `dao.stream.waitset/check` — this engine was that library's\n   seed and is now its first consumer. Every entry is resolved to a live\n   handle and an opaque cursor out of the store by `waitset-resolver` and\n   polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution happens per\n   poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle. There is no\n   transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only mechanism.\n\n   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values —\n   the value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next —\n   instead of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll\n   cursor. An entry that stays waiting is retained in its stored,\n   resource-id form.\n\n   Each woken result's `:status` is stamped onto its ready entry, where\n   `terminal-resume-outcome` reads it: an outcome the immediate operation\n   raises as an error fails the same way when the resume pops it — and a\n   waitset diagnostic, an entry the sweep could not poll at all, raises\n   there too, before any continuation is restored.\n\n   The link wait entries `require` adds (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.2) are\n   polled first, by the engine rather than the stream sweep: a\n   `:link-request` retries its envelope, a `:link-response` advances its\n   own kept cursor to the response carrying its id, and an `:install`\n   waits on its install child. The install children are then stepped in\n   this same round (section 7.3), so a child that halts restores its\n   waiters here; nothing about a child runs inside a restore.\n\n   The FFI response readers are routed next, also outside the stream\n\n**P1, plan/implementation gap | plan:115; src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:2118; :2248 | explicit state retention and exclusive authority | return the new boundary as fenced data on tenure loss, and specify which authority may adopt it.**\n\nAfter `m1` is computed, `require-tenure!` at `driver.cljc:2123` may throw before `m1` is associated with holder state (`:2139`). The catch at `:2253` calls `end-run` on the original argument, not on `m1`. Fuel changes neither this control flow nor the later throws inside replay/emit/read. When tenure dies between completed ticks, the next entry check also ends the run rather than making a successor. `exit-mint` itself requires tenure (`:1144`).\n\nThe plan's sentence that a holder can hand off *before* the bound is useful composition policy, but it does not resolve its claim about tenure dying between slices. Specify separate outcomes for successful preemption while tenure is live, expired-tenure retention, and recovery from the last durable checkpoint. Retain the newest completed pure state before a post-slice check can discard it. An expired holder must not offer a valid successor or resume merely because it still owns bytes: adoption needs authority consistent with the ledger's existing origin/completion rules. If expiry intentionally replays the last committed checkpoint, say that progress since it is lost and do not call the invariant fully repaired by fuel.\n\nAdd clock-controlled cuts immediately before the slice, immediately after it, during the later driver stages, and between slices. The source must remain fenced, with either a durably authorized successor or an explicitly documented recovery checkpoint. Keeping automatic export a composition decision is sound; leaving the expiry semantics unspecified is not.\n\n### F5 — The all-PC stack equation is false with today's layout\n\n**P1 for 6e, specification defect also blocking 6d-a item 7 | plan:322; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc:118; src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/code.cljc:349 | every-PC reconstruction | derive layouts at control-flow and terminator PCs, not just expression nodes.**\n\n`stack-layouts` walks `(:expr body)` and its `:children`, assigning only each node's `:pc`. The parser stores a conditional's jump in `:jump`, not as a child (`code.cljc:389`), and excludes the body terminator from the expression (`:413`). Thus the purported existing layout is not defined at every PC.\n\nA concrete structured conditional is:\n\n```clojure\n[[:const 1 true]             ; 0: test\n [:branch-false 1 4]         ; 1\n(defn- check-custody!\n  \"Step 10, once for the tree after the three staged checks: the custody\n   grammar (custody-baseline), then by the root's slot: a fork (nil) is\n   refused under :exclusive as :yin.k/profile-mismatch; an exclusive\n   blocked or parked root runs the grant checks unless :fenced (an\n   administrative rehydration: no grant, gate :exporting); a halted\n   exclusive root needs no grant and is gated :ended. Answers\n   {:exclusive? :fenced? :state}, which restore applies to every task.\"\n  [task wire opts]\n  (custody-baseline task)\n  (let [[_ _ kind] (:slots task) c (nth (:slots task) 16) fenced? (boolean (:fenced opts))]\n    (if (nil? c)\n      (do (when (:exclusive opts)\n            (outcome! :yin.k/profile-mismatch nil {:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive}))\n          {:exclusive? false :fenced? fenced?})\n      {:exclusive? true :fenced? fenced?\n       :state (when-not (or fenced? (= :halted kind)) (accept-grant! task c (pack-address wire) opts))})))\n\n\n(defn- with-custody\n  \"Restore's custody for one task of the tree: an exclusive tree numbers\n   a program effect -- and it ends the run.  An uncertain journal\n   append is none of these: it unwinds untouched, and `step` stalls\n   everything on it.\"\n  [state mode]\n  (try (step-active-held state mode)\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) failure\n         (if (or (::journal-uncertain (ex-data failure)) (::inbox/refusal (ex-data failure)))\n           (throw failure)\n           (if (= :lease-bound (::cause (ex-data failure)))\n             (end-run state :lease-bound {:dao.lease/lease (:lease state)})\n             (end-run state :program-failure\n                      {:failure {:message (ex-message failure)\n                                 :data (ex-data failure)}}))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The release step\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '360,410p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '798,847p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1244,1255p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '2415,2428p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '657,690p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '445,475p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '790,806p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '908,920p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '655,676p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '798,825p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1895,1917p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2836,2846p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '590,628p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md; cat docs/agents/known-gaps.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '360,410p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '798,847p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1244,1255p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '2415,2428p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '657,690p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '445,475p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '790,806p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '908,920p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '655,676p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '798,825p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1895,1917p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2836,2846p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '590,628p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md; cat docs/agents/known-gaps.md\"","aggregated_output":"- **Linked registry entries do not travel.** Their binding facts travel in\n  `requires`: `:yin.k/modules {name address-keyword-or-nil}` and\n  `:yin.k/host-exports {qualified-symbol profile-keyword}` for used host\n  exports only. Both maps are always present, including `{}`. The receiver's\n  registry stays as given and must satisfy exact address equality and each\n  used host export's profile equality before any stream attaches.\n- **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: phase 7 admits exclusive custody on rows.\n  Fork custody is nil on every task row. An exclusive blocked or parked\n  root carries policy, occurrence, arbitration and next-op-seq, with\n  origin on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries origin only.\n  Every child has nil custody and is checked in the root's context.\n\n### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n\n**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3\n   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N\n   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n```\n\nNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\nprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1).\n\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner chose nil fork custody and phase 7 enforces the slot grammar.\nThe custody slot admits only `:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/occurrence`,\n`:yin.k/arbitration`, `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and `:yin.k/origin`.\nFork roots of every kind and all children carry nil. Exclusive blocked\nor parked roots require the first four keys, with policy\n`:yin.k/exclusive`, and carry origin when present; halted exclusive\nroots carry origin only. A header-bearing halt without origin refuses.\nNo current lease, epoch or holder enters the body; the predecessor lease\ninside origin is lineage evidence.\n\n### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n   syntax).\n\n**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\noperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\ncaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\nslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n\n```\nslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\nslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\nslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n```\n\n(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\na tail `:call`:\n\n1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n5. pending from the engine fields.\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\nlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n\nhalted in. On lift, `install-node` refuses a phase outside the set as\n`:yin.k/non-portable :incomplete-install` and a response CBOR cannot\nencode as `:yin.k/non-portable :install-response`.\n\nRefusal rows: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` (`a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`,\n`:591-603`); `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a register kernel stepped one\ninstruction past a `:const`, asked to lift); `:yin.k/non-portable\n:reason-mismatch` (the `:observe` hold, `:675-692`); and the validator's\n**membership tampers**, each a row-set edit of a valid body before\nlower: a window with an extra live key → `:window-extra`; a live key\nremoved → `:window-missing`; `rd` added → `:window-self`; `rd` replaced\nby another register → `:destination`; a return frame's `pc` moved after\n[sp :yin.safepoint/use #{…}] [sp :yin.safepoint/live #{…}]`; in the\nrules replace \"`stack-effect` and `lexically-required` are derived from\nthe canonical vector alone, by the same static walk the linearizer\nperforms (§5.3). They are identical for every engine and checkable\nagainst the reference machine by a conformance test that runs the\nsegment to each safepoint and compares the observed deltas and E-reads.\nAbsolute depth, activation bases, captured environments, and physical\nlayouts are not claimed\" with \"`def`, `use`, `live` and\n`lexically-required` are derived from the canonical vector alone\n(`yin.vm.semantic.analysis`). They are identical for every\nengine and checkable against the reference machine by a conformance test\nthat runs the segment to each safepoint and compares the saved window's\nkey set with `live − {rd}` and the E-reads. Window values, environments,\n`K` and physical layouts are not claimed\"; replace \"physical slot →\n### 4.1 The safepoint table (UCF §7.4.1, rewritten)\n\nA safepoint is a transition at which the machine parks. The semantic\nkinds are unchanged; the state columns are new.\n\nEvery wait is one of the two closed states of §2.4 (`act` with\n`{:deliver :rd}` or `tail` with `{:deliver :return}`); an effectful\ntail call raises the `tail` form of whichever row its effect falls in.\n`saved` is `saved(pc+1, rd)` of §2.4.\n\n| Safepoint kind | Raised by | State | Delivery |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| blocked read | `:stream-next rd c` → `blocked` | `act`, pending cursor cell `c` | `rd` ← read value; nil on `end`; `:dao.stream/gap` on gap |\n| blocked write | `:stream-put rd s v` → `full` | `act`, pending retains `W[v]` and the stream | `rd` ← written value on retry `ok` |\n| FFI call, sent | `:ffi-call rd op args` → `ok` | `act`, pending `:ffi` (call id, response cursor cell); in-VM `:reason :next` with `:call-id` | `rd` ← correlated `ok` value; `error` raises; bookkeeping removed |\n| FFI call, retained | `:ffi-call` → `full` | `act`, pending `:ffi-request` (envelope verbatim); in-VM `:reason :put` with `:request-sent` | none at this phase; on append `ok` the entry becomes the sent phase |\n| effectful call, non-tail | `:call rd f args false` whose operator yields a blocking effect | `act`, pending per the effect kind | per the kind's row |\n| effectful call, tail | `:call rd f args true` likewise | `tail`, pending per the effect kind | **through return** against `K` |\n| link request | `:module/require` miss, request in hand | `act` or `tail`, pending `:link-request` | none at this phase; on append `ok` the entry becomes link response |\n| link response | request appended; polling for the correlated response | same state, pending `:link-response` | none at this phase; a matching `ok` makes it install; refusal raises |\n| install | the child runs | same state, pending `:install` + the body's install entry | on `linked`: the module symbol to `rd` or through return; on `refused`: raises |\n| halt | `:halt r`, or `:return r` with empty `K` | — | not resumable; a `:yin.k/result` travels |\n\nTwo cases sit outside the table: an **explicit park** (`:park rd`) is a\n**no-wait safepoint**: it is a migration safepoint like every row\nabove, but it raises no wait entry; its `act` record is the body's\nparked record and a body of kind `:parked` names it by id (UCF §7.4.3\nr5, \"explicit park is the no-wait shape\"). A **reified continuation**\n(`:current-continuation`) is the one thing here that is *not* a\nsafepoint: it is a captured value, never a task state.\nWhole-task quiescence (empty ready queue, UCF §7.4.1) and the existing\nrefusal conditions (unsupported observation states, pending close) are\nunchanged.\n\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, see below\n```\n\n(Root replaced 2026-10-11 by continuation §2.6: `free-env-cref` added,\n`contract` is the one profile map, `code` holds `\"v4\"` canonical vectors,\n`requires` gains `:yin.k/trees`.) No `layout` slot and no native image:\nthose were the positional profiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1).\n\nModule-pins ruling (2026-10-16): `requires` always carries BOTH\n`:yin.k/modules` and `:yin.k/host-exports`, using `{}` when empty, just as\nexisting requires collections are emitted when empty. A missing pin-map key\nis `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`; omission is not an alternative empty\nencoding. The module map records registry binding addresses (nil for host\nmodules); the host-export map records profile keywords for used qualified\nexports only. The module map is `{module-symbol address-keyword-or-nil}`:\ncarry `(:address entry)` unchanged and compare with `=`. The earlier ruling's\nstring-or-nil wording was a typing slip; linked entries hold Jing segment\nkeywords. The host-export map is `{qualified-symbol profile-keyword}`.\nBoth maps are validated during decode and dependency checking, including\nsymbol key cardinality. Malformed or absent maps refuse `:yin.k/undecodable\n:requires`. After custody, `check-modules!` checks every task before the first\nattach: terminal `:module-pin-mismatch` or `:host-export-mismatch` wins,\nthen one `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/discovery :complete` collects\nmissing pins and unadmitted install children under `:yin.k/missing`.\n\n\nKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\nparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\nnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\n5. **Canonical form, recursive.** Every literal is in the portable\n   domain; every sorted slot is in the §5 order; every keyed row has\n   unique keys, and every `:set` row unique elements, under canonical\n   bytes and under portable decoded equality; optional absence has its\n   one spelling (nil in the tabled slot); a reference-free collection is\n   spelled `[:lit d]`, never a row; empty collections are literals;\n   metadata obeys §4. A breach is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row\n   and slot. Without this step two different valid encodings could lower\n   to one state, or a `:bindings` row could carry one symbol twice and\n   lose a binding on lower.\n6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\n   root and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n   `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the\n   tie refusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\n   recomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed\n   by the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound\n   is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\nruns `custody-baseline` for the tree and emits sorted rows (§7). It can\nserve streams during the walk before a custody refusal; the holder\nretains successful answers for retries. The nested carrier's value table\n(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cursor cell\ntable becomes the census.\n\n**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: format-2 recovery uses an administrative fenced lower.\n`rows/lower-task {:fenced true}` runs the carrier checks and custody\ngrammar before attach, skips grant/protection, then gates every task\n`:exporting`. Waits and all parked records move to\n`:yin.k/recovery-waits` and `:yin.k/recovery-parked`, outside the scheduler;\n\n### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift, and the table is the machine's\n\nSuperseded for the register-shaped machine by\n`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1 (two state columns, `act` and\n`tail`, and the `deliver` record); the table below describes the\nstack-shaped machine, which left at that design's phase 8 (2026-10-15),\nand the stack-shaped halves of the old `yin.vm.ucf` that served it —\nthe safepoint table, `stack-effect`, `parking-kinds` — were deleted\nwith it, un-ported (8f-1 ruling R2); the register machine's safepoint\nfacts live in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §4 and the frame validator of\n`yin.vm.semantic.frame`.\n\nThe linear machine's registers are canonical only *between* instructions.\nInside a `:call` that has popped its operands but not yet pushed a frame, or\ninside a superinstruction the loader fused (§6.4), no ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ exists\nthat both describes the state and is expressible in the §2.4 vocabulary.\nUCF resolves this by rule rather than by metadata: **a continuation may be\nlifted only at a safepoint**, and a safepoint is a transition of the\nreference machine at which the machine parks. The first draft's table\npresented itself as complete while omitting the parks that arrive through\n`:call` — a called host function that returns an effect blocks exactly as an\n\n- `def`, `use`, `live` and `lexically-required` are **derived from the\n  canonical vector alone** (`yin.vm.semantic.analysis`). They are\n  identical for every engine and checkable against the reference machine\n  by a conformance test that runs the segment to each safepoint and\n  compares the saved window's key set with `live − {rd}` and the E-reads.\n  Window values, environments, `K` and physical layouts are **not**\n  claimed, not derived, and not in this table; they are the frame's\n  business.\n- `:yin.safepoint/layout` is opaque to the protocol. It is a memo the engine\n  keeps so that its `lift` is a table lookup. It never travels in a UCF\n  value; a resumer that is a different engine has its own.\n- `lift(engine-state, layout) → canonical frame` and\n  `lower(canonical frame, layout) → engine-state` are total on safepoints and\n  undefined elsewhere. The conformance obligation is `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n  canonical frames, tested against the reference machine's own frames —\n  which is possible precisely because the dynamic half rides in the frame.\n- A request to lift at a pc that is not a safepoint of the segment returns\n  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` naming the pc. An engine that wants finer\n  interruption granularity must lower the program to a segment that\n  *contains* a `:park` where it wants one: that is a different instruction\n  record, a different address, and a different profile. An engine may not\n  invent safepoints a record does not have, and two engines that disagree\n  about the safepoint set of one address are not both conforming.\n\nSafepoints are datoms, so a query can ask \"where can this program be\nmigrated?\" without running it, and a debugger's breakpoint set is a subset of\nthe safepoint set by construction.\npoll) — may restore a woken entry from the ready queue, may satisfy a\ndirect `:resume`, and may retry a blocked write, all after the value is in\nother machines' hands. Delaying register restoration is too late; the\nappend is the effect.\n\nUCF therefore defines an explicit **exporting** transition that precedes\nany publication, and the lift driver performs it as one step over the\nmachine value:\n\n1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\n2. **Detach the wait set**: every wait entry moves from the machine's\n   wait-set into the export record. The machine's wait-set is now empty,\n   so *there is nothing to poll* — no poll, no append, no effect. The\n   entries are not lost: the export record retains them verbatim; they are\n   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\n3. **Neutralize control**: the record's control and K are already absent\n   (the machine is parked or blocked); direct `:resume` resolves against\n   `:parked`, and the parked records referenced by reachable code travel\n   inside the export record — a local `:resume` against them is refused\n   while exporting (the restore function observes the exporting machine\n   and declines), because the parked id now names a checkpoint that may be\n   granted elsewhere.\n4. **Publish, then offer**: append segments and the value body; record\ndriver's inbox retention (`:yin.k/journal :yin.k/inbox`) is one\nrecord per selected observation; its journal position is the merge\norder, with no second persisted ordering counter, and control\nrecords may pass earlier deferred program records while deferred\nprogram order and selected control order are preserved\nindependently (7.11).\n\n## §7.10 Invariant compliance\n\n| Invariant | How UCF honors it |\n|---|---|\n    :ffi-call    -> [:ffi-sent :ffi-retained]\n    :call        -> [:effectful-call]\n\nThe two FFI values name possible outcomes, not two entries at a pc.\nThe `:call` value likewise does not select the effect or pending\nvariant. The static map stays a pure function of the vector. This is\na naming correction to the Phase 1 safepoint API, not a new `v2`\nexecution revision: `:yin.safepoint/reasons` was not a component named\nby UCF 7.3.3 or specified in UCF 7.4.2. Consumers must migrate the\nfield when M4 implements the lift driver; Phase 1 has no runtime\nconsumer, though `ucf_test.cljc` asserts its current values.\n\nThe lift driver must check the pc against the static safepoint set,\nthen inspect the actual parked record, wait entry, and effect outcome\nto construct `:yin.k/reason` and the corresponding UCF 7.4.3\n`:yin.k/pending` variant. It may translate `:next`/`:put` where their\npending data suffices, but may not infer a reason from a kind alone.\nFor an effectful `:call`, the observed stream effect determines\n`:next` or `:put`; the instruction's kind does not force\n`:call-effect`. For FFI, the sent or retained state determines\n`:ffi` or `:ffi-request` on the wire, even though the local wait\nentry is `:next` or `:put`. Export must refuse when evidence is\ninsufficient. This ruling does not change the reference wait-set\nclassifier or mint new wait-entry reasons.\n\nUCF 7.4.1 lists `:park` and `:call-effect` in its frame enum, while\n7.4.3's claimed exhaustive pending examples show neither variant.\nM4 must reconcile those rows before exporting them: an explicit park\nneeds a specified no-wait representation, and any use of\n`:call-effect` needs a portable pending shape. This is a UCF protocol\ngap, not grounds to invent a dynamic reason from static code.\n\nUCF 7.11's safepoint harness must run each 7.4.1 row, including both\nFFI outcomes and effectful calls. It checks static kind membership,\nthe observed reason and pending variant, and lift/lower parity. The\nPhase 1 reviewer and architect identified this as a Phase 2 gate:\n`collab/1790268690622-reviewer-ucf-phase1.glm-flash.findings.md`\nand `collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log`.\n\n# Known-gaps ledger and the standing review rule (Q6)\n\nAdopted by the owner 2026-10-14 (mob consolidation Q6).\n\n## Review rule\n- Stop on satisfied gates, not a round count.\n- The reviewer is a different model family from the author.\n- Reviewer prompts quote the slice row and its acceptance lines.\n- Blocking is P0 or P1, or a P2 in claimed acceptance, inside the slice's scope.\n- Fix rounds may not add public entry points, catalog ids or frontends.\n- A finding in code the slice should not have written means revert, not fix.\n- A disputed in-scope P1 goes to a third-family Architect, not a third engineer round.\n\n## Ledger\n| gap | evidence | owner seat | closing slice | severity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| absent is permitted; a host entry under a pinned name refuses with `:linked nil` | fable L-d review P3-2 / L-d-2 | linker | open | P3 |\n| `semantic.scan-test` (then `semantic-register.scan-test`) C4 golden mismatch (JVM, Node) | slow tier at 355bd84b | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed 2026-10-14: the `[:c4 :py]` scanner golden regenerated mechanically after the other seat's linker relocation | P2 |\n| JVM `hash-registry-contract-test` sweep flags `ucf/rows.cljc` `decode*` | quick tier | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed 2026-10-14: the address-validation lint in `rows.cljc` repaired | P2 |\n| register design doc §2/§2.1 derivation prose still names the deleted resolve/lower-register stages | 8e Architect review P2-2 / 8g brief item 6 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed in 8g: dated amendment in `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md` §2 (2026-10-15) | P3 |\n| native binder refusal collapsed to the projection's `:slot-kind` rule (reduced defence-in-depth coverage) | 8e review P2-3 | yin.vm.semantic (the register-VM track) | closed in follow-up-1, not applicable: native images carry no binders (closure = arity + body pc, derived from A; pins: opcode tables under contract 2/r2, `derive_test:179`, `debruijn_code_test:566`); the one binder validator is the projection's `:slot-kind` rule (`host_typed_values_test:242-247`); the native-side defence is the existing `:closure-arity` rule. | P2 |\n| 8d partial mappings (park beside a live reader wait; noise before the correlated FFI response) rest on bare-rows law-1 equality, not named successor tests | phase 8 final review P3-2 | yin.vm.semantic (the